window.SNAP = {"updated": "2026-11-12T21:48:30Z", "batch": {"batch": "batch-15", "state": "running", "spent_usd": 4.1272, "budget_usd": 90.0, "asks_unread": 0, "heartbeat": "2026-11-12T21:48:18Z", "slots": {"taken": 4, "total": 4}, "crs": [{"cr": "CR-196", "title": "A release CR asked in a batch is a normal CR of that batch", "state": "working", "round": 1, "cost": 1.014, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/2-actives/196-release-cr-in-batch", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# A release CR asked in a batch is a normal CR of that batch\n\n## Why\n\nToday a release CR that shares a batch is an exception to it. It waits until every other CR is\nmerged (not ready), then for a separate confirmation; it sits outside a series and a stack, on\nthe default branch; its PR is never part of the stack. The user reviews and merges the work\nfirst, and only then sees a version PR and release notes, in a second pass.\n`docs/batch-pace.svg` draws it that way: a frame around \"every CR in here\", and the release\noutside the frame, like a batch of one CR.\n\nThe user (2026-11-12) wants the other shape to be the normal, documented one: \"Si une CR de\nrelease est demandée dans un batch, elle est typiquement mise en dépendance de tous les autres\nCR et donc ne pourra pas démarrer tant que le reste de la stack est prête.\"\n\n## Outcome\n\n- A release CR asked in a batch is a CR of that batch like the others: it waits for every other\n  CR through intra-batch dependencies and starts once a single branch holds all the work it\n  ships.\n- In a stack or a series batch that is when every other CR is ready (not merged): the release\n  stands on the top CR, and its PR is the top of the stack. Its version bump, changelog and\n  release notes cover everything below it, plus what the default branch already holds since the\n  previous tag. The user reviews the work and the release together and merges from the bottom\n  up, the release PR last.\n- Preparing the release PR is reversible. The irreversible actions (tag, forge release,\n  publication, announcement) wait until the release PR is merged on the default branch, which\n  means the whole stack is merged, and each still needs the user's go (an approval or a grant).\n- The cards, the CLI where it refuses this, the project's release procedure\n  (`CONTRIBUTING.md` \"Release\", `docs/release-process.md`), the use-case docs and\n  `docs/batch-pace.svg` say so; the diagram draws the release inside the batch with its\n  intra-batch dependencies.\n\n## Not in this CR\n\n- More than one release per batch; a release that ships only part of a batch.\n- The fix grant, the \"auto\" grant and the `release go` vocabulary, other than the new wait for\n  the merge.\n- A new CLI command.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n## Grill of 2026-11-12 (advisor in the user's place)\n\nThe user, 2026-11-12: \"fait toutes les P1 et P2 ... pour la plupart je ne pourrais pas te\nrepondre, utilise un advisor Fable. demande moi uniquement quand il y a un impact utilisateur\".\nThe user's wish for this CR is in `intake/request.md` and `01-intent.md`. Every answer below is\nthe advisor's, not the user's own word; the questions under \"Open for the user\" are not decided.\n\n### Behaviour\n\n- Q: Is a release CR in a batch still a kind of its own? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes for who runs it and what guards it (a Release driver, the project's procedure, `release go` before each irreversible action, one release per batch). No for its place in the batch: it is a CR of the batch like the others, with dependencies, a place in the series or the stack, a branch on the CR under it and a PR in the stack. The sentence \"a release CR sits outside a series and a stack, on the default branch\" goes away for the release.\n- Q: What does the release wait for? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Every other CR of the batch, always, the ones added later too. The CLI keeps that list as it does today; it is not hand-edited. A release that waits for only some CRs is out of scope: a stack merges from the bottom up, so the release can only be its top.\n- Q: When does it start: the others ready, or merged? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): One rule: it starts once a single branch holds all the work it ships. In a stack or a series batch, that is when every other CR is ready or merged: the top CR's branch holds everything. A blocked or working CR keeps it waiting. A cancelled CR does not hold it back (as today): it ships nothing.\n- Q: And in a parallel batch? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): In a parallel batch each PR is on the default branch and no branch holds all the work until it is merged, so the single rule gives today's timing there: the release starts once the others are merged or cancelled. It is still drawn and reported as a CR of the batch with its dependencies. See \"Open for the user\", question 2.\n- Q: Where does it stand in a stack or a series? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): On top, always. Its branch starts on the top CR's branch, its PR's base is that branch, and the stack driver treats it as the top piece (it restacks it with the rest and refuses an order that puts a CR above it). In a series it is the last of the order. A CR added later goes under it.\n- Q: Is its PR a draft? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): It follows the shared definition of \"ready to merge\": a draft while its base is not the default branch, ready once the stack under it is merged and it is retargeted to the default branch and green. \"A release PR is no draft\" stays true only for a release whose base is the default branch.\n- Q: What do its version bump, changelog and notes cover? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Everything between the previous tag and its own head: what the default branch already holds since that tag, plus every CR under it in the stack. The procedure's commands run on the release branch, which is on top of all of it, so `git log vPREVIOUS..HEAD` and the \"N changes\" count are taken there instead of on the default branch. The version commit is the last commit of the stack; nothing may merge after it before the tag.\n- Q: How does the prepared release read in `status` and the reports while it waits for the merge? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): As a CR that is ready (its PR open and green, waiting for the user's merge), not as a blocked CR. Today the only way is `handback blocked merge:`, which reads as trouble. After its merge it goes on to the tag and the publication and ends \"released\" as today.\n- Q: What happens if a CR below changes after the release PR is open? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Two cases. A move with no change of content (a restack, a rebase onto the default branch after a partial merge): the release PR is restacked like any CR above, and its checks run again; its notes are carried. A change of content (a CR below gets a new round, is dropped, or a CR is added under the release): what the release ships changed, so the release goes back to a Release driver, which runs the procedure again from its first step on the new base and refreshes the same PR (version commit, count, notes, captures). The drafts the user edited are read again, never rewritten from scratch. The steering session decides which case it is from the CR's log; when unsure, it is a change of content.\n- Q: A defect the release finds before the stack is merged? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): It goes back to the CR that owns it when one does (a note and a take, as for any ready CR), and the release is redone after. A fix CR, on the fast track on the default branch, stays what it is today for a defect found once the stack is merged. Before the merge, a fix that belongs to no CR of the batch joins like any added CR, under the release.\n- Q: When may the irreversible actions happen? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Only once the release PR is merged on the default branch, which means every CR under it is merged. Until then `release go` answers no for the tag, the forge release, a publication and an announcement, whatever the approvals or the grant say; \"auto\" does not lift this. After the merge each action still needs the user's go, exactly as today (an approval per action, or the grant). The announcement is never posted by a\n\n…", "03-change-spec.md": "# A release CR asked in a batch is a normal CR of that batch\n\n## Goal\n\nThe user (2026-11-12): \"Si une CR de release est demandée dans un batch, elle est typiquement mise\nen dépendance de tous les autres CR et donc ne pourra pas démarrer tant que le reste de la stack\nest prête. Ça doit faire partie d'une possibilité normale et documentée et apparaissant dans\ndocs/batch-pace.svg.\"\n\nA batch can run fully on its own (overnight, say) and deliver, in the morning, a complete PR\nstack whose top PR is the release PR with its release notes. The user reviews the work and the\nrelease together, can still fix the release PR, and merges the whole stack from the bottom up.\nThat merge is the go: the Release driver is started again and follows the project's release\nprocedure. chantier never names the steps of that procedure (a tag, a forge release, a\npublication…): it knows only that there is a procedure to follow, and that the release PR, when\nthe procedure has one, is the top of the stack.\n\n## Words used in this specification\n\n- **Shared release:** a release CR that is not alone in its batch (`rails.alone()` is false: some\n  CR other than a fix was in the batch).\n- **Ordered batch:** a batch whose mode is `stack` or `series`.\n- **The stack under the release:** the other CRs of the batch, fix CRs excepted, that are not\n  merged or cancelled.\n- **Top CR:** the last of the stack under the release in its order (`order`, which `stack`\n  rewrites in a stack batch and `open` / `order add` set in a series).\n- **Release PR:** the PR the project's procedure opens for the release (a version PR in\n  chantier's own procedure). A procedure may have none.\n- **The grant:** the explicit release grant the user gives with `order grant` (\"auto\"), as today.\n\n## The behaviour\n\n### Every release, in every mode\n\n1. A release CR is a CR of its batch. It waits for every other CR of the batch, the ones added\n   later too; the CLI keeps that list (`after`), as today, and `order after` stays refused on it.\n2. **No version confirmation, ever.** The release no longer waits for `order confirm` before it\n   starts. The version is the one the release CR's grill settled; when the grill left it open,\n   the Release driver picks the best version from its context (the changes it ships, the\n   project's versioning rules) and says why in the release PR's description (with no release PR,\n   in its draft notes) and in a `note`. It never asks for the version.\n3. A cancelled CR never holds the release back (as today); a blocked or working one keeps it\n   waiting.\n4. A release alone in its batch starts when taken, on the default branch, and its PR is ready on\n   the default branch. Rules 5 to 11 below hold for it as for any release.\n5. **Who merges the release PR:** the user. In an ordered batch, with the rest of the stack, from\n   the bottom up, the release PR last; in a parallel batch or alone, once it is the only open PR\n   of the batch. The steering session merges a release PR only under the grant, and then only\n   once every other CR of the batch is merged or cancelled and the PR's base is the default\n   branch. An approval (`order approve`) never lets it merge a release PR.\n6. **The go:** the merge of the release PR (the user's, or the steering session's under the\n   grant) is the go for the rest of the procedure. On the user's merge, the steering session\n   starts the Release driver again, and it follows the project's procedure to its end with no\n   further go from chantier; a go is asked only where the project's procedure itself asks for\n   one (`handback blocked approval: <step>`, answered with `order approve`, or covered by the\n   grant).\n7. **A procedure with no release PR:** chantier waits until every other CR of the batch is merged\n   (alone: none to wait for), then asks the user whether to run the release procedure (the user\n   is back and interacting then). The Release driver may start before (rules 12 and 20) to\n   prepare the drafts the user reviews; it then hands back waiting. The user's yes is recorded\n   with `order confirm` (below), and it is the go for the rest of the procedure, as the merge is\n   in rule 6.\n8. **No irreversible step before the merge.** Until every other CR of the batch is merged or\n   cancelled, and while an unmerged release PR is recorded, `release go` answers no, whatever\n   the approvals say. The grant lifts only the second wait (rule 5: the steering session's merge\n   under the grant); nothing lifts the first. After the merge: rule 6 or 7.\n9. **Its gates:** what the project's procedure names, at the release PR's pushed head, and the\n   forge's checks green there. No Verifier verdict. chantier adds no gate of its own.\n10. **How it reads while it waits for the merge:** as a `ready` CR (its PR open and green,\n    waiting for the merge), never as a blocked one.\n11. **`order add` once the release has started stays refused** (the user declined the advisor's\n    recommendation): a CR added after a release CR complicates the batch. The exception is the\n    urgent fixes of a release that went wrong: `order add --fix-for <release-cr>`, as today.\n    Afterwards comes a retro, which may feed the same batch or the next; the batch is normally\n    paused at that point. Before the release starts, `order add` is accepted and the new CR goes\n    under the release, as today.\n\n### Ordered batch (stack or series): the release on top\n\n12. **When it starts:** once a single branch holds all the work it ships, which is when every\n    other CR is `ready` or `merged` (or cancelled) and the top CR's branch holds the recorded\n    head of every other open CR of the stack under it. In a stack batch that means the stack\n    driver has stacked them first.\n13. **Where it starts:** on the top CR's branch (`take` and `handoff` print it as `base`; `ship`\n    records it). With no CR left open under it, on the default branch.\n14. **Its place:** always the top of the stack or the last of the series. `take stack` lists it\n    last, with the top\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-196 A release CR asked in a batch is a normal CR of that batch\n\n🟡 working Round 1/3 · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** A coder and its reviews are working.\n**Next:** The long gate, then the ship.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [x] Round 1: r1: CLI (rails.py, commands.py, cli.py) and scenario per spec sections CLI and Scenario\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [today-refusals](work/today-refusals.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [README.md](../../../../../README.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [docs/cr-lifecycle-texts.md](../../../../../docs/cr-lifecycle-texts.md)\n- [docs/release-driver-texts.md](../../../../../docs/release-driver-texts.md)\n- [docs/release-process.md](../../../../../docs/release-process.md)\n- [docs/use-cases-direct.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases-direct.md)\n- [docs/use-cases-foreman.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases-foreman.md)\n- [scenario/README.md](../../../../../scenario/README.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-196-a-release-cr-asked-in-a-batch-is-a-norma`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-196`\n- Driver: `af7dbe14ba58523a1`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- back to a Driver: resume: direct steering session stopped, batch moved to Foreman mode\n\n## Cost\n\n$1.01 so far (at the last heartbeat).\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-197", "title": "A Driver's claim about CI comes from the forge's checks at the pushed head", "state": "working", "round": 1, "cost": 0.4988, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/2-actives/197-driver-ci-from-pr-checks", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# A Driver's claim about CI comes from the forge's checks at the pushed head\n\n## The problem\n\nIn real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137) three Drivers reported \"no CI\" while a `test` check ran\non every PR. The claim came from somewhere other than the forge (the project's files, a guess, a\nlook taken before the checks were registered), and nothing in the Driver card says where a claim\nabout CI must come from.\n\n## Where it shows\n\n- `skills/chantier/references/driver.md`, \"Your means\", the `CI:` bullet: it says how to wait\n  (`gh pr checks <pr> --watch`, `glab ci status --live`) and how to judge a result, but it has no\n  word on the case \"no CI\", so a Driver is free to conclude it from anything.\n- On a PR with no check registered yet, `gh pr checks` answers that no check is reported and the\n  watch ends at once: a look taken right after the push reads as \"no CI\" when CI is only late.\n- `skills/chantier/references/pr-template.md`, Test plan, `CI green at <sha7>`: the box has no\n  form for a project without CI, so the Driver improvises it.\n- The Driver's `note` on CI (\"Say what CI showed in a `note`\") then carries the wrong fact to the\n  steering session and to the report.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nIn the `CI:` bullet of the Driver card:\n\n- The forge's list of checks for the PR at the pushed head (`gh pr checks <pr>`, `glab ci list`,\n  or the forge's equivalent) is the only source for any claim about CI: running, green, red, or\n  none. The project's files, its rules and an earlier Driver's note are not a source.\n- \"No CI\" is said only when that list holds no check after the push, on a look taken once the\n  forge has had time to register them, and with the PR's head equal to the pushed head.\n- The note that says what CI showed names the command and the head; the PR body's CI box says the\n  same thing in the template's words.\n\nThe rule sits in \"Your means\", so it holds for every Driver that pushes (CR, stack, release).\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Any CLI change: the CLI runs no forge command, and `handback` keeps checking the gate and the\n  verdict only.\n- How a result is judged (skipped, red on the default branch, flake, the 3-hour limit): unchanged.\n- The agent files of both hosts: the contract lives in the card.\n- An automated audit of the claim in the eval kit.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Is the forge's list the only source even when the project's rules say the project has no CI? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes: the claim always comes from the forge's list at the pushed head; the rules only say how to judge what the list shows, and a list that contradicts the rules is said in a `note`. One look is cheap, and a stale rule is exactly how a running check gets reported as absent.\n- Q: When may a Driver conclude \"no CI\", given that checks appear some time after the push? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Only after a second look, taken after a short bounded wait (about a minute) following the push, still lists no check; an empty first look, or a watch that ends at once, is not a conclusion. A forge registers its checks a few seconds late, and that is the likely cause of the three false reports.\n- Q: Must the claim be tied to the head? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes: the Driver confirms the PR's head on the forge is the head it pushed before it reads the checks, and its note names that head. Checks listed for an older commit prove nothing about the pushed one.\n- Q: Which Drivers does the rule bind? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Every Driver that pushes (CR, stack, release), since the rule sits in the common `CI:` bullet. A stack or a release that reports \"no CI\" wrongly costs as much as a CR.\n- Q: What does the PR body's CI box say when the forge lists no check? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The box stays, checked, and reads `No CI: no check listed at <sha7>`; it is never deleted and never written \"CI green\". The template already asks for \"None\" rather than a missing section, and the SHA keeps the claim checkable.\n- Q: Does the Verifier's card change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): No: a CI line in the description is already a claim the Verifier judges, and it reads the forge's list itself to judge it. The existing rule (a claim with no proof is `NOT VERIFIED`) covers it.\n- Q: Should the CLI refuse a `handback ready` that has no CI reading? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): No. The CLI runs no forge command and knows no role; the card carries the rule.\n- Q: Is an eval check part of this change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): One row in `evals/SCORECARD.md`, checked by hand on the real runs (a Driver's \"no CI\" follows a checks call made after the push); no automated audit. The defect was found in a real run and must be looked for in the next one, but an audit is more than the fix needs.\n- Q: The one-minute wait meets rule 8 of concepts.md (no sleep, no polling loop). If the Driver's other work ends in under a minute after an empty first look, what then? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): one bounded wait, once, until a minute after the push, then the second look: a named exception in the Driver card's CI: bullet, which rule 8 points to. Never a loop; a second empty look concludes \"No CI\".\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# A Driver's claim about CI comes from the forge's checks at the pushed head\n\n## Goal\n\nA Driver never again says \"no CI\" while a check runs. In real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137;\n`evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`, finding 1) three Drivers wrote \"no CI\" in their\nhand-backs and PR bodies while a `test` check ran green on every PR. The claim came from\nsomewhere other than the forge, most likely from a look taken before the forge had registered the\nchecks. After this change, every claim a Driver makes about CI (running, green, red, or none)\ncomes from the forge's list of checks for the PR, read at the head the Driver pushed, and \"no CI\"\nneeds a second empty look taken a minute after the push.\n\nThis is a card change only: no CLI change, no agent file change.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. `skills/chantier/references/driver.md`, \"Your means\", the `CI:` bullet (lines 109-120 today)\n\nThe bullet keeps everything it says today (do the work that needs no CI first, one long wait with\nthe forge's watch in a background shell, no look in between, how a result is judged, the flake\nnote, the 3-hour limit). It gains the rule below, written in the card's style (short sentences,\nplain words). Because it sits in \"Your means\", it binds every Driver that pushes: CR, stack and\nrelease Drivers alike. For a stack driver, which opens no PR, \"the PR\" is the PR whose branch it\npushed (the top of the stack, where the heavy CI runs).\n\nThe rule, in substance:\n\n- **The only source.** Any claim about CI (running, green, red, or none) comes from the forge's\n  list of checks for the PR at the pushed head: `gh pr checks <pr>`, `glab ci list`, or the\n  forge's equivalent. The project's files (a workflow folder or its absence), its rules, an\n  earlier Driver's note and a guess are not a source.\n- **The project's rules do not replace the look.** Even when the project's rules say the project\n  has no CI, the claim comes from the forge's list; the rules only say how to judge what the list\n  shows. A list that contradicts the rules (a check listed where the rules say none, or none where\n  the rules say some) is said in a `note`.\n- **The head first.** Before it reads the checks, the Driver confirms that the PR's head on the\n  forge is the head it pushed (`gh pr view <pr> --json headRefOid`, or the forge's equivalent).\n  Checks listed for an older commit prove nothing about the pushed one.\n- **\"No CI\" needs two looks.** An empty first look, or a watch that ends at once because no check\n  is reported yet, is not a conclusion: a forge registers its checks a few seconds after the push.\n  \"No CI\" is said only when a second look, taken a minute or more after the push, still lists no\n  check at the same head. The minute is filled with the work that needs no CI result\n  (`pr-body.md`, the archive's `delivery.md`, the notes), which the bullet already puts first.\n- **The one bounded wait.** When that work ends before a minute has passed since the push, the\n  Driver runs one bounded wait, once, until a minute after the push (for instance a single `sleep`\n  of the seconds that remain), then takes the second look. The card names this as the exception\n  to rule 8 of `concepts.md` (\"No `sleep`, no polling loop\"): at most once per pushed head, never\n  a loop. A second empty look concludes \"No CI\"; a second look that lists checks leads to the\n  normal long wait.\n- **What the note says.** The `note` that says what CI showed (\"Say what CI showed in a `note`\",\n  already in the bullet) names the command it ran and the head (seven characters) it read at, for\n  instance `CI: gh pr checks 12 at abc1234: test pass` or `CI: no check listed at abc1234 (gh pr\n  checks 12, two looks, the second a minute after the push)`.\n- **What the PR body says.** The PR body's CI box says the same thing in the template's words\n  (section 3 below).\n\nKeep the bullet's existing ban on looks during the long wait (\"no `gh pr checks` or `glab ci`\nlook in between\"): the two looks of the \"no CI\" rule happen before the long wait starts, or\ninstead of it when no check is listed; the Driver writes the text so the two do not read as a\ncontradiction.\n\n### 2. `skills/chantier/references/concepts.md`, \"Rules\", rule 8\n\nRule 8 says \"No `sleep`, no polling loop: a wait is the forge's own watch, in a background shell.\"\nIt gains a short pointer to the one exception, in substance: \"except the one bounded wait before\na second CI look that [driver](driver.md), `CI:`, names\". Nothing else in the rule changes.\n\n### 3. `skills/chantier/references/pr-template.md`, the template's Test plan\n\nThe last Test plan item, today `- [ ] CI green at \\`<sha7>\\``, gets the form for a PR whose forge\nlists no check:\n\n- When the forge lists no check at the head (after the two looks of section 1), the box stays,\n  checked, and reads `No CI: no check listed at <sha7>`.\n- It is never deleted and never written \"CI green\" in that case.\n\nPut this in the item's HTML comment in the template (as the other items carry their alternatives,\ne.g. `<!-- or \"No CI: no check listed at <sha7>\" when the forge lists no check at the head -->`),\nand, if the Driver judges it reads better, one line in \"The rules\" next to the rule that each\nproof names its SHA. The Inspector's audit list (\"The audit\") is unchanged.\n\n### 4. `evals/SCORECARD.md`, one new row\n\nAdd one row after J24, with the next free id, `J25`, marked by hand (no automated audit):\n\n| id | rail | from | where seen | how marked |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| J25 | a Driver's claim about CI comes from the forge's list of checks at the pushed head: a \"no CI\" follows a checks call made after the push, at the PR's head, with a second look a minute after the push still empty (one bounded wait at most, never a loop); the note and the PR body's CI box name that head | driver.md, pr-template.md, concepts.md rule 8 | full, real-foreman, real-direct, claude-planted | by hand |\n\n`evals/kit/audit.py` is not changed: it reads the rows at run time and a `by hand` row needs n\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-197 A Driver's claim about CI comes from the forge's checks at the pushed head\n\n🟡 working Round 1/3 · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** A coder and its reviews are working.\n**Next:** The long gate, then the ship.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [x] Round 1: apply the spec's four edits (driver.md CI bullet, concepts.md rule 8, pr-template.md CI item, SCORECARD J25)\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [long gate log](work/gates/20261112-224821.log)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [evals/README.md](../../../../../evals/README.md)\n- [evals/SCORECARD.md](../../../../../evals/SCORECARD.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/concepts.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/driver.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/driver.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/pr-template.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/pr-template.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/worker.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/worker.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-197-a-driver-s-claim-about-ci-comes-from-the`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-197`\n- Driver: `a5b72cbfdbbf330d3`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- back to a Driver: resume: direct steering session stopped, batch moved to Foreman mode\n\n## Cost\n\n$0.50 so far (at the last heartbeat).\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-198", "title": "A Driver stops its own long gate by a named means, never with `cancel`", "state": "working", "round": 1, "cost": 0.7283, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/2-actives/198-stop-own-gate", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# A Driver stops its own long gate by a named means, never with `cancel`\n\n## The problem\n\nIn batch-11 (2026-11-11) CR-183's Driver had a long gate running when a rebase note arrived. The\ngate's result could no longer count (the head was about to move), so the Driver wanted it\nstopped, found no means for that in its card, and ran `chantier cancel CR-183`: the gate stopped,\nand the CR was cancelled and moved to the trash with its work. `cancel` is the steering session's\ncommand for a CR the user dropped.\n\n## Where it shows\n\n- `skills/chantier/references/driver.md`: the long gate bullet says how to run a gate and nothing\n  on stopping one; \"stop only one you started, by its id\" is the only nearby rule; `cancel` is\n  not named at all, so nothing forbids it to a Driver.\n- That nearby rule does not work for a gate: `gate` starts the suite in its own process group\n  (`commands.py`, `gate`), so ending the `gate` command leaves the suite running, and the next\n  long gate of the repository waits for that process, up to an hour (`_LongGateLock`).\n- The only code that stops a running suite properly is inside `cancel` (`procs.stop_group` on the\n  group the lock names), bound to cancelling the CR.\n- `cancel` takes `--epoch` but does not require it (`state.check_epoch` returns on a missing\n  epoch): a bare `chantier cancel <cr>` from any agent is accepted.\n\n## What would fix it\n\n- A named CLI means, on the `gate` command, that stops the running long gate of the CR it is\n  given: it stops the suite's process group, frees the gate for the next CR, records the run as\n  stopped (no pass, not a failure of the code) and changes nothing else in the CR's state. It\n  touches only a gate the lock names for that CR.\n- driver.md, the long gate bullet: when a Driver stops its own gate (its result can no longer\n  count because the head will move, or the steering session said `stop`), by that means only;\n  and one sentence: `cancel` is the steering session's command on the user's drop, never a\n  Driver's, whatever the reason.\n- `cancel` without `--epoch` is refused, as the steering commands' rule already implies.\n\nThis is a CLI addition from a measured incident (a CR lost with its work), inside the CLI's\nscope (the batch's frame and its gate), not a speculative tool.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Any change to what `cancel` does for the steering session.\n- Recovering CR-183 or any CR cancelled by mistake (`restore` exists).\n- Stopping CI, a worker, or any process other than the long gate.\n- A timeout on a gate that hangs.\n- The agent files of both hosts: the contract lives in the card.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Is the named way a rule in the card alone (stop the process you started, by its id), or a means of the CLI? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): A means of the CLI, on the `gate` command, named in the card. Ending the `gate` command by its id leaves the suite running in its own process group, and the next long gate waits for it for up to an hour; only the CLI knows the group, from its lock.\n- Q: Does this respect the rule that a new CLI tool comes only from measured behaviour? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes: it comes from a recorded incident that cost a whole CR, it adds no command (a way of `gate`, which is already the CLI's), and it reuses the stop that `cancel` already has. The gate and its lock are the batch's frame, which is the CLI's scope.\n- Q: When may a Driver stop its own gate? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Only when the run's result can no longer count (the head is about to move: a `rebase:` note, a change that needs a round) or on the steering session's `stop`; never because the gate is slow. A gate that would still be a valid pass is worth more finished than restarted.\n- Q: On a `rebase:` note received mid-gate, does the Driver stop the gate or let it finish? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): It stops it, then rebases and runs the gate at the new head. The gate is alone in the repository, so a run whose result is void holds every other CR's gate back for nothing.\n- Q: What does a stopped gate leave in the record? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): A gate event and a last log line that say `stopped` at its head, distinct from a failure, and no pass; the CR's status, notes, worktree and folder are untouched. A stop read as a red gate would send a later reader to hunt a defect that is not there.\n- Q: May the means stop another CR's gate, or be used when no gate runs? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): It stops only a gate the lock names for the CR given, and says so without stopping anything otherwise (another CR's gate: refused; none running: nothing to stop, no error). A Driver must never be able to end a neighbour's suite.\n- Q: Should the CLI itself refuse `cancel` from a Driver? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The CLI knows no role, so not by role; but `cancel` without `--epoch` is refused (exit 2) with a line that names the gate's stop, since every steering command already carries its epoch (scorecard C9) and only `cancel` accepts its absence. The card's sentence alone would not have stopped an agent that never read it as a rule; every steering use in the cards, the scenario and the evals already passes the epoch.\n- Q: Who else gets the rule: the Verifier, the stack and release Drivers? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The sentence on `cancel` and the stop go in the common \"Your means\" of driver.md, so every Driver has them; the Verifier's card is unchanged (it runs a gate only when a claim needs it, and lets it end). The incident is a Driver's, and a worker has no reason to stop a gate.\n- Q: Is a mistaken cancel made easier to undo by this change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): No, out of scope. `restore` already brings a trashed CR back, and the fix is to make the mistake impossible, not cheaper.\n- Q: The CLI cannot stop a Driver from stopping a neighbour's gate; is the card's \"own CR only\" rule enough? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): yes. The CLI knows no role, and a check on the calling worktree would be a guess at who calls; the card's rule holds.\n- Q: Does a `stopped` run void an earlier pass at the same head? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): yes, as the current rule says: the last gate run at a head decides. A stopped gate means the head is to be proved again.\n- Q: A Driver's own gate still waiting for the lock behind another CR: the card tells the Driver to end its own `gate` command by its id? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): yes, intended.\n- Q: `take`, `pause`, `order done`, `agents set` and others also accept a missing epoch; change them here? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): no, this CR changes `cancel` only; the others are filed as their own inbox item.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# A Driver stops its own long gate by a named means, never with `cancel`\n\n## Goal\n\nIn batch-11 (2026-11-11) CR-183's Driver had a long gate running when a rebase note arrived. Once\nthe head is about to move, that run's result cannot count, so the Driver wanted to stop it. Its\ncard gave it no way to do that, so it ran `chantier cancel CR-183`. That stopped the gate, but it\nalso cancelled the CR and moved it to `6-trash/` with all its work.\n\nThis change gives every Driver a named CLI means to stop its own running long gate:\n`gate <cr> --stop`. The means stops the suite, frees the gate for the next CR, records the run as\n`stopped` (not a pass and not a failure) and changes nothing else. `driver.md` says when a Driver\nuses it, and says that `cancel` is never a Driver's command. The CLI refuses `cancel` without\n`--epoch`, so an agent that never read that sentence still cannot cancel a CR by mistake.\n\nThis adds to the CLI because of a measured incident: a whole CR was lost. It stays inside the\nCLI's scope (the batch's frame: the long gate and its lock) and adds no command, only a way of\n`gate`. It reuses the stop that `cancel` already has.\n\n## Facts in the code today (read-only, at `main` 8c003ae)\n\n- `skills/chantier/scripts/cli/src/chantier/commands.py`, `gate()`: each command of the gate runs\n  in its own process group (`CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` on Windows, `start_new_session` on POSIX),\n  under `_LongGateLock`. After each `Popen`, the lock file `<planning folder>/.long-gate.lock` reads\n  ` <cr> pid <gate pid> pg<group pid>` (`lock.holding(...)`). Before the first `Popen` it reads\n  ` <cr> pid <gate pid>`. `_LongGateLock.__exit__` empties it. Each command is recorded as a\n  `gate` event (`result` = `pass` | `fail` | `moved`, `head` = the start SHA, `command`, and\n  `run` = `<stamp>-<pid>`, which all the events of one invocation share). The log's last line is\n  `result: …`.\n- Ending the `gate` command process alone leaves the suite running in its group. The next\n  `_LongGateLock.__enter__` then waits for that group (`procs.alive`), for up to\n  `LONG_GATE_WAIT_S` (one hour).\n- `_lock_holder(repo)` reads the holder text. Today the only code that stops a running suite is\n  in `cancel()`: `re.fullmatch(rf\"{cr} pid \\d+ pg(\\d+)\", holder)` then `procs.stop_group(pg)`.\n- `gate_passed(events, head)`: a head has a pass when the last `gate` invocation (`run`) at that\n  head has every event `pass`.\n- `cancel()` calls `state.check_epoch(b, args.epoch)`, which returns at once when the epoch is\n  `None`. So a bare `chantier cancel <cr>` is accepted from any caller. In `cli.py`, `cancel` is\n  declared with `epoch=True`, so `--epoch` is optional. Other steering commands (`take`, `pause`,\n  `order done`, `agents set`, …) also accept a missing epoch today; they stay as they are here (see\n  Out of scope).\n- `status.py` shows each `gate` event's `result` word in the CR's STATUS (\"Long gate\" rows).\n  `status --refresh` shows `gate running` when the lock names the CR.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. CLI: `gate <cr> --stop` (`cli.py`, `commands.py`)\n\n- `cli.py`: `gate` gets a `--stop` flag. Its help says: \"stop this CR's running long gate: its\n  suite, the gate lock; recorded `stopped`, no pass; nothing else changes\". `command` becomes\n  optional at the parser (`nargs=\"*\"`). `gate()` then requires at least one command without\n  `--stop` (the existing usage error, exit 1) and refuses any command with `--stop` (exit 1,\n  `gate <cr> --stop takes no command`). The `gate` line's help names `--stop`.\n- `gate <cr> --stop` reads the lock's holder:\n  - **The lock names this CR** (` <cr> pid <n>`, with or without ` pg<m>`): it stops that run's\n    suite with `procs.stop_group(<m>)`, the same stop `cancel` uses, on POSIX and on Windows. No\n    later command of that run starts. It prints one line, `stopped the long gate of <cr> at <sha7>\n    (pg<m>)`, and exits 0. It does not touch the CR's status, its notes, its agent, its worktree,\n    its folder, its PR or its round count.\n  - **The lock names another CR:** it stops nothing. Exit 2 with\n    `the long gate running is <other cr>'s: not stopped`. This also covers the caller's own gate\n    while it still waits for the lock behind another CR. That gate has started no suite, and the\n    card tells the Driver how to end it (section 3).\n  - **The lock is empty or missing (no gate running):** it stops nothing. Exit 0 with\n    `no long gate running for <cr>: nothing to stop`.\n- **Whose gate.** The CLI knows no role and does not check who calls: a call whose argument names\n  another CR acts on that CR's gate. A Driver keeps to its own CR because of the card's rule\n  (section 3), not because of a CLI check. There is no check on the calling worktree or any other\n  guess at the caller.\n- **The record of a stopped run.** Each stopped run gets exactly one record, whether the\n  stopped `gate` invocation is still alive (the normal case) or already gone:\n  - one `gate` event with `result=\"stopped\"`, the run's start `head` (full SHA), the `command`\n    that was running and the run's `run` id;\n  - the run's log ends with `result: stopped at <full sha>: no pass`.\n  - When its command ends because of a stop, the stopped `gate` invocation does not record `fail`\n    for that command. It prints `STOPPED <command> at <sha7>: no pass` and the log path, and\n    exits 4. Its output must never read as a failure of the code.\n  - How the running invocation learns of the stop is the implementer's choice. Examples: a field\n    the stopper writes for the run, or the run id and head kept in the lock's holder text. It must\n    work on POSIX and on Windows, with the lock file's byte 0 locked, and stay in the planning\n    folder. A new state file is listed in the planning folder's `.gitignore` (`state.py`,\n    `IGNORED`), like `.long-gate.lock`.\n- **A stopped run and the pass.** The rule of `gate_passed()` does not change: the last gate run\n  at a head decides. A `stopped` event is not `pass`, so a stopped run is no pas\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-198 A Driver stops its own long gate by a named means, never with `cancel`\n\n🟡 working Round 1/3 · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** A coder and its reviews are working.\n**Next:** The long gate, then the ship.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [x] Round 1: implement spec: gate --stop, cancel needs --epoch, cards, docs, scenario step\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [evals/SCORECARD.md](../../../../../evals/SCORECARD.md)\n- [scenario/README.md](../../../../../scenario/README.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/concepts.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/driver.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/driver.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-198-a-driver-stops-its-own-long-gate-by-a-na`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-198`\n- Driver: `a595203340cb54f45`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- back to a Driver: resume: direct steering session stopped, batch moved to Foreman mode\n\n## Cost\n\n$0.73 so far (at the last heartbeat).\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-199", "title": "`just check` rejects a byte-order mark; the worker cards repeat the rule", "state": "working", "round": 1, "cost": 0.5047, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/2-actives/199-check-rejects-bom", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# `just check` rejects a byte-order mark; the worker cards repeat the rule\n\n## The request\n\nTwo retros report the same fault. In batch-14, CR-191's round 3 Coder wrote a UTF-8 byte-order\nmark (BOM) into `evals/kit/dry_run.py` on Windows, and a whole round existed only to strip it.\nIn real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137), conflict resolutions twice left a BOM in files of the\nsandbox. The request asks for two things: `just check` rejects a BOM in any tracked text file,\nand the Coder and Conflict-resolver cards repeat the no-BOM rule.\n\n## The problem\n\n- The rule exists already, once: `skills/chantier/references/concepts.md`, rule 9 (\"Every file\n  you write, a PR body included, is UTF-8 without a byte-order mark\"). It names the cause too:\n  on Windows PowerShell 5.1, `Out-File` and `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` add one. The workers\n  read `concepts.md`, yet the fault came back twice, both times from a worker that writes files\n  through a shell (the Coder, the Conflict-resolver).\n- Nothing mechanical catches it. `just check` (`test` and `release-notes`) has one BOM check\n  only: `tests/test_structure.py` refuses a BOM at the head of an agent file, because it hides\n  the frontmatter from a host. Any other file passes the gate with a BOM, so the fault is found\n  at review, one round later.\n- No tracked file carries a BOM today (checked on 2026-11-12, 291 tracked files), so the gate\n  can be strict from its first day.\n\n## What would fix it\n\n1. In this repository: `just check` is red when a tracked file starts with the UTF-8 BOM\n   (`EF BB BF`), on the three systems, and its message names each file. A Driver's fast gate\n   then stops the fault in the round that made it.\n2. In every project chantier runs in: the worker card (`worker.md`) says it again where the\n   fault happens, in the Coder's and the Conflict-resolver's sections, with the Windows cause.\n   This is the only part that reaches a user's project, such as the sandbox of PR 137.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- A CLI refusal (`gate`, `ship`) of a BOM in a user's project: a project owns its gate, and some\n  projects keep a BOM on purpose.\n- Other encodings and marks (UTF-16, UTF-32), line endings, and any other content check.\n- Stripping BOMs from existing files: there are none here, and a user's project is not ours to\n  rewrite.\n- The agent files (`agents/`, `com.github.copilot/agents/`): the rule lives in the cards.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Which files does the gate check? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): every tracked file of this repository, with no list of exceptions; a file is red when its first three bytes are the UTF-8 BOM. No tracked file has one today, no binary format in the repository starts with those bytes, and a list of exceptions would be the place where the next BOM hides.\n- Q: Does the gate also look at untracked or ignored files? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, tracked files only. A gate binds to a commit, and the user's untracked files in a checkout are not the gate's business.\n- Q: Is it part of `just check` on the three systems, or a separate recipe? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): part of `just check`, so it runs on Linux, macOS and Windows in CI and in every Driver's fast gate. The request wants a BOM red at the gate, and a separate recipe would be skipped; it must stay fast, as AGENTS.md asks of the structure tests.\n- Q: What does a red result say? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it names every file that carries a BOM, in one run, and says the file must be UTF-8 without a byte-order mark. A Coder then fixes all of them in the same round instead of one per run.\n- Q: Does the gate also reject UTF-16 or UTF-32 marks, or files that are not valid UTF-8? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, the UTF-8 BOM only. It is the only fault measured (twice); anything more is speculation.\n- Q: Does chantier refuse a BOM in a user's project (in the CLI's `gate` or `ship`)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The CLI's scope is the frame, a project's own gate decides what is red there, and a project may keep a BOM on purpose; the cards carry the rule to the agents.\n- Q: Which cards repeat the rule, and what do they say? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `worker.md`, in the Coder's and the Conflict-resolver's sections, one short line each: never add a byte-order mark to a file, with the Windows cause (`Out-File`, `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` on PowerShell 5.1) and a pointer to `concepts.md` rule 9, which stays the one full statement. Both faults came from these two roles; the other roles keep the rule through `concepts.md`.\n- Q: What does a worker do with a file of the user's project that already has a BOM? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it leaves the mark as it found it: the rule is \"add none\", not \"strip all\". A resolved conflict or an edit keeps the file's encoding as the base has it, so chantier never rewrites a project's choice.\n- Q: Does the Conflict-resolver check its resolved files before it continues the rebase? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, the card says a resolved file starts as it did before the conflict (no new BOM) before the rebase continues. PR 137 shows the fault enters at that exact step, and in a user's project no gate may catch it later.\n- Q: Does the existing BOM test on the agent files stay? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the Driver's choice, as long as an agent file with a BOM stays red and its reason (the hidden frontmatter) stays written. It is an implementation detail once the general check covers every tracked file.\n- Q: Which gates does this CR pass? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and, because it changes a card, `just scenario`; no `just mod`. AGENTS.md says so for a change to the cards.\n\n## Open for the user\n\nNone.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# `just check` rejects a byte-order mark; the worker cards repeat the rule\n\n## Goal\n\nA UTF-8 byte-order mark (BOM) written by an agent is caught in the round that wrote it, not one\nround later at review. Two retros measured the fault: in batch-14, CR-191's round 3 Coder wrote a\nBOM into `evals/kit/dry_run.py` on Windows and a whole round existed only to strip it; in\nreal-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137) conflict resolutions twice left a BOM in files of the\nsandbox. So:\n\n1. In this repository, `just check` is red when a tracked file starts with the UTF-8 BOM, on\n   Linux, macOS and Windows, and names every such file in one run.\n2. In every project chantier runs in, the worker card repeats the no-BOM rule where the fault\n   happens: the Coder's and the Conflict resolver's sections.\n\nThe CR has no tracker issue.\n\n## Today\n\n- `skills/chantier/references/concepts.md`, rule 9 (end of the \"Rules\" section), is the one full\n  statement: \"Every file you write, a PR body included, is UTF-8 without a byte-order mark (on\n  Windows PowerShell 5.1, `Out-File` and `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` add one).\"\n- `justfile`: `check: test release-notes`; `test` runs `uv run --group dev pytest -q` in\n  `skills/chantier/scripts/cli`.\n- `skills/chantier/scripts/cli/tests/test_structure.py` has one BOM check, inside\n  `test_both_hosts_give_a_role_the_same_body_and_their_own_frontmatter` (line ~120): an agent file\n  of either host must not start with `b\"\\xef\\xbb\\xbf\"`, with the comment \"a BOM hides the\n  frontmatter from a host\". Any other file passes with a BOM.\n- `test_a_byte_order_mark_from_windows_powershell_is_read_through` writes BOMs into files of a\n  temporary repository to prove the CLI reads through them. Those files are not tracked by this\n  repository; that test is unchanged.\n- No tracked file carries a BOM (checked on 2026-11-12: 291 tracked files, 0 with `EF BB BF` at\n  the head), so the check can be strict from its first day.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. The check, in `tests/test_structure.py`\n\nAdd one structure test to `skills/chantier/scripts/cli/tests/test_structure.py` (it then runs in\n`just test`, hence in `just check`, on the three systems in CI and in every Driver's fast gate; no\nnew recipe, no change to the `justfile`):\n\n- **Which files:** every file git tracks in this repository, listed with `git ls-files` run at\n  `PLUGIN_ROOT` (the repository's root, from `conftest.py`). Tracked files only: no untracked and\n  no ignored file is read. No list of exceptions, no filter by extension: a binary file is read\n  the same way (no binary format here starts with those bytes, and an exception list is where the\n  next BOM would hide).\n- **What is red:** a file whose first three bytes are `EF BB BF`. Nothing else: no UTF-16 or\n  UTF-32 mark, no check that the content is valid UTF-8, no line-ending check. Read the first\n  three bytes only, so the test stays fast.\n- **The message:** one run names every file with a BOM (collect them all, then assert the list is\n  empty), and says each must be UTF-8 without a byte-order mark. For example:\n  `files with a UTF-8 byte-order mark (write them as UTF-8 without one): evals/kit/dry_run.py, ...`.\n- **Portability:** the test uses the standard library and `subprocess` only, like the rest of the\n  file; paths from `git ls-files` are joined to `PLUGIN_ROOT` so they resolve on Windows too. A\n  tracked path absent from the working tree (deleted, not yet committed) has no bytes to check and\n  is not red. A failure of `git ls-files` itself fails the test (no fallback that would pass\n  silently).\n- **Consequence to know:** a file a Coder creates is untracked until the Driver commits it, so the\n  round's fast gate does not see it; the long gate (`$CHANTIER gate` runs on a clean, committed\n  tree) and CI do. A file the Coder modifies is tracked and is checked at once.\n\nThe existing agent-file check: the Driver chooses to keep it or to drop it in favour of the\ngeneral test, provided an agent file with a BOM stays red and the reason (a BOM hides the\nfrontmatter from a host) stays written in the test file.\n\n### 2. The worker card, `skills/chantier/references/worker.md`\n\nOne short line in each of two sections; `concepts.md` rule 9 stays the one full statement and is\nnot changed.\n\n- **`## Coder`:** never add a byte-order mark to a file: on Windows PowerShell 5.1, `Out-File`\n  and `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` add one ([concepts](concepts.md), rule 9). A file of the\n  project that already has a BOM keeps it: the rule is \"add none\", not \"strip all\".\n- **`## Conflict resolver`:** the same rule, plus a check before the rebase continues: each\n  resolved file starts as it did before the conflict (the base's encoding, so no new BOM, and a\n  BOM the file already had is kept) before `git rebase --continue`.\n\nWording is the Driver's, in the card's style (short sentences, no host tool named beyond the\nPowerShell cmdlets already named in rule 9; `test_each_host_has_its_reference_and_the_cards_hold_no_host_tool`\nmust stay green).\n\nNothing else changes: no agent file (`agents/`, `com.github.copilot/agents/`), no other card, no\nCLI code.\n\n## Decisions carried from `02-answers.md`\n\n| Question | Decision |\n| --- | --- |\n| Which files does the gate check? | Every tracked file, no exceptions; red when its first three bytes are the UTF-8 BOM. |\n| Untracked or ignored files? | No: tracked files only. |\n| Part of `just check` or a separate recipe? | Part of `just check` (via the structure tests), on the three systems; it stays fast. |\n| What does a red result say? | Every file with a BOM, in one run, and that it must be UTF-8 without a byte-order mark. |\n| UTF-16, UTF-32, invalid UTF-8? | No: the UTF-8 BOM only. |\n| Does the CLI (`gate`, `ship`) refuse a BOM in a user's project? | No: a project's own gate decides; the cards carry the rule. |\n| Which cards repeat the rule? | `worker.md`, Coder and Conflict resolver sections, one short line each, with the Windows cause and a pointer to `concepts.md` rule 9. |\n| \n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-199 `just check` rejects a byte-order mark; the worker cards repeat the rule\n\n🟡 working Round 1/3 · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** A coder and its reviews are working.\n**Next:** The long gate, then the ship.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [x] Round 1: add BOM structure test and worker.md lines per spec\n- [x] Note: r1 approved, committed as 1a891d4\n- [x] Note: resumed: tree clean at 1a891d4 on origin/main base; long gate (just check, just scenario) restarted\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [pr-body](work/pr-body.md)\n- [long gate log](work/gates/20261112-224819.log)\n- [README.md](../../../../../README.md)\n- [agents/coder.md](../../../../../agents/coder.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/concepts.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/worker.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/worker.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-199-just-check-rejects-a-byte-order-mark-the`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-199`\n- Driver: `a013dcd0cbeb6b04c`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- r1 approved, committed as 1a891d4\n- back to a Driver: resume: direct steering session stopped, batch moved to Foreman mode\n- resumed: tree clean at 1a891d4 on origin/main base; long gate (just check, just scenario) restarted\n\n## Cost\n\n$0.50 so far (at the last heartbeat).\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-200", "title": "Cost: steering turns are not grill turns", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/200-cost-steering-vs-grill", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Cost: steering turns are not grill turns\n\n## The request\n\nFrom the retro of real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137, finding 3): in a direct batch, `cost`\nmoves steering turns of the Chief of Staff to the line \"grill and specifications with the user\n(outside the budget)\" although no CR was grilled and no spec-writer ran.\n\n## The problem\n\n- In direct mode the Chief of Staff's transcript is priced from its claim as one row, `Chief of\n  Staff, steering the batch`, which counts in the spend and the budget. The turns that grill or\n  write specifications leave that row for the grill line, outside the budget\n  (`chief-of-staff.md`, \"Your epoch\").\n- `costs.py` decides this per turn (`_grill_use`, `GRILL_NAMES`): a turn is a grill turn as soon\n  as the input of any of its tool uses names `01-intent.md`, `02-answers.md`,\n  `03-change-spec.md` or `work/specifying`, or starts a spec-writer. The turn goes whole to the\n  grill line.\n- A steering session names those paths all the time without grilling: the prompt of each Driver\n  it starts names the CR's `03-change-spec.md`, and so may a message to a Driver, a command or a\n  plain read of a specification. Each such turn leaves the budgeted row.\n- Measured on run 1 (every call priced again from the raw transcripts; the per-role and per-CR\n  figures were exact to the cent): steering shown $1.18, really $3.61; the batch shown $9.41,\n  really $11.84 of a $20 budget; orchestration shown 50%, really 61%. The steering row also\n  shrank between two readings ($1.33 then $0.99), because later tool uses reclassified turns.\n- Consequences: the budget rail under-counts a direct batch, the report's steering share is\n  understated, and the scorecard's direct/Foreman comparison reads a wrong row.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nInside the steering window, a turn belongs to the grill only when it does grill or specification\nwork: it writes a CR's intent, answers, specification or `work/specifying` marker, or it starts a\nspec-writer. A path that is only named (in the prompt of another agent, a message, a command) or\nonly read is steering. The request's own wording (\"a path that only appears in a `take` output or\nan Agent prompt\") is the likely cause, not a proved one: a `take` output is a tool result, which\nthe attribution never reads, so the first step is to find on run 1's transcripts which tool uses\ndid reclassify the turns.\n\nDone when: on run 1's transcripts the grill line is $0 inside the steering window and the\nsteering row is the repriced figure ($3.61 at the close, to the cent of the report's method);\na turn that records an answer or starts a spec-writer while steering still goes to the grill\nline; the card's sentence on the split says the same thing as the CLI.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The split outside a steering window (a Chief of Staff beside a Foreman): unchanged.\n- The shape of the cost table and of the reports: the same lines, only the figures move.\n- The default budget, and any re-run of the eval; the run 1 report already carries the\n  correction and is not rewritten.\n- Copilot's cost path (`copilot-session-usage`), which has no such split.\n", "02-answers.md": "- Q: Inside the steering window, what makes a turn a grill turn? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): only a write of a CR's `01-intent.md`, `02-answers.md`, `03-change-spec.md` or `work/specifying` marker, or the start of a spec-writer; a path that is only named (an Agent prompt that is not a spec-writer's, a message to a Driver, a command) or only read is steering. The request names two causes as likely, not proved, and a rule on what the turn does covers them and the next one; an error on this side counts too much in the budget, which is the safe side.\n- Q: Is the request's diagnosis taken as given? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, the CR first finds on run 1's transcripts which tool uses reclassified the turns, and says so in its PR. A `take` output is a tool result, which `costs.py` never reads, so part of the stated cause cannot be right as written.\n- Q: What proves the fix? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): run 1's transcripts priced again: a grill line of $0 inside the steering window and a steering row equal to the repriced figure of the run 1 report ($3.61 at the close); plus a case where a real grill turn (an answer recorded, a spec-writer started) still leaves the row. The run gave exact figures to check against, and both directions of the split must hold.\n- Q: A turn that both steers and records an answer: where does it go? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): whole to the grill line, as today. A turn is attributed whole, and splitting inside a turn is a larger change nobody measured a need for.\n- Q: Does the split change outside the steering window (a Chief of Staff beside a Foreman, the whole-session line)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The request and the measure are about the steering window; that Chief of Staff starts no Driver and its spend is outside the budget either way.\n- Q: Does the card change too? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, the sentence of `chief-of-staff.md` (\"Your epoch\") that defines the grill turns says the new rule, in the same PR. The card says today \"a turn that names\", which is the bug written down; card and CLI must agree.\n- Q: Does the default budget, or the shape of the cost table or of a report, change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, the same lines with corrected figures. This is a fix towards what the card already promises (steering counts in the budget); a direct batch will show a higher, true steering share, and the release notes say so in one line.\n- Q: Is the run 1 report rewritten, or the eval run again? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): neither. The report already carries the corrected figures, and an eval is the user's to start.\n- Q: Which gate? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and `just scenario`. It changes the CLI and a card (AGENTS.md).\n- Q: Decision 3's target is $3.61, the steering window gives about $3.56; which? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): the window's own figure (about $3.56, grill $0 inside the window); the PR explains the gap (the calls between `open` and the claim stay outside the window, decision 5).\n- Q: A shell command that writes a CR's file counts as steering under the new rule; keep it? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): yes, the safe side (that spend counts in the budget); no shell-write detection. The card says to write a CR's files with the host's Write tool.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# Cost: steering turns are not grill turns\n\n## Goal\n\nIn a direct batch, `cost` must count the Chief of Staff's steering as steering. Today a turn\nthat only *names* a CR's specification (in a Driver's prompt, for example) moves from the\nbudgeted `Chief of Staff, steering the batch` row to the `Chief of Staff, grill and\nspecifications with the user (outside the budget)` line. So the budget rail under-counts a direct\nbatch, the report's steering share is too low, and the scorecard compares direct and Foreman mode\non a wrong row. Run 1 of real-direct (2026-11-12, finding 3) showed steering at $1.18 instead of\nabout $3.6, the batch at $9.41 instead of $11.84, and orchestration at 50% instead of 61%.\n\nThe fix: inside the steering window, a turn goes to the grill line only when it does grill or\nspecification work. It must write a CR's intent, answers, specification or `work/specifying`\nmarker, or start a spec-writer. Naming a path, or reading one, is steering.\n\n## The rule (decisions of 02-answers.md)\n\n1. **Inside the steering window, a turn is a grill turn only if one of its tool uses:**\n   - **writes** one of these: a CR's `01-intent.md`, `02-answers.md`, `03-change-spec.md` or\n     `work/specifying` marker. A write is a host write tool whose **target path** ends in one of\n     those names: `Write`, `Edit`, `MultiEdit` (`file_path`), `NotebookEdit` (`notebook_path`). On\n     Windows, compare with `\\` turned into `/`. Only the target path counts: a write to another\n     file whose *content* names such a path is steering. Run 1's `retro.md` is one.\n   - **starts a spec-writer**: an `Agent` (or `Task`) tool use whose `subagent_type` ends in\n     `spec-writer`. This is unchanged.\n\n   Everything else is steering:\n   - an `Agent` prompt that is not a spec-writer's, even if it names `03-change-spec.md`;\n   - a message to a Driver;\n   - a shell command (`Bash`, `PowerShell`), whatever paths it names, even one that writes\n     `02-answers.md` or `work/specifying`: no shell-write detection (02-answers.md, the shell-writes\n     answer). This is the safe side: that spend counts in the budget. The card tells the Chief of\n     Staff to write a CR's files with the host's Write tool (see \"Cards\");\n   - a `Read`, `Grep` or `Glob` of those files.\n\n   This rule decides by what the turn does, so it covers the causes the request suspected and any\n   later one. A mistake on this side counts too much in the budget, which is the safe side.\n2. **A turn is still attributed whole.** A turn that steers and also records an answer (or starts\n   a spec-writer) goes entirely to the grill line, as today. Nothing is split inside a turn.\n3. **Outside the steering window nothing changes.** This covers a Chief of Staff beside a Foreman,\n   the part of a direct batch's Chief of Staff session before its claim or after a Foreman took\n   over, and the `Chief of Staff, whole session` line. There the old rule stays: any tool use\n   whose input names one of `GRILL_NAMES`, or that starts a spec-writer, makes a grill turn. That\n   Chief of Staff starts no Driver, and its spend is outside the budget either way.\n4. **The diagnosis is not taken as given.** The request says the cause is \"a path that only\n   appears in a `take` output or an Agent prompt\". A `take` output is a tool result, and\n   `costs.py` never reads tool results, so that part cannot be right as written. The Driver\n   finds, on run 1's transcript, which tool uses moved the turns. The PR description says what it\n   found (see \"Evidence\").\n5. **The card says the same thing as the CLI**, in the same PR (see \"Cards\").\n6. **Same lines, corrected figures.** The default budget, the shape of the `cost` table, the\n   `cost --summary`, STATUS.md's Cost section and the reports do not change. A direct batch will\n   now show a higher, true steering share. The PR description carries **one line for the next\n   release notes** that says so. No release-notes file is written in this PR: the release CR\n   writes them.\n7. **The run 1 target is the steering window's own figure** (02-answers.md, the answer on\n   decision 3's target): about **$3.56** at the close, with grill at $0 inside the window, not the\n   report's $3.61. The PR explains the gap: the calls between `open` and the claim are outside the\n   steering window and stay on the grill line (rule 3).\n8. **No rewrite of the run 1 report and no new eval run.** `evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`\n   already carries the corrected figures, and only the user starts an eval.\n\n## What changes\n\n### CLI: `skills/chantier/scripts/cli/src/chantier/costs.py`\n\n- `GRILL_NAMES` stays as it is.\n- `_grill_use(block)` today returns true when the JSON of the tool use's input contains a\n  `GRILL_NAMES` entry anywhere, or when it starts a spec-writer. It gets a strict form for the\n  steering window: a spec-writer start, or a write tool whose target path ends in a\n  `GRILL_NAMES` entry (rule 1). The loose form (today's) stays for tool uses outside the\n  steering window (rule 3). The Driver chooses how to express this: one function with a\n  parameter, or two functions.\n- `usage(path, since, until, split, prices)` needs to know where the steering window is, so that\n  it judges each tool use by the rule of the window it falls in. Today the steering window is the\n  `since`/`until` of the call made with the claim window. The `since` call, made from the batch's\n  opening, also covers the window.\n- **Invariant:** one transcript gives one classification per turn, whichever call prices it. The\n  `steered` call (the claim window) and the `since` call (from the batch's opening) in\n  `_chief_line` must agree on every turn inside the window. Otherwise the lines stop adding up:\n  `steer` is computed as `since` minus `steered`, and the grill line is `since`'s grill. Today\n  `grill_turns` is built from every assistant record, including records outside the `since`/`until`\n  window (the check sits before the `inside` test). Under the rule of the window a tool use fall\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-200 Cost: steering turns are not grill turns\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n- [scenario/README.md](../../../../../scenario/README.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-200-cost-steering-turns-are-not-grill-turns`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-200`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-201", "title": "Change Specification: boot mode by surface, README rework and the board's docs", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/201-boot-mode-by-surface", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Boot mode by surface, README rework and the board's docs\n\n## The request\n\nOne change request, in three parts (the user, 2026-11-12; first filed \"after the v0.9.0 release\",\nthen moved before it by the user):\n\n1. **Mode by surface.** `/chantier:chief-of-staff` with no argument means Foreman mode in a local\n   session and direct mode under Remote Control. Two arguments force one: `foreman` or `direct`.\n2. **README rework.** One boot skill named in \"How chantier works\"; a new opening for \"Planning\n   modes\" (what a CR is, from an intent to a Change Specification) and for \"Batch modes\" (why\n   batches: plan a set of CRs, grill by day, let the batch run overnight up to a ready-to-merge\n   stack); a \"Releases\" section that says a release procedure goes from one package publish to a\n   long procedure, with the example of a project publishing to GitHub Pages and a marketplace;\n   \"What is chantier?\" and \"Repository layout\" leave the README for `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n3. **The board mod, documented.** A short README section with one screenshot\n   (`board-mod-batch.png`) and a link to a richer docs page that says what the mod is, how it\n   works and how to open it, illustrated with the screenshots of `intake/screenshots/`. Direct\n   mode is illustrated with `direct-mode-session.png`, and the docs say that a Chief of Staff\n   steering a direct batch declines to grill and points to a Griller session.\n\n## The problem\n\n- **The mode is a thing to know before starting.** Today the user picks the mode at the entry:\n  no argument is Foreman mode, `direct` is direct mode\n  (`skills/chief-of-staff/SKILL.md`, `chief-of-staff.md` \"Starting a batch\", step 2). Under\n  Remote Control a Foreman cannot be started (`hosts/claude-code.md`, \"Classes of session\":\n  `start_session` is refused), so the no-argument entry leads to a plan that proposes direct mode\n  and waits for a yes. The surface already tells which mode can run; the user should not have to.\n- **The README explains chantier twice and late.** \"What is chantier?\" repeats, after six diagram\n  sections, what the top bullets and the diagrams say, and \"Repository layout\" is a contributor's\n  matter. \"Planning modes\" and \"Batch modes\" open on the mechanism, not on what a CR is or why a\n  batch exists. \"Releases\" is two lines and does not say how far a project's procedure can go.\n- **The board mod is nearly invisible.** `docs/boards.md` holds one dense paragraph and no\n  picture; the README only links it under \"More\". Nothing shows what the pane looks like, what\n  its header counts, or when its cost moves.\n\n## Where it shows\n\n- The entries: `skills/chief-of-staff/SKILL.md` (description, last paragraph),\n  `skills/chantier/SKILL.md` (the router asks three roles: Chief of Staff, Chief of Staff in\n  direct mode, Griller), `.github/plugin/plugin.json` (descriptions).\n- The cards: `session-start.md` (the table of section 2, the introduction's \"Direct or Foreman\"\n  bullet, the remote line), `chief-of-staff.md` (the row \"a batch\", \"Starting a batch\" step 2,\n  \"Switching to Foreman mode\"), `hosts/claude-code.md` (\"Classes of session\", \"Surfaces and\n  options\", \"A Chief of Staff and Grillers in remote\"), `hosts/copilot.md` (how the argument is\n  passed is unproved there).\n- The docs: `README.md`, `docs/hosts.md`, `docs/use-cases*.md`, `docs/tutorial-claude-code.md`,\n  `docs/boards.md`, the two simple diagrams and their `-texts.md`\n  (`how-chantier-works-simple-foreman`, `-direct`: each names its entry), `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n- The eval runbooks (`evals/*/RUNBOOK.md`) start a session with the entry; `real-direct` already\n  passes `direct`.\n\n## What would fix it\n\n- **The mode is read from the surface when it is not forced.** With no argument, the session's\n  batches are Foreman batches when the user is at the machine and direct batches when the host\n  signals Remote Control (its notice in the conversation, `hosts/claude-code.md`, \"Surfaces and\n  options\"). `foreman` and `direct` force one; the user's word (\"you are in direct mode\") still\n  changes it. A forced `foreman` where no Foreman can start is refused with the reason, as today.\n  The known path to a remote Chief of Staff is \"start locally, then turn on Remote Control\"\n  (`hosts/claude-code.md`), so the surface is read when the mode matters, at the plan, not frozen\n  at the invocation.\n- **The README**, top to bottom: the bullets; \"How chantier works\" opening on \"a new session and\n  one boot skill, `/chantier:chief-of-staff`, with or without an argument\"; \"Planning modes\" and\n  \"Batch modes\" with the user's openings; the two lifecycle sections unchanged; \"Releases\" with\n  the range of procedures and the GitHub Pages plus marketplace example, ending on the retro that\n  improves the procedure the project keeps; a board section with one screenshot and the link;\n  then Install, Use, More, Develop. \"What is chantier?\" and \"Repository layout\" go to\n  `CONTRIBUTING.md` (this reverses CR-195's \"What is Chantier? stays in the README\").\n- **The board's docs page**: what the mod is (a read-only pane of Claude Code over the planning\n  folder's `STATUS.json`), how to open it (`/chantier-board`, or loading the `chantier` or\n  `chief-of-staff` skill), how to read it, with the screenshots in the order of a batch\n  (just opened, PRs and costs, waiting for you, merged, done). Two facts the screenshots raise are\n  said: the header's \"waiting for you\" counts unread asks as well as the table's rows; the\n  batch's cost is priced when the steering session refreshes the status, not live ($0.57 over\n  three screenshots, then $9.22 once done). `status-report-done.png` illustrates what \"status\" or\n  `/chantier:status` gives.\n- The screenshots are copied from `intake/screenshots/` to `docs/`; the intake is never edited.\n\n## What the screenshots show (seven, all read)\n\n- `board-mod-batch.png` (dark): batch-01 just opened, 3 CRs working round 1/3, 2 queued, no PR,\n  no cost. The README's one picture.\n- `board-mod-batch-prs-light.png`: $0.57 of $20, three\n\n…", "02-answers.md": "# Answers: boot mode by surface, README rework and the board's docs\n\nGrill of 2026-11-12. The user's word that day: \"fait toutes les P1 et P2 en terminant sur la\nrelease. pour la plupart je ne pourrais pas te repondre, utilise un advisor Fable. demande moi\nuniquement quand il y a un impact utilisateur\". The advisor answers in the user's place; the\nquestions with user impact are under \"Open for the user\", each with a recommendation, not decided.\n\n## The mode\n\n- Q: How is \"remote\" told from \"local\"? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): by the host's own signal only, the Remote Control notice in the conversation (`hosts/claude-code.md`, \"Surfaces and options\"); no notice means local. It is the one signal proved in use, and a missing notice fails safe: Foreman mode is chosen, and where no Foreman can start the existing rule of the plan applies.\n- Q: When is the surface read: at the invocation, or when a batch starts? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): at the plan of each batch, and the introduction says which mode the session would use now. The known path to a remote Chief of Staff is \"start locally, then turn on Remote Control\"; a mode frozen at the invocation would make that session a Foreman one, the opposite of the request.\n- Q: Does a forced argument hold for the whole session? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, `foreman` or `direct` holds for every batch of the session until the user's word changes it; the user's word (\"you are in direct mode\", \"use a Foreman\") keeps working with or without an argument. This is today's rule for `direct`, extended to `foreman`.\n- Q: What does a forced `foreman` do where no Foreman can start (Remote Control with no Foreman to reuse, a terminal)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it is refused at the plan with the reason and what the user can do (say \"direct\", or start from the desktop); under Remote Control with a Foreman to reuse, it reuses it. The project's rule is no fallback: a forced mode is never swapped silently.\n- Q: Is `direct` still allowed locally? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, unchanged. Small batches and the `real-direct` eval rely on it.\n- Q: Does a running batch change mode when the user changes surface (goes to the phone, comes back)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. A batch keeps the steering it was opened with; \"Switching to Foreman mode\" stays the user's word, and a Foreman batch does not become direct. A Foreman batch followed from the phone already works (orders to the Foreman).\n- Q: How is the argument read when a request follows it (`/chantier:chief-of-staff direct Run the campaign …`)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the first word alone, when it is exactly `foreman` or `direct`; anything else is the user's request and forces nothing. The eval runbooks already use this form.\n- Q: What about a cloud session and Copilot? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): unchanged. A cloud session has no main checkout and stops at the start checks; on Copilot the no-argument mode follows what the host's reference already says (an SDK host starts a Foreman, an agent session is direct), and how Copilot passes `foreman` or `direct` stays marked unproved. The request names Remote Control only.\n- Q: Does the CLI learn the surface or the aliases? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The CLI knows no role and no host signal; `open --direct` and `preflight --direct` stay the only switches, set by the Chief of Staff.\n- Q: Do the eval runbooks change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `real-foreman` and `full` start with `foreman` forced, `real-direct` keeps `direct`. An eval must not depend on whether Remote Control happens to be on.\n\n## The README and the docs\n\n- Q: Is this one CR or two (the mode; the README and the board's docs)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): one, as the user filed it (\"Met une CR\"). The README's new \"How chantier works\" opening describes the new entry, so the two halves would conflict if run apart.\n- Q: Are the user's English openings for \"Planning modes\" and \"Batch modes\" taken word for word? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): their content and order, in the README's plain English (grammar fixed, \"Change Request\" and \"batch\" as the README spells them). The user dictated a draft, not a final text.\n- Q: Does the \"Releases\" example promise that chantier publishes to a marketplace or posts a thread? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. It is written as an example of a project's own procedure that the Release Driver follows with the tools the session has, each irreversible step on the user's approval; \"about ten minutes\" for the VS Code marketplace is given as an order of size. The README must not claim a capability chantier does not ship.\n- Q: Where do \"What is chantier?\" and \"Repository layout\" land in `CONTRIBUTING.md`? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): under \"How the project is built\", moved as they are, with their links fixed. The user asked for a move, not a rewrite; CR-195's \"stays in the README\" is reversed on the user's later word.\n- Q: Does the README still say somewhere what the moved \"What is chantier?\" alone said (the CR's files, the grill, the spec-writer)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, through the new \"Planning modes\" opening (intent, then Change Specification) and the tutoria\n\n…", "03-change-spec.md": "# Change Specification: boot mode by surface, README rework and the board's docs\n\nOne CR, one PR, in three parts (the user, 2026-11-12, \"Met une CR\"): the mode a Chief of Staff's\nbatches use follows the surface unless forced; the README is reworked; the CR board mod is\ndocumented with the user's screenshots. Sources: `intake/request.md`, `01-intent.md`,\n`02-answers.md` (an advisor's answers given in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12,\nplus the user's own two answers at its end). No tracker issue.\n\n## Goal, in the user's terms\n\n- `/chantier:chief-of-staff` with no argument: Foreman mode in a local session at the desktop,\n  direct mode under Remote Control (and wherever a Foreman cannot start). Two arguments force one:\n  `foreman` or `direct`.\n- The README says that everything starts with a new session and one boot skill,\n  `/chantier:chief-of-staff`, with or without an argument; it opens \"Planning modes\" on what a CR\n  is and \"Batch modes\" on why batches exist; \"Releases\" shows how far a project's procedure can go;\n  a new section shows the CR board; \"What is chantier?\" and \"Repository layout\" move to\n  `CONTRIBUTING.md`.\n- The CR board mod gets a real docs page, with pictures: what it is, how to open it, how to read it.\n\n## Part 1: the mode by surface\n\n### The rule (decisions of 02-answers.md)\n\n1. **The argument.** Only the invocation's first word counts, and only when it is exactly\n   `foreman` or `direct`. Anything else (`init`, `prepare yourself…`, `Run the campaign…`) is the\n   user's request and forces nothing. `/chantier:chief-of-staff direct Run the campaign …` is\n   `direct` plus a request.\n2. **Forced holds for the session.** `foreman` or `direct` holds for every batch of the session\n   until the user's word changes it. The user's word (\"you are in direct mode\", \"use a Foreman\")\n   keeps working with or without an argument, and also holds for the session (today's rule for\n   `direct`, extended to `foreman`).\n3. **No argument: the mode this surface can run.** Read at the plan of each batch, never frozen at\n   the invocation (the known path to a remote Chief of Staff is \"start locally, then turn on Remote\n   Control\", so a session started at the desk and later driven from the phone must plan its next\n   batch as direct):\n   - **direct** when the conversation carries the host's Remote Control notice\n     (`hosts/claude-code.md`, \"Surfaces and options\"), even if a Foreman could be reused;\n   - **direct** on every host that cannot start a Foreman: a terminal, VS Code, and on Copilot a\n     session where the session tools do not load. The test is the one the card already has: the\n     Foreman-mode preflight's P1 (`$CHANTIER preflight`, host is Claude Desktop or a Copilot agent\n     session) and P2 (the host's session tools load: `start_session` on Claude Code;\n     `create_session` and `send_message` on Copilot). This reconciles the two answers on Copilot:\n     a Copilot host that can start a Foreman session (the SDK host) runs Foreman, one that cannot\n     runs direct; how Copilot passes the argument stays **unproved**;\n   - **Foreman** otherwise. No notice means local: a missing notice fails safe to Foreman, and\n     where no Foreman can start the bullet above applies.\n4. **The plan says it, no question.** The plan states the mode and why in one line (\"direct: this\n   session is under Remote Control\", \"Foreman: at the desktop\", \"direct: forced by the argument\")\n   and, when direct, what that changes (no Foreman session; the Chief of Staff steers the Drivers;\n   no grill while the batch runs, backlog work and the spec-writer stay open; new ideas go to the\n   inbox). There is no separate yes for the mode: the go is the yes. The user may still change the\n   mode before the go by saying so.\n5. **A forced `foreman` where no Foreman can start** (Remote Control with no Foreman to reuse, a\n   terminal, VS Code) is refused at the plan, with the reason and what the user can do (say\n   \"direct\", or start from the desktop app). Under Remote Control with a Foreman to reuse, it reuses\n   it (`hosts/claude-code.md`, \"Reusing the last Foreman\"). A forced mode is never swapped silently\n   (no fallback).\n6. **`direct` stays allowed locally**, unchanged (small batches, the `real-direct` eval).\n7. **A batch keeps the steering it was opened with.** Going to the phone or coming back changes no\n   running or paused batch. \"Switching to Foreman mode\" stays the user's word, unchanged; a Foreman\n   batch never becomes direct. A Foreman batch followed from the phone keeps working through orders.\n8. **The introduction** (first turn) says which mode this session would use now, and why, in the\n   \"Direct or Foreman\" bullet.\n9. **The router** (`/chantier:chantier`) asks two roles only: the Chief of Staff or a Griller. It\n   loads `chief-of-staff` with no argument, and the mode follows the surface.\n10. **Unchanged:** a cloud session (no main checkout, stops at the start checks); the CLI (no\n    command, no flag, no surface detection: `open --direct` and `preflight --direct` stay the only\n    switches, set by the Chief of Staff); no new skill (`foreman` and `direct` are arguments of the\n    one entry).\n\n### Files\n\n- `skills/chief-of-staff/SKILL.md`: the description's last sentence and the body's last paragraph\n  say: with no argument the session's batches follow the surface (Foreman at the desktop, direct\n  under Remote Control or where no Foreman can start); a first word `foreman` or `direct` forces\n  one for the session; the user's word changes it; pointer to `chief-of-staff.md`, \"Starting a\n  batch\". The description's \"(Foreman mode on Claude Desktop or a Copilot SDK host, direct mode\n  anywhere)\" stays true and may stay.\n- `skills/chantier/SKILL.md`: the description (\"the Chief of Staff (with a Foreman, or in direct\n  mode) or a Griller\") and the first bullet ask two roles, Chief of Staff or Griller, and load\n  `chief-of-staff` with no argument or `griller`. Remove \"(with the argument `direct` fo\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-201 Change Specification: boot mode by surface, README rework and the board's docs\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [README.md](../../../../../README.md)\n- [docs/boards.md](../../../../../docs/boards.md)\n- [docs/costs.md](../../../../../docs/costs.md)\n- [docs/hosts.md](../../../../../docs/hosts.md)\n- [docs/planning-folder.md](../../../../../docs/planning-folder.md)\n- [docs/tutorial-claude-code.md](../../../../../docs/tutorial-claude-code.md)\n- [docs/use-cases-direct.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases-direct.md)\n- [docs/use-cases-foreman.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases-foreman.md)\n- [docs/use-cases.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases.md)\n- [evals/full/RUNBOOK.md](../../../../../evals/full/RUNBOOK.md)\n- [evals/real-direct/RUNBOOK.md](../../../../../evals/real-direct/RUNBOOK.md)\n- [evals/real-foreman/RUNBOOK.md](../../../../../evals/real-foreman/RUNBOOK.md)\n- [skills/chantier/SKILL.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/SKILL.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/hosts/claude-code.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/hosts/claude-code.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/hosts/copilot.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/hosts/copilot.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/init.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/init.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/session-start.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/session-start.md)\n- [skills/chief-of-staff/SKILL.md](../../../../../skills/chief-of-staff/SKILL.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-201-change-specification-boot-mode-by-surfac`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-201`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-202", "title": "Change Specification: the board's Refresh button and refresh period", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/202-board-refresh-button", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Board mod: a Refresh button and the refresh period\n\n## The request\n\nThe user asked how often the `/chantier-board` pane refreshes and how to force it, then asked for\n(2026-11-12): a **Refresh button** in the pane, and a **note** saying how often it refreshes\n(\"refreshed every ...\"), as a CR of its own, apart from `boot-mode-by-surface`.\n\n## The problem\n\n- The pane checks its files every 15 seconds (`EVERY_MS`, `hooks/register.tsx`) and nothing in\n  the pane says so: the period is written only in `docs/boards.md`.\n- The only way to force a check is `/chantier-board` twice (hide, then show), which also drops the\n  page the user was reading (a CR's sheet, a document) since opening resets the pages.\n- The header's \"read Ns ago\" is the age of the last *read*, and the file is read again only when\n  its modification time changed. So on a quiet batch it shows \"read 45s ago\" (the\n  `board-mod-batch-done.png` screenshot of `boot-mode-by-surface`) although a check ran less than\n  15 seconds before. A note \"refreshed every 15 s\" beside \"read 45s ago\" would look like a\n  contradiction, and a Refresh press on an unchanged file would change nothing on screen.\n\n## Where it shows\n\n- `hooks/register.tsx`: `check()` (the indexes, then the top page), `checkIndex()` (skips an\n  unchanged file), the timer in `begin()`, the header line of `Board` (\"N live · N waiting for\n  you · read Ns ago\"), the page views with their \"< Back\" button, the single-line views (missing\n  or wrong-schema `STATUS.json`).\n- `hooks/register.test.tsx`: the pane's tests, run by `just mod`.\n- `types/index.d.ts`: the mod's contract, if the state it keeps changes shape.\n- `docs/boards.md`: \"It refreshes every 15 seconds while open\".\n\n## What would fix it\n\n- A **Refresh** button in the pane that runs a check at once, keeps the pane open, and keeps the\n  page and the folds as they are.\n- A press shows that it did something: the age in the header returns to 0.\n- A **note** that states the period, from the same constant as the timer, worded so that it does\n  not contradict the age beside it.\n- `docs/boards.md` says the button exists and what it does not do.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Pricing or refreshing the batch's record: the button reads files again and nothing more. The\n  cost on the board moves when the steering session refreshes the status (`status --refresh`);\n  the pane writes nothing and runs no process but `git rev-parse`, and that stays.\n- A configurable period, a pause of the timer, a setting in `chantier.yaml`.\n- The remote board (`chantier board`, the web page that reloads every 30 seconds): a browser has\n  its own reload.\n- The README and the board's documentation page with screenshots: `boot-mode-by-surface`.\n\n## Gates\n\nA change under `hooks/` (and `types/` if the state changes): `just mod` before the PR, run by the\nagent itself, which stops and says so if it cannot (`AGENTS.md`); `just check` as always. No\nchange to the CLI, the cards or the scenario: `just scenario` does not apply.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers: the board's Refresh button and refresh period\n\nGrill of 2026-11-12. The user's word that day: \"fait toutes les P1 et P2 en terminant sur la\nrelease. pour la plupart je ne pourrais pas te repondre, utilise un advisor Fable. demande moi\nuniquement quand il y a un impact utilisateur\". The advisor answers in the user's place; the\nquestions with user impact are under \"Open for the user\", each with a recommendation, not decided.\n\n- Q: Does Refresh do more than read the files again (price the batch, run `status --refresh`, call the CLI)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The pane stays read-only: it writes nothing and runs no process but `git rev-parse`; the batch's cost is the steering session's to refresh. The docs say so, since a user will expect the cost to move.\n- Q: Does a press keep what the user was looking at? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes: the pane stays open, the page shown (a CR's sheet, a document) and the folds stay. Losing the page is what makes hide-then-show a poor way to force a check today.\n- Q: Does a press also look for the planning folders again (a project added to a surface, a `.agents/changes/` created after the pane opened)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes. It is what hide-then-show does today, and \"No chantier status here\" is a moment where the user presses it.\n- Q: Does the periodic check change (period, what it skips)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no: 15 seconds, and a file is read again only when its modification time changed. The request is a button and a note, not a new rhythm.\n- Q: Is the period configurable? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. Nobody asked, and `chantier.yaml` holds no setting of the mod; the note prints the same constant the timer uses, so the two cannot drift.\n- Q: Does the remote board (`chantier board`) get the same button and note? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, out of scope. The user spoke of the `/chantier-board` pane, and a browser has its own reload.\n- Q: A press while a check is already running, or repeated presses? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): harmless and unthrottled: a check only reads a few local files. A failure shows in the board's existing note line, as for the periodic check.\n- Q: Does this CR wait for `boot-mode-by-surface` (both touch `docs/boards.md`)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no; no `depends.json` for a shared file. This CR changes the one sentence on refreshing; the second to merge rebases. Screenshots taken before this CR will lack the button, which the docs page may say or retake later.\n- Q: Which gates? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just mod` (run by the agent itself; it stops if it cannot) and `just check`; new tests in `hooks/register.test.tsx` for the press and the note. This is `AGENTS.md` for a change under `hooks/`.\n\n## Open for the user\n\n1. **Where the button sits, what the note says, what a press shows.** Recommended:\n   - one **Refresh** button at the top of the pane, on every view: above the header line on the\n     board, beside \"< Back\" on a CR's sheet or a document, and on the one-line views (\"No chantier\n     status here ...\");\n   - a press reads the files again **even when unchanged**, so the header returns to \"0s ago\":\n     without that, a press on a quiet batch changes nothing on screen and the button looks dead;\n   - the header keeps one line and one wording: `5 live · 0 waiting for you · read 45s ago ·\n     refreshed every 15 s` becomes `5 live · 0 waiting for you · checked 3s ago, every 15 s`.\n     Reason: today \"read Ns ago\" is the age of the last time the file *changed and was read*, so\n     it shows 45 s although a check ran under 15 s ago; beside \"refreshed every 15 s\" it would\n     read as a bug. The alternative is the user's literal wording, \"read Ns ago · refreshed every\n     15 s\", with the age reset at every check.\n\n- Q: (open) The button's place, the note's wording, what a press shows? — User (2026-11-12, \"board-refresh-button: ok, en haut a droite si possible\"): the advisor's recommendation (one Refresh button on every view of the pane; a press re-reads even an unchanged file, so the age returns to 0; header `... - checked 3s ago, every 15 s`), with the button at the top right of the pane where the mod's layout allows it.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# Change Specification: the board's Refresh button and refresh period\n\n## Goal\n\nThe user asked how often the `/chantier-board` pane refreshes and how to force it (2026-11-12).\nToday nothing in the pane says the period, the only way to force a check is `/chantier-board`\ntwice (which drops the page being read), and the header's \"read Ns ago\" is the age of the last\ntime the file *changed*, so it shows \"read 45s ago\" on a quiet batch although a check ran under\n15 seconds before.\n\nThis CR gives the pane:\n\n1. a **Refresh** button, on every view, that checks at once without hiding the pane and without\n   losing the page or the folds, and that visibly does something (the age returns to 0);\n2. a **note of the period** in the header, from the same constant as the timer, worded so that it\n   does not contradict the age beside it.\n\nNo tracker issue: no `issue:` line.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. The Refresh button (`hooks/register.tsx`, the `ui.render` handler of the `Pane`)\n\n- One button, label `Refresh`, key `refresh`, a plain `Button` like `< Back`.\n- **Where:** at the top right of the pane, on every view, where the mod's layout allows it (the\n  user's word: \"en haut a droite si possible\"). If the mod's `Box` can lay out a row with the\n  button pushed to the right (for example a `flexDirection=\"row\"` box whose props align it right),\n  the button sits on the pane's first line, at its right. If it cannot, the button is the pane's\n  first line, at its top; the PR says which was possible and why.\n- **Every view:** the board (one project or a surface's several projects: one button for the\n  whole pane, not one per project), a CR's sheet, an inbox or backlog item's file list, a folder,\n  a document, a page that can show nothing (`top.note`), and each one-line view: `where.error`\n  (e.g. \"Cannot look for the main checkout: ...\"), \"Looking for the chantier files...\", a\n  `fatal` schema line, \"No chantier status here: ... not found.\", and \"Reading the status...\".\n  On a page view the button sits on the same top line as `< Back`, `< Back` at the left.\n  A one-line view stays one line of text, with the button above it or beside it.\n\n### 2. What a press does\n\nA press runs, at once:\n\n1. **Looks for the planning folders again**, as opening does (`findRoot`, then `findBases`): a\n   project added to a surface, or a `.agents/changes/` created after the pane opened, appears; a\n   view whose folder is still found keeps its state; a new folder gets a new view; a folder no\n   longer found drops its view. A `findRoot` failure is shown as today (`where.error`).\n2. **Checks every planning folder and the top page, reading each file again even when its\n   modification time did not change** (a forced read; the periodic check keeps its skip). The\n   parse failure, wrong schema and missing file cases behave exactly as in the periodic check.\n3. Records the time of the check (`checkedAt`), as every check does.\n\nA press does **not**:\n\n- reset or drop the pages (`pages`): the page shown (a CR's sheet, a document, a file list) stays,\n  read again; unlike `begin()`, which empties them at opening;\n- change the folds (`turned`);\n- close, hide or reopen the pane, or start, cancel or restart the timer (exactly one timer while\n  the pane is open, as today);\n- write any file, call `$CHANTIER`, price the batch, or run any process but the `git rev-parse` of\n  `findRoot`: the pane stays read-only. The cost on the board moves when the steering session\n  refreshes the status (`status --refresh`), not on a press.\n\nPresses are **unthrottled**: a press while a check runs, or many presses in a row, are harmless\n(a check reads a few local files). A failure shows in the board's existing note line\n(`feed.note`, or the view's `note`), as for the periodic check; the press adds no new message\nkind.\n\nThe implementation shape is the Driver's (for example `check($, { force: true })` with\n`checkIndex` and `checkPage` taking the flag, and a `refresh($)` that re-finds the bases and\nmerges the views by `dir` without touching `pages`), within the behaviour above. Navigation\nhandlers already wrap their work in `guarded()`; the press does the same.\n\n### 3. The periodic check: unchanged\n\n`EVERY_MS = 15000` stays; the timer still calls `check($)`, which still reads a file again only\nwhen its modification time changed. The period is not configurable (no setting in `chantier.yaml`,\nno pause of the timer).\n\n### 4. The header: the age of the last check, and the period\n\nThe board's header line of each project (`Board`, today\n`` `${index.active_crs} live · ${waitingCount} waiting for you · read ${age}s ago` ``) becomes:\n\n```text\n5 live · 0 waiting for you · checked 3s ago, every 15 s\n```\n\n- `checked Ns ago` is the age of the **last check** (the `checkedAt` atom, set at the end of every\n  check, periodic or pressed), no longer the age of the last read (`View.readAt`). It is therefore\n  under 15 s on a quiet batch, and 0 right after a press.\n- `every 15 s` is written from `EVERY_MS` (`EVERY_MS / 1000`), never a literal `15`, so the note\n  and the timer cannot drift.\n- While no check has finished yet (`checkedAt` null), the line ends `· every 15 s` with no age.\n- One line, one wording, on each project's view of a surface too (they share the one `checkedAt`).\n\n`View.readAt` may stay or go; it is no longer drawn. The `checkedAt` atom keeps its shape\n(`number | null`, tag `checked-1`): no atom's shape changes, so no shape tag changes.\n\n### 5. `types/index.d.ts`\n\nNo shape change is needed. If the Driver touches it, only the comment of `checkedAt` (\"The time\nof the last check, drawn as the age of the read\") changes, to say it is drawn as the header's\n\"checked Ns ago\". Correcting the file's other drift from the code (`site` without `bases`, `feed`\nas `ChantierFeed` rather than `{ views, note }`) is not this CR.\n\n### 6. `docs/boards.md`, section \"The CR board (Claude Code)\"\n\nReplace the sentence \"It refreshes every 15 seconds while open, writes nothing, and runs no\nprocess but `git \n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-202 Change Specification: the board's Refresh button and refresh period\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [docs/boards.md](../../../../../docs/boards.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-202-change-specification-the-board-s-refresh`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-202`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-203", "title": "The steering session reads `status` before a note that carries work, and takes a ready CR instead of messaging it", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/203-rebase-note-check-status", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# The steering session reads `status` before a rebase note, and takes a ready CR instead of messaging it\n\n## The problem\n\nIn real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137) the steering session sent a `rebase:` note as a message\nto CR-101's Driver, believing the CR was `working`. The Driver had just handed it back ready. By\nits card the Driver could not start work on a note with no `taken back:`, so it passed the note\nback and waited for a formal take-back: one exchange lost, and a rebase delayed.\n\n## Where it shows\n\n- `skills/chantier/references/foreman.md` already has both paths: a `ready` or `blocked` CR goes\n  back to a Driver only through `take <cr> --note \"<why>\"`, then the Driver is resumed with\n  `note taken back: <the take's note>`; a `working` CR gets the message `[foreman→driver <cr>]\n  note rebase: …`. What is missing is the moment of the choice: nothing says to read the CR's\n  state just before sending, so the session acts on a state it read at its last wake.\n- `skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md`, \"Steering a batch yourself\": direct mode\n  follows the Foreman's card for the Drivers, so the same gap is there.\n- `skills/chantier/references/driver.md` handles the late note correctly (it passes it up and\n  waits); that is the safety net, and it is what cost the exchange.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nIn the steering card (foreman.md, which direct mode follows):\n\n- Just before sending a note that carries work to a Driver (a `rebase:` first of all), read\n  `$CHANTIER status` and choose the path from the state it shows now.\n- `ready` (or `blocked`): no message; `take <cr> --note \"<the note>\"` and the take-back path the\n  card already gives. `working`: the message, as today.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The Driver's rule for a note received after its hand-back: unchanged, it stays the net for the\n  rare case where the hand-back lands between the `status` and the message.\n- Any CLI change (no command that sends or refuses a note: messaging is the host's).\n- The order in which CRs are rebased after a merge, and the stack driver's restack.\n- The agent files of both hosts.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Does the rule cover the rebase note only, or every note that carries work to a Driver? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Every note that carries work (a `rebase:`, an order note, an answer), with the rebase note named as the first case. The race is the same for all of them, and one sentence covers them at no extra cost.\n- Q: Which read: a plain `status`, or the wake's `status --refresh --epoch`? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): A plain `$CHANTIER status`, which moves nothing. The read is a check before an action, not a wake, and must not count as a heartbeat.\n- Q: Does the rule hold in direct mode too? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes, for both steerings: the incident happened in a direct run, and the Chief of Staff follows the Foreman's card for its Drivers. The rule is written once, in the Foreman's card, and direct mode must visibly inherit it.\n- Q: For a CR found `ready`, is the whole take-back path run, or a shorter one for a rebase? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The whole path as the card has it (`agents set <cr> -`, `take <cr> --note`, the Driver resumed with `note taken back:`, `agents set` again). A second, shorter path would be a new thing to get wrong; the fix is to choose the existing path at the right moment.\n- Q: Does the Driver's side change, so that a `rebase:` note received just after a hand-back is acted on at once? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): No. The CR is `ready` in the record and its `handback` would be refused; the Driver passing the note up stays the net for the hand-back that lands between the `status` and the message.\n- Q: Should the CLI close the race (a command that sends the note, or refuses it on a ready CR)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): No. Messaging is the host's, the CLI knows no role, and one lost exchange in a rare window does not measure up to a new tool.\n- Q: Is the remaining window (a hand-back between the read and the message) accepted? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes. It is seconds wide, costs one exchange when it happens, and loses no work.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# The steering session reads `status` before a note that carries work, and takes a ready CR instead of messaging it\n\n## Goal\n\nWhen the steering session (a Foreman, or the Chief of Staff in direct mode) is about to send a\nDriver a note that carries work, a `rebase:` note first of all, it reads the CR's state just\nbefore, with a plain `$CHANTIER status`, and chooses the path from what that read shows now, not\nfrom the state it read at its last wake. A CR found `ready` or `blocked` gets no message: it is\ntaken back with `take <cr> --note \"<the note>\"` and the take-back path the Foreman's card already\ngives. A CR found `working` gets the message, as today.\n\nThis closes the race seen in real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137, finding 8 of\n`evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`): after PR #4 merged, the Chief of Staff sent\nCR-101's Driver a plain `note rebase:` just after that Driver had handed back ready. The Driver,\nby its card, refused to start work on a note with no `taken back:` and asked for a `take`: one\nexchange lost and a rebase delayed.\n\n## Decisions carried from `02-answers.md`\n\n1. **Scope of the rule:** every note that carries work to a Driver (a `rebase:` note, an order\n   note, an answer), with the `rebase:` note named as the first case. One sentence covers all of\n   them.\n2. **The read:** a plain `$CHANTIER status`, with no `--refresh` and no `--epoch`. It moves\n   nothing and is not a wake: it must not count as a heartbeat.\n3. **Both steerings:** the rule holds for the Foreman and for the Chief of Staff in direct mode.\n   It is written once, in the Foreman's card, and direct mode visibly inherits it (a line in\n   `chief-of-staff.md`, \"Steering a batch yourself\").\n4. **The path for a CR found `ready`:** the whole take-back path as the Foreman's card has it\n   (note the Driver's agent id, `agents set <cr> - --epoch <n>`, `take <cr> --note \"<the note>\"\n   --epoch <n>`, the Driver resumed with `[foreman→driver <cr>] note taken back: <the take's\n   note>`, `agents set <cr> <id> --epoch <n>` again; a Driver that does not answer goes by the\n   existing \"does not answer\" step). No second, shorter path for a rebase. A CR found `blocked`\n   keeps the existing path too (`take <cr> --note`, a new CR Driver).\n5. **The Driver's side is unchanged:** a note with no `taken back:` received after its hand-back\n   is still passed up and waited on (`driver.md`, the `[foreman→driver <cr>] note <text>` bullet).\n   That stays the net for a hand-back that lands between the `status` and the message.\n6. **No CLI change:** no command that sends a note or refuses one on a ready CR. Messaging is the\n   host's and the CLI knows no role.\n7. **The remaining window is accepted:** a hand-back between the read and the message is seconds\n   wide, costs one exchange when it happens, and loses no work.\n\n## What changes\n\nCards only, both in `skills/chantier/references/`. Both hosts read the same cards, so no\nhost-specific text is needed. Keep the cards' style: plain English, lines wrapped near 100\ncharacters, CLI commands in backticks.\n\n### `skills/chantier/references/foreman.md`\n\n1. **\"Orders are the user's word\" bullet** (section \"Your rules\", the bullet that ends \"...before\n   `handback ready`\", around lines 56-66). It already says that work on a ready or blocked CR\n   reaches a Driver only through `take <cr> --note \"<why>\"`, never as a message. Add the moment of\n   the choice, in substance:\n\n   > Just before you send a Driver a note that carries work (a `rebase:` first of all, an order\n   > note, an answer), read the CR's state with a plain `$CHANTIER status` (no `--refresh`, no\n   > `--epoch`: it is a check before an action, not a wake, and records no heartbeat), and choose\n   > the path from the state it shows now, never from the one read at your last wake: `ready` or\n   > `blocked`, no message, `take <cr> --note \"<the note>\"` and the take-back path below;\n   > `working`, the `[foreman→driver <cr>] note <text>` message. A hand-back that lands between\n   > that read and the message is caught by the Driver, who passes the note back: then take the\n   > CR back as for `ready`.\n\n   The last sentence states the accepted window and what the steering session does when the\n   Driver passes a note back (the take-back path); it adds no new path.\n\n2. **The `order merged` bullet, \"Parallel\" and \"Series\" sub-bullets** (around lines 143-153).\n   Where they choose between `take <cr> --note \"rebase: …\"` for a `ready` or `blocked` CR and the\n   `note rebase:` message for a `working` one, make the state the one the `status` read just\n   before sending shows, by a short back-reference to the rule above (for instance \"the state\n   from the `status` read just before sending, as above\"). Do not restate the rule.\n\n3. **\"A CR taken back from `ready`\" bullet** (around lines 155-169). Its first step already reads\n   `status` for the Driver's agent id. Say that this may be the same read as the one before the\n   note, so the session does not run `status` twice. No other change to the path.\n\n4. **\"Your means\", the `$CHANTIER status` bullet and the `--epoch` list** (around lines 77-81 and\n   238-242): no change. A plain `status` already carries no `--epoch`; check that nothing added\n   above contradicts the list of commands that carry it.\n\n### `skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md`\n\nSection \"Steering a batch yourself\", next to the \"A dependent draft PR\" bullet (around lines\n546-547), add one bullet that makes the inheritance visible, in substance:\n\n> **A note that carries work to a Driver.** The Foreman's rule applies to you: just before you\n> send one (a `rebase:` first of all), read a plain `$CHANTIER status` and choose from the state\n> it shows now: `ready` or `blocked`, `take <cr> --note` and the take-back path; `working`, the\n> message.\n\nNo other text in this file changes; the rule itself lives only in `foreman.md`.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- `skills/chantier/references/driver.md`: the Driver's rule for a note received after its\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-203 The steering session reads `status` before a note that carries work, and takes a ready CR instead of messaging it\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/driver.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/driver.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/foreman.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/foreman.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-203-the-steering-session-reads-status-before`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-203`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-204", "title": "A Driver records each Inspector pass before the PR body cites it", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/204-record-inspector-pass", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# A Driver records each Inspector pass before the PR body cites it\n\n## The problem\n\nIn real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137) the Verifier said `NOT VERIFIED` three times for one\ncause: the PR description said the change was reviewed and approved (`Review: approved at\n<sha7>`), and nothing in the CR's record showed that pass. Each time cost a Verifier pass and a\ndescription fix, on a change that was sound.\n\n## Where it shows\n\n- `skills/chantier/references/worker.md`, Inspector: it is read-only and \"writes nothing: its\n  reply is its output\". The approval exists only in the Driver's context.\n- `skills/chantier/references/driver.md`, \"CR driver\": the only recorded approval is the note\n  after a round's commit (`r<n> approved at <head>, committed as <sha>`). The other passes leave\n  no trace: a body-only re-inspection in the same round, the inspection of a rebase that changed\n  the patch, a review step of the `Team:`, the stack driver's inspector after a move. And nothing\n  orders the note before the body is written.\n- `skills/chantier/references/pr-template.md`, Test plan: the `Review: approved at <sha7>` box\n  cites the pass.\n- `skills/chantier/references/worker.md`, Verifier: a claim of the description \"with no proof in\n  `verify.md`, the gates or the evidence is `NOT VERIFIED`\", and the Inspector's notes are not\n  proof. So the Review box has nowhere to be proven from, unless the record holds the pass.\n\n## What would fix it\n\n- driver.md: every `APPROVE` an Inspector (or a review step) returns is recorded at once with\n  `$CHANTIER note`, with what the JSON says (the verdict, its `head`) and what was reviewed (the\n  round, the body alone, a move), and the commit once there is one. The Review box of\n  `pr-body.md` is filled or updated only from such a note, with its SHA.\n- worker.md, Verifier: the Review box is judged against that note in the CR's record (`log`,\n  `handoff`): present with the same SHA, the claim holds; absent or another SHA, `NOT VERIFIED`\n  as today.\n- pr-template.md: the Review box names the SHA of a recorded pass.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- A CLI record of the Inspector's verdict (a `verdict`-like command) or a `handback` check on it.\n- The Inspector writing anything: it stays read-only.\n- What the Inspector reviews, and when a new inspection is needed after a move.\n- The agent files of both hosts.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Who records the pass: the Driver, or the Inspector itself (as the Verifier records its own verdict)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The Driver, with the verdict and the `head` copied from the Inspector's JSON. The Inspector stays read-only with its reply as its only output; a second writer in the record is a larger change than the defect asks for.\n- Q: A note, or a new record in the CLI like `verdict`? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): A `note`. It is already in the CR's record and its log, no hand-back depends on the Inspector's pass, and a new CLI record would come from no measured cost.\n- Q: Which passes are recorded? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Every `APPROVE`: a round's, a body-only re-inspection, the inspection of a move (a rebase or a restack that changed the patch), and each review step of the `Team:`; a `FIX-REQUIRED` needs none, since the next round's brief carries it. The run's three failures came from passes the round's commit note does not cover.\n- Q: Which SHA does the PR body's Review box cite? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The one the note holds for the reviewed state at the PR's head: the commit of the approved round (the note has the reviewed `head` and `committed as <sha>`), or the head of the move for a later pass. The Inspector's `head` predates the round's commit, so the note must tie the two for the box to be checkable.\n- Q: What does the Verifier accept as proof of the Review box? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The Driver's note of the pass in the CR's record, with the SHA the box cites; with none, or another SHA, `NOT VERIFIED` as today. It is proof that a review happened and where, not of the code, which the Verifier still proves itself.\n- Q: In what order: the note, then the body? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): The note first, always; the Review box is filled or updated only from a recorded note, and a box whose pass is not recorded stays unchecked. That order is the whole fix.\n- Q: Does the rule bind the stack driver too? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): Yes: when it starts an `inspector` after a move and updates a PR's Test plan SHAs, it records the pass on that CR first. It edits the same box and would fail the same way.\n- Q: Should `handback ready` be refused when no Inspector pass is recorded? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): No. The CLI knows no role and does not read notes; the Verifier's judgment of the description is the check.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# A Driver records each Inspector pass before the PR body cites it\n\n## Goal\n\nA PR description must never claim a review the CR's record does not hold. In real-direct run 1\n(2026-11-12, PR 137) the Verifier said `NOT VERIFIED` three times for one cause: the PR body's\nTest plan said `Review: approved at <sha7>`, and nothing in the CR's record showed that pass. Each\ncost a Verifier pass and a description fix on a sound change.\n\nThe fix is an order, in the cards only: every `APPROVE` an Inspector or a review step returns is\nrecorded at once by the Driver with `$CHANTIER note`; the Review box of the PR body is filled or\nupdated only from such a note, with the SHA it holds; the Verifier judges the box against that\nnote. No CLI change.\n\n## What changes\n\nThree cards, all in `skills/chantier/references/`. The agent files (`agents/`,\n`com.github.copilot/agents/`) are not touched.\n\n### 1. `driver.md`, section \"CR driver\"\n\nToday the only recorded approval is the note after a round's commit (`note <cr> \"r<n> approved at\n<head from the JSON>, committed as <sha>\"`), and nothing orders a note before the body is\nwritten. Change the section so that:\n\n- **Every `APPROVE` is recorded at once**, as soon as the Driver reads the JSON, before any other\n  step (before the next review step, the commit, or any write of `pr-body.md` or the posted\n  description). This covers:\n  - the round's `inspector`;\n  - a body-only re-inspection in the same round (the `FIX-REQUIRED` whose blocking items are all\n    `pr-body.md: …`);\n  - each review step the `Team:` lists;\n  - the `inspector` after a move (a rebase whose range-diff shows the CR's patch changed, a\n    resolved conflict).\n- **What the note holds:** the verdict and the `head` copied from the Inspector's (or review\n  step's) JSON, and what was reviewed (the round, the body alone, a move and its range, or the\n  review step's name). Suggested forms, to keep the record greppable:\n  - `r<n> inspector approved at <head>`\n  - `r<n> body re-inspection approved at <head>`\n  - `r<n> <review step> approved at <head>`\n  - `move <old7>..<new7> (<kind>) inspector approved at <head>`\n- **The commit ties the pass to the commit.** The existing note after the round's commit stays,\n  `r<n> approved at <head from the JSON>, committed as <sha>`: the Inspector's `head` predates\n  the round's commit, and this note is what lets the Review box cite the commit. A move's pass\n  needs no second note: its `head` is already the moved, committed head.\n- **A `FIX-REQUIRED` needs no note:** the next round's brief carries it.\n- **Note first, then the box, always.** The Review box of `pr-body.md` (and of the posted\n  description) is filled or updated only from a recorded note, citing the SHA that note holds for\n  the reviewed state at the PR's head: the round's `committed as <sha>`, or the head of the move\n  for a later pass. A box whose pass is not recorded stays unchecked. A pass carried over a later\n  move (a clean rebase, no patch change) is written `carried from <sha7>` as the PR template\n  already says, and the `<sha7>` is the one in the note.\n\n### 2. `driver.md`, section \"Stack driver\"\n\nWhen it starts an `inspector` after a move (the CR's patch changed) and its reply is `APPROVE`, the\nstack driver records the pass on that CR (`$CHANTIER note <cr> \"move <old7>..<new7> (restack)\ninspector approved at <head>\"`) before it updates that PR's Test plan SHAs with `gh pr edit\n--body-file` / `glab mr update --description`. The Review box it writes cites only a recorded\nnote, as for the CR driver.\n\n### 3. `worker.md`, section \"Verifier\"\n\nToday: \"their notes and green CI are not proof\", and a description claim \"with no proof in\n`verify.md`, the gates or the evidence is `NOT VERIFIED`\". Add the one case of the Review box:\n\n- The Review box (`Review: approved at <sha7>`) is judged against the Driver's note of the pass in\n  the CR's record, read with `$CHANTIER log <cr>` (complete; `handoff` shows only the last ten\n  notes). A note of an approved pass whose SHA is the one the box cites (the reviewed `head`, the\n  round's `committed as <sha>`, or the move's head; for a box `carried from <sha7>`, that\n  `<sha7>`) proves the claim. No such note, or another SHA: `NOT VERIFIED`, as today.\n- That note proves that a review happened and where, not the code: every other claim is still\n  proven by the Verifier itself, and the sentence \"their notes and green CI are not proof\" stays\n  true of everything else (say so where the exception is written).\n- This applies to the description-only pass too.\n\n### 4. `pr-template.md`\n\n- The Test plan's Review item says it names the SHA of a recorded pass, e.g.\n  `- [ ] Review: approved at \\`<sha7>\\` <!-- the SHA of the pass recorded in the CR's notes; unchecked until it is -->`.\n- In \"The rules\", the paragraph on proofs and SHAs gains one line: the Review item cites the SHA\n  of a pass the Driver recorded with `$CHANTIER note`, and stays unchecked while none is recorded.\n- The template's head comment and the Inspector's audit list are unchanged (what the Inspector\n  reviews is out of scope).\n\n## Decisions carried from `02-answers.md`\n\n- The Driver records the pass, copying the verdict and `head` from the Inspector's JSON; the\n  Inspector stays read-only, its reply its only output.\n- The record is a `$CHANTIER note`, not a new CLI record.\n- Every `APPROVE` is recorded: a round's, a body-only re-inspection, a move's inspection (rebase or\n  restack that changed the patch), each review step of the `Team:`; `FIX-REQUIRED` needs none.\n- The Review box cites the SHA the note holds for the reviewed state at the PR's head: the approved\n  round's commit (the note ties the reviewed `head` to `committed as <sha>`), or the move's head.\n- The Verifier accepts the Driver's note with the SHA the box cites as proof that a review\n  happened and where; none or another SHA is `NOT VERIFIED`. It still proves the code itself.\n- Note first, always; the box is filled or updated only from a recorded note; \n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-204 A Driver records each Inspector pass before the PR body cites it\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-204-a-driver-records-each-inspector-pass-bef`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-204`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-205", "title": "The steering session reads the rules after its claim", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/205-rules-after-claim", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# The steering session reads the rules after its claim\n\n## The request\n\nFrom real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137, audit J14, finding 6): the direct-mode Chief of Staff\nran `chantier rules` at the start of its session, before `open`, and not again after it claimed\nthe batch. The steering card should say that the steering session reads the rules right after\nits claim, every batch, in direct mode too.\n\n## The problem\n\n- A Foreman reads the project's rules at the start of every batch (`foreman.md`: the brief, the\n  cards and the rule files `$CHANTIER rules` lists, read again and not from memory).\n- `chief-of-staff.md`, \"Steering a batch yourself\", says to do what the Foreman's card says, but\n  its own list of steps (\"Your epoch\") goes from the claim straight to the Drivers. A Chief of\n  Staff that read the rules at its session's start sees no reason to read them again, and its\n  session may be hours old, compacted, or on its second batch by then.\n- The audit reads the steering transcript from the claim on, so it found no `rules` and failed\n  J14. The audit is right: the rail is \"the steering session reads the rules\", per batch.\n- The real-foreman run of the same day showed the weaker form of the same gap (its finding 5): a\n  Foreman ran `rules`, which prints the list of rule files, and read none of them.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nOne line in `chief-of-staff.md`, \"Steering a batch yourself\": right after the claim, every batch,\nrun `$CHANTIER rules` and read the rule files it lists, as a Foreman does at its start. The\ncommand is the anchor the audit reads; the files are the substance.\n\nDone when: the card says it, and a direct batch's audit passes J14 with the rules read after the\nclaim.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The audit: J14 stays as it is (item `audit-direct-false-fails` does not touch it).\n- The Foreman's card, unless its wording on the same step disagrees with the new line.\n- The agent files (`agents/`, `com.github.copilot/agents/`): the contract is in the card.\n- A CLI refusal (for instance `take` refused until `rules` ran): the CLI knows no role, and\n  nothing measured asks for it.\n", "02-answers.md": "- Q: Is running the command enough, or are the rule files read again? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the command right after the claim, every batch, and the files it lists read again unless this session has read them since its last compaction. The real-foreman run showed a `rules` with no file read meets the rail in name only; a full re-read minutes after the session's start would pay tokens for nothing.\n- Q: Every batch, or only a session's first? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): every batch. A session can steer several batches and the project's rules change between them (a merged CR often edits AGENTS.md).\n- Q: Is the audit relaxed instead, to accept a `rules` run before the claim? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The request chose the card, and the claim is the one point the audit can anchor a per-batch read on.\n- Q: Does a resumed steering session (a new session that claims a later epoch of the same batch) read them too? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, \"after its claim\" covers every claim. A session that takes a batch over knows nothing of the rules the first one read.\n- Q: Does the Foreman's card change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): only if its wording on reading the rule files is weaker than the new line; the two cards must ask the same thing of a steering session. One contract for both modes, and the Foreman's card already asks for the files.\n- Q: Does the CLI enforce it? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The CLI knows no role and a new refusal needs a measured cost (AGENTS.md); one audited slip in one run is a card fix.\n- Q: Does the user see anything new? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no: one more command and at most a few file reads per batch in the steering session, cents on a batch. No output, default or habit changes.\n- Q: Which gate? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and `just scenario`. It changes a card (AGENTS.md).\n- Q: Does the skip cover a file read before this batch's `open`? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): no. The skip covers only files read since this batch's `open` and since the last compaction, so both cards ask the same thing; an earlier reading is not this batch's.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# The steering session reads the rules after its claim\n\n## Goal\n\nIn direct mode, the Chief of Staff steering a batch reads the project's rules right after it\nclaims the batch's epoch, every batch, as a Foreman does at its start: it runs `$CHANTIER rules`\nand reads the rule files that command lists. The command is the anchor the eval audit reads\n(J14); the files are the substance.\n\nSource: real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137, audit J14, finding 6 of\n`evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`): the Chief of Staff ran `$CHANTIER rules` at the\nstart of its session, before `open`, and not again after its claim, so the audit (which reads the\nsteering transcript from the claim on) failed J14. The real-foreman run of the same day\n(`evals/results/real-foreman-2026-11-12.md`, finding 5) showed the weaker form of the same gap: a\n`rules` run with no rule file read, which meets the rail in name only.\n\n## Decisions carried from 02-answers.md\n\n1. **Command and files.** Right after the claim, every batch: run `$CHANTIER rules`, and read again\n   the rule files it lists. The command itself runs every time; only the re-read of a file is\n   skipped, and only for a file this session has read both since this batch's `open` and since\n   its last compaction (a full re-read minutes after a reading done for this batch pays tokens\n   for nothing). A reading before this batch's `open` is not this batch's: it never exempts a\n   file.\n2. **Every batch**, not only a session's first: a session can steer several batches, and the\n   project's rules change between them (a merged CR often edits `AGENTS.md`).\n3. **The audit is not relaxed**: J14 keeps anchoring on the claim; a `rules` run before the claim\n   does not count.\n4. **Every claim**, a resumed steering session too: a new session that claims a later epoch of the\n   same batch (a resume, a seat hand-over in direct mode) runs the command and reads the files; it\n   knows nothing of what the first session read.\n5. **The Foreman's card changes only if its wording on reading the rule files is weaker than the\n   new line**; the two cards must ask the same thing of a steering session. Checked: it is not\n   weaker and, with the skip bounded as in decision 1, it asks the same thing (see \"Foreman card\"\n   below), so `foreman.md` does not change.\n6. **No CLI enforcement**: no new refusal (the CLI knows no role; a refusal needs a measured cost,\n   per `AGENTS.md`; one audited slip in one run is a card fix).\n7. **Nothing new for the user**: one more command and at most a few file reads per batch in the\n   steering session. No output, default or habit changes.\n8. **Gates**: `just check` and `just scenario` (a change to a card, per `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## What changes\n\n### `skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md`, section \"Steering a batch yourself\"\n\nIn the bullet **\"Your epoch.\"**, right after the claim sentence (\"Claim it as a Foreman does:\n`$CHANTIER foreman set <your session id> --epoch <n>` (`1` for a new batch), then `$CHANTIER\nforeman transcript <your transcript id> --epoch <n>`.\"), add the rules step, so that it sits in\nthe claim itself and every place that says \"claims ... as in Steering a batch yourself\" carries\nit. Suggested wording (the Driver may tune the words, not the content):\n\n> Right after each claim, every batch you steer, a resumed or taken-over one too: run\n> `$CHANTIER rules` and read the rule files it lists, as a Foreman does at its start. A reading\n> before this batch's `open` is not this batch's; skip only the re-read of a file this session\n> has read since this batch's `open` and since its last compaction. The command runs every time,\n> after the claim, whatever ran before it.\n\nThen the existing text of the bullet (\"Carry `--epoch <n>` on exactly these commands...\")\ncontinues unchanged. `rules` does not carry `--epoch` (it is not in that list, and stays out of it).\n\nNothing else in the card changes:\n\n- \"The project\" (\"At the start of each session, `$CHANTIER rules` ...\") stays: it is the session's\n  start, the new line is the batch's.\n- \"Handing the seat over\" already says the new session of a direct batch \"claims the next epoch\n  as in Steering a batch yourself\"; with the step inside \"Your epoch\", it covers decision 4 with no\n  edit there.\n\n### Foreman card (`skills/chantier/references/foreman.md`): no change\n\nIts opening paragraph already asks the same: at the start of every batch, in a reused session\ntoo, and again after a compaction, \"read the cards and the rule files `$CHANTIER rules` lists\nagain, not only the command's output. An earlier reading is not this batch's.\"; and its claim\nbullet says \"Then the brief and the rules\" right after `foreman set` / `foreman transcript`. With\nthe skip bounded to this batch and the last compaction (decision 1), the new line and the\nForeman's card ask the same thing of a steering session, so per decision 5 it stays as it is.\n\n### Agent files, CLI, audit: no change\n\n- `agents/` and `com.github.copilot/agents/`: the contract lives in the card.\n- The CLI (`skills/chantier/scripts/cli/`): no refusal, no new output.\n- The eval kit (`evals/kit/audit.py`, the J14 check `RULES_COMMAND` read from the claim on) and\n  `evals/SCORECARD.md`: unchanged.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The audit and J14 (the item `audit-direct-false-fails` does not touch J14 either).\n- The Foreman's card (see above).\n- The agent files on both hosts.\n- A CLI refusal such as `take` refused until `rules` ran.\n- The audit's blind spot for `Invoke-Expression \"$c rules\"` (real-foreman finding 5): another item.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `just check` passes (Linux, macOS and Windows in CI).\n- `just scenario` passes (both forges).\n- The diff touches only `skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md`, within the \"Your epoch.\"\n  bullet of \"Steering a batch yourself\"; quote the new sentence in the PR description.\n- A read of the two cards side by side, recorded in the CR's `work/` notes: the new line and\n  `foreman.md`'s opening paragraph and claim bullet ask the same of a\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-205 The steering session reads the rules after its claim\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [evals/SCORECARD.md](../../../../../evals/SCORECARD.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n- [evals/results/real-foreman-2026-11-12.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-foreman-2026-11-12.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/foreman.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/foreman.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-205-the-steering-session-reads-the-rules-aft`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-205`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-206", "title": "A change that adds or changes a CI job proves it once with a cold uv cache before the push", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/206-ci-job-cold-cache-check", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# A change that adds a CI job proves it once with a cold uv cache before the push\n\n## The request\n\nBatch-14's retro: CR-191 added the CI job `just eval-dry-run`. Its long gate passed on the\nDriver's machine, and the job was red on `ubuntu-latest`: the dry run calls `uv lock --offline`\n(`evals/kit/dry_run.py`), which reads the registry from uv's cache, and the runner's cache was\nempty. One round was spent on it (the fix is commit `27c1539`: a \"warm the uv cache\" step in\n`.github/workflows/ci.yml`). The request asks for two things: an eval-kit change that adds a CI\njob runs its recipe once locally with an empty uv cache (`UV_CACHE_DIR` set to a fresh folder)\nbefore the push, and the Spec-writer names the CI environment when a specification adds a job.\n\n## The problem\n\n- A developer's machine and a CI runner differ in state, not only in system: the local uv cache\n  is warm after months of work, the runner's is empty at each run. A recipe that depends on that\n  state passes every local gate and fails at its first CI run.\n- Nothing asks for the difference to be looked at. `evals/README.md`, \"Changing the kit\", asks\n  for `just eval-dry-run` at the pushed head, which runs with the warm cache. A specification\n  that adds a job says what the job runs, not where: the Spec-writer card\n  (`skills/chantier/references/spec-writer.md`) has no line about a CI job.\n- The cost is a full round and a CI cycle each time, for a fault one local run would show.\n\n## What would fix it\n\n1. A project rule of this repository, where its contributors and agents read it\n   (`CONTRIBUTING.md` and `evals/README.md`, \"Changing the kit\"): a change that adds a CI job\n   runs that job's steps once, in the workflow's order, with `UV_CACHE_DIR` set to a fresh empty\n   folder, before the push; the output is evidence.\n2. A line in the Spec-writer card, for every project: a specification that adds or changes a CI\n   job names the environment the job runs in (the system, and what is absent there: caches,\n   tools, credentials, network state) and the evidence that proves the job before the push.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- A new `just` recipe or CLI tool that empties the cache: one measured case does not justify one.\n- Emulating the runner (a container, `act`): only the cache is cold.\n- Differences of system (Linux on CI, Windows locally): CI stays the proof for those; the\n  specification names them so the Driver knows what a local run cannot prove.\n- Changing how `dry_run.py` or the CI job works today: the warm step of `27c1539` stays.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Which changes owe the cold-cache run: eval-kit changes that add a CI job only, or any change to this repository that adds one? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): any change that adds a CI job, or changes the steps of one. The fault is about a job's first run on a runner, not about the eval kit, and such changes are rare, so the wider rule costs almost nothing.\n- Q: Does an ordinary change (no CI job touched) owe it too? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. A cold run downloads everything again; paid on every change it would cost more than the one round it saves.\n- Q: What exactly is run with the cold cache? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the job's own steps, in the workflow's order, in one fresh empty `UV_CACHE_DIR` (so a warm-up step such as the one before `just eval-dry-run` is run too, and proved). Running the recipe alone would fail by design where the job has a warm-up step, and would not prove the job.\n- Q: Is the cold run's output kept as evidence? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, it is the Verifier's evidence and the PR body quotes it, as \"Changing the kit\" already asks for the dry run. A rule with no trace is not checked by the Inspector.\n- Q: Where does the cold-cache rule live: in a card or in this project's own rules? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): in this project's rules (`CONTRIBUTING.md`, daily work, and `evals/README.md`, \"Changing the kit\"), not in a card. `uv` and its cache are this repository's tools; a card serves every project and must not name them.\n- Q: What does the Spec-writer card gain? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): one generic line: a specification that adds or changes a CI job names the environment the job runs in (the system, and what is absent there: caches, tools, credentials) and the evidence that proves it before the push. It applies to any project and names no tool; the project's rules say how.\n- Q: Does the Spec-writer decide the environment when the answers do not give it? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it reads it from the project's CI files, read-only, as it reads the code; what it cannot find goes in its list of open questions. The card already forbids it to invent a decision.\n- Q: Does the rule cover differences other than the cache (system, installed tools)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, the local proof is the cold cache only; the specification names the other differences so the Driver knows CI is their only proof. Emulating a runner is a tool no measurement asks for.\n- Q: Does this CR add a recipe (`just ci-cold …`) or a structure test? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, text only. AGENTS.md wants new tools from measured cost, and one case is not a measure.\n- Q: Which gates does this CR pass? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and, for the card's line, `just scenario`. AGENTS.md says so for a change to the cards; the docs part alone would need none.\n\n## Open for the user\n\nNone.\n- Q: Which of a job's steps does the local cold run repeat? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): the `run:` steps that do the job's work, the warm-up step included, in the workflow's order; skip the actions (checkout, setup-uv, setup-just) and the runner-setup steps such as the global git identity, which must never touch the developer's own config. CONTRIBUTING.md says so.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# A change that adds or changes a CI job proves it once with a cold uv cache before the push\n\n## Goal\n\nBatch-14's retro: CR-191 added the CI job `eval-dry-run` (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`). Its long\ngate passed on the Driver's machine, and the job was red at its first run on `ubuntu-latest`:\n`evals/kit/dry_run.py` calls `uv lock --offline`, which reads the registry from uv's cache, and the\nrunner's cache was empty. A full round and a CI cycle were spent on a fault one local run would\nhave shown (the fix is commit `27c1539`, the step \"Warm the uv cache with the sandbox's registry\npackages\" before `just eval-dry-run`).\n\nA developer's machine and a CI runner differ in state, not only in system: the local uv cache is\nwarm, the runner's is empty at each run. This CR makes that difference looked at, in two places:\n\n1. **This repository's rules** (not a card): a change that adds a CI job, or changes the steps of\n   one, runs that job's work steps once locally, in the workflow's order, with a fresh empty uv\n   cache, before the push; the output is the Verifier's evidence, quoted in the PR body.\n2. **The Spec-writer card**, for every project, naming no tool: a specification that adds or\n   changes a CI job names the environment the job runs in and the evidence that proves the job\n   before the push.\n\nText only: no recipe, no CLI change, no test.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. `CONTRIBUTING.md`, section \"Daily work\"\n\nIn the bullet list after the `just mod` / `tsc` paragraphs, next to the bullet that ends\n\"A change to the eval kit (`evals/kit/`) runs `just eval-dry-run`, as\n[Changing the kit](evals/README.md#changing-the-kit) says.\", add one bullet (or extend that one;\nthe Driver chooses, in the style of the surrounding text) that says:\n\n- A change that adds a CI job to `.github/workflows/`, or changes the steps of one, runs that\n  job's steps once on the local machine before the push, in the workflow's order, with\n  `UV_CACHE_DIR` set to a new empty folder (the same folder for every step of that run; outside\n  the repository). A runner's uv cache is empty at each run and the local one is warm: a step that\n  reads the cache passes every local gate and fails at its first CI run.\n- The steps repeated are the job's `run:` steps that do the job's work, a warm-up step included\n  (such as the one before `just eval-dry-run`), which is so proved. The actions\n  (`actions/checkout`, `astral-sh/setup-uv`, `extractions/setup-just`) and the steps that only set\n  up the runner's machine are not repeated: above all the git identity step, whose\n  `git config --global` must never touch the developer's own configuration.\n- The output is the Verifier's evidence (`work/verifier/evidence/`), quoted in the PR body.\n- A change that touches no CI job's steps does not owe it: a cold run downloads everything again.\n- The cold cache is the only local proof. Other differences with the runner (its system, the tools\n  installed there) are proved by CI alone; the specification names them.\n\n### 2. `evals/README.md`, section \"Changing the kit\"\n\nAdd one bullet to the list, after the first one (\"A change to `evals/kit/` passes\n`just eval-dry-run` at its pushed head…\"), that states the same rule in short and points to\n`CONTRIBUTING.md` (\"Daily work\") for the full text: a kit change that adds a CI job or changes the\nsteps of one also runs that job's work steps once with `UV_CACHE_DIR` set to a new empty folder,\nbefore the push, its output kept as the Verifier's evidence and quoted in the PR body. The\nexisting bullets are unchanged.\n\n### 3. The Spec-writer card, `skills/chantier/references/spec-writer.md`\n\nGeneric text, for any project: it names no tool (no `uv`, no `just`, no GitHub Actions).\n\n- **Input**, after \"Read the code the CR touches, read-only, to name real files, functions and\n  commands.\": when the CR adds or changes a CI job, read the project's CI files too, read-only, to\n  name the environment the job runs in. What the CI files and the answers do not give goes in the\n  list of open questions (the card's last paragraph already says a point left open is not the\n  Spec-writer's to decide).\n- **Output**, a new bullet in the list \"A rich specification holds:\", before \"The evidence that\n  proves it\": when the CR adds or changes a CI job, the environment that job runs in (its system,\n  and what is absent there that the local machine has: caches, tools, credentials, network\n  state), the evidence that proves the job before the push as the project's rules ask for it, and\n  which of those differences only the job's first CI run can prove.\n\nThe agent files `agents/spec-writer.md` and `com.github.copilot/agents/chantier-spec-writer.agent.md`\nare not changed: the contract lives in the card.\n\n## Decisions carried from `02-answers.md`\n\n| Answer | Where it lands |\n| --- | --- |\n| Any change that adds a CI job, or changes the steps of one, owes the cold-cache run (not eval-kit changes only) | §1, §2 |\n| An ordinary change (no CI job touched) does not owe it | §1 |\n| What runs: the job's own steps, in the workflow's order, in one fresh empty `UV_CACHE_DIR`; a warm-up step is run too, and proved | §1, §2 |\n| Which steps: the `run:` steps that do the job's work, warm-up included, in order; the actions and the runner-setup steps (the global git identity above all) are skipped | §1 |\n| The output is the Verifier's evidence and the PR body quotes it | §1, §2 |\n| The rule lives in this project's rules (`CONTRIBUTING.md` \"Daily work\", `evals/README.md` \"Changing the kit\"), not in a card | §1, §2 |\n| The Spec-writer card gains one generic line (environment: system, what is absent; the evidence before the push), naming no tool | §3, Output |\n| The Spec-writer reads the environment from the CI files, read-only; what it cannot find is an open question | §3, Input |\n| The local proof is the cold cache only; other differences are named so the Driver knows CI is their only proof | §1, §3 |\n| No recipe (`just ci-cold …`), no structure test: text only | Out of \n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-206 A change that adds or changes a CI job proves it once with a cold uv cache before the push\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [agents/spec-writer.md](../../../../../agents/spec-writer.md)\n- [com.github.copilot/agents/chantier-spec-writer.agent.md](../../../../../com.github.copilot/agents/chantier-spec-writer.agent.md)\n- [evals/README.md](../../../../../evals/README.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/spec-writer.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/spec-writer.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-206-a-change-that-adds-or-changes-a-ci-job-p`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-206`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-207", "title": "A text sent to the forge is written with the Write tool and posted from its file, never from a shell string", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/207-pr-body-from-file-only", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# A PR body is written with the Write tool and posted from its file, never from a shell string\n\n## The request\n\nBatch-14's retro: a Driver built a PR body inside a PowerShell string; an escape sequence put a\nform feed into the body, and two description passes failed before the body was posted again from\na file. The request: the cards say the PR body is always written with the Write tool and posted\nwith `--body-file`, never built in a PowerShell string.\n\n## The problem\n\n- The cards already show the right way, but only as an example. `driver.md` (Push) gives\n  `gh pr create --draft --body-file <work folder>/pr-body.md` and `gh pr edit --body-file`, and\n  `concepts.md` rule 9 says a PR body is UTF-8 without a BOM. No line says \"never another way\",\n  so a Driver in a hurry types the text in the command.\n- A shell string is not a safe carrier for Markdown: PowerShell reads the backtick as its escape\n  character (`` `f `` is a form feed, `` `n `` a newline, `` `t `` a tab), and Markdown is full of\n  backticks. Quotes and `$` break the same way, and Bash has its own cases.\n- For GitLab the card gives `glab mr create --draft` with `--description`, which takes the text\n  as an argument: the card leaves that host with the unsafe form only.\n- The cost: two failed description passes of the Inspector and a re-post, on a fault that is\n  not about the change at all.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nThe cards state the rule as a rule: a text sent to the forge (a PR or MR body first) is a file\nwritten with the Write tool in the work folder, and the forge command takes it from that file;\nits content is never typed, built or escaped in a shell command. `concepts.md` rule 9 holds the\nrule for everyone, `driver.md` (Push, and the places that edit the description) says \"always\",\nand the GitLab form is given so that the text still comes from the file.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- A CLI command that posts the body, or a CLI refusal: the CLI runs no forge command.\n- A new check of control characters in the Inspector's description pass: the pass caught the\n  fault as it is.\n- Commit messages and one-line texts (a PR title): no fault measured.\n- The content and template of the PR body (`pr-template.md`).\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Does the rule cover the PR body only, or every multi-line text an agent sends to the forge? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): every multi-line text sent to the forge: a PR or MR body, a PR or issue comment, release notes. The fault is the shell string, not the PR; `chief-of-staff.md` already posts an issue comment with `--body-file` and the release already uses `--notes-file`, so the rule only names what the cards do.\n- Q: Are one-line texts (a PR title, a short `--note`) covered? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, a single line with no Markdown stays in the command. No fault was measured there, and a file per title would cost calls for nothing.\n- Q: Are commit messages covered? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, out of scope. No fault was measured, and the project's commit messages are one line.\n- Q: Is the rule about PowerShell only, or about any shell? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): any shell; PowerShell's backtick is given as the example that happened. Bash breaks Markdown too (quotes, `$`, backticks as command substitution), and a card that names one shell invites the other.\n- Q: Which tool writes the file? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the host's Write tool (or Edit for a change), never a shell redirection or here-string. A shell write brings back the same escapes, and on Windows PowerShell 5.1 a BOM as well.\n- Q: What about GitLab, where the card gives `--description` with the text as an argument? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the same rule holds: the text is authored in the file with the Write tool and the command only passes the file's content on, never text written in the command. How `glab` takes it is the Driver's to settle and prove, and the card marks the form unproved until a trial, as the host references do.\n- Q: Where does the rule live? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): once in `concepts.md` rule 9, next to the no-BOM sentence, since every role reads it; `driver.md` turns its examples into \"always from the file\" and points there. One statement, one meaning; the Chief of Staff's and the Release driver's posts are then covered without a line of their own.\n- Q: Does the CLI or a test enforce it? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, a card rule only. The CLI runs no forge command and knows no role, and the structure tests do not judge behaviour.\n- Q: Does the Inspector's description pass gain a check for control characters? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The pass already failed the broken body twice; the fix belongs at the source.\n- Q: Which gates does this CR pass? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and `just scenario`, as a change to the cards. AGENTS.md says so.\n\n## Open for the user\n\nNone.\n- Q: chief-of-staff.md's example `glab issue note <id> --message ...` passes the text as an argument; replace it? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): yes, replace that one example with a form that reads the file, marked unproved like the other glab forms; still no line of its own for the Chief of Staff.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# A text sent to the forge is written with the Write tool and posted from its file, never from a shell string\n\n## Goal\n\nIn batch-14 a Driver built a PR body inside a PowerShell string. An escape sequence put a form\nfeed into the body, and two description passes failed before the body was posted again from a\nfile. The cards already show the file form (`--body-file`), but only as an example, and for\nGitLab they give only the unsafe form (`--description` with the text as an argument).\n\nAfter this CR the cards state it as a rule that every role reads. A multi-line text sent to the\nforge is authored in a file with the host's Write tool, and the forge command takes it from that\nfile. Its text is never typed, built or escaped in a shell command, in any shell.\n\nThe CR has no tracker issue: no `issue:` line.\n\n## Decisions carried from `02-answers.md`\n\n1. **Scope of texts:** every multi-line text an agent sends to the forge. That covers a PR or MR\n   body, a PR, MR or issue comment, and release notes, not only the PR body. The fault is the\n   shell string, not the PR.\n2. **One-line texts are not covered.** A single line with no Markdown, such as a PR title or a\n   short `--note`, stays in the command.\n3. **Commit messages are not covered** (out of scope).\n4. **Any shell**, not PowerShell only. PowerShell's backtick is given as the example that\n   happened. Bash is named too: quotes, `$`, and backticks as command substitution.\n5. **The writing tool:** the host's Write tool, or Edit to change an existing file. Never a shell\n   write: no redirection, here-string, `Out-File`, `Set-Content` or `echo > file`. A shell write\n   brings back the same escapes, and on Windows PowerShell 5.1 a BOM as well.\n6. **GitLab:** the same rule holds. The text is authored in the file with the Write tool, and the\n   command only passes the file's content on, never text written in the command. Which `glab`\n   form does that is for the Driver to settle and prove. The card marks the form **unproved**\n   until a trial, as the host references do.\n7. **Where the rule lives:** once, in `concepts.md` rule 9, next to the no-BOM sentence.\n   `driver.md` turns its examples into \"always from the file\" and points to rule 9. The Chief of\n   Staff's and the Release driver's posts are covered by rule 9 without a line of their own.\n8. **No enforcement by the CLI or a test.** This is a card rule only. The CLI runs no forge\n   command and knows no role, and the structure tests do not judge behaviour.\n9. **No new Inspector check** for control characters in the description pass. The fix belongs at\n   the source.\n10. **Gates:** `just check` and `just scenario`, because this is a change to the cards.\n11. **The Chief of Staff's `glab` example:** the one example in `chief-of-staff.md`,\n    `glab issue note <id> --message …`, is replaced with a form that reads the file. It is marked\n    unproved like the other `glab` forms. The Chief of Staff still gets no line of its own.\n\n## What changes\n\nOnly three cards change: `skills/chantier/references/concepts.md`,\n`skills/chantier/references/driver.md` and, for one example,\n`skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md`. Nothing changes in the CLI\n(`skills/chantier/scripts/`), the agent files (`agents/`, `com.github.copilot/agents/`), the\nhooks, the types, the scenario or the tests. The wording below is a target: the Driver may\ntighten it, keeping every element named.\n\n### 1. `concepts.md`, Rules, rule 9 (\"Commands and files\")\n\nRule 9 now ends with: \"Every file you write, a PR body included, is UTF-8 without a byte-order\nmark (on Windows PowerShell 5.1, `Out-File` and `Set-Content -Encoding utf8` add one).\"\n\nDirectly after that sentence, add the rule. It must contain each of these elements:\n\n- **What it covers:** a multi-line text sent to the forge. The examples are a PR or MR body, a\n  PR, MR or issue comment, and release notes.\n- **Where the text lives:** a file written with the host's Write tool (Edit to change it). The\n  file goes in the work folder, or where the card or the project's procedure keeps it (for\n  example `work/issue-comment.md` or a release's draft notes).\n- **How it reaches the forge:** the forge command takes the text from that file (`--body-file`,\n  `--notes-file`, or the forge CLI's equivalent).\n- **What is forbidden:** the text is never typed, built or escaped in a shell command, in any\n  shell. The file is never written by the shell (a redirection, a here-string, `Out-File`,\n  `Set-Content`).\n- **Why:** a shell string mangles Markdown. In PowerShell the backtick is the escape character\n  (`` `f `` is a form feed: the batch-14 fault). In Bash, `$` and backticks expand, and quotes\n  break.\n- **What it does not cover:** a one-line text with no Markdown (a PR title, a `--note`) and a\n  commit message.\n\nSuggested wording:\n\n> A multi-line text sent to the forge (a PR or MR body, a PR, MR or issue comment, release notes)\n> is a file written with the host's Write tool (Edit to change it), in the work folder or where\n> your card or the project's procedure keeps it, and the forge command takes it from that file\n> (`--body-file`, `--notes-file`, or the forge CLI's equivalent). Its text is never typed, built\n> or escaped in a shell command, in any shell, and the file is never written by the shell (a\n> redirection, a here-string, `Out-File`, `Set-Content`): PowerShell reads a backtick as an escape\n> (`` `f `` is a form feed), Bash expands `$` and backticks, and Markdown is full of both. A\n> one-line text with no Markdown (a PR title, a `--note`) and a commit message are not covered.\n\nConstraint from `tests/test_structure.py`\n(`test_each_host_has_its_reference_and_the_cards_hold_no_host_tool`): `concepts.md`,\n`driver.md` and `chief-of-staff.md` must not contain any name in `HOST_TOOLS` (`SendMessage`,\n`send_message`, `write_agent`, `TaskStop`, `ScheduleWakeup`, `start_session`, `create_session`,\n`get_session`, `/every`, `/loop`, `/compact`, `/autocompact`, `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`,\n`COPILOT_AGE\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-207 A text sent to the forge is written with the Write tool and posted from its file, never from a shell string\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/concepts.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/driver.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/driver.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-207-a-text-sent-to-the-forge-is-written-with`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-207`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-208", "title": "Change Specification: Claude Desktop's Auto-fix events are left to the CR's Driver; the heartbeat's prompt names no interval", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/208-autofix-event-vs-driver", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Claude Desktop's Auto-fix events are left to the CR's Driver; the heartbeat's prompt names no interval\n\n## The request\n\nThree findings, all about `skills/chantier/references/hosts/claude-code.md`:\n\n1. Batch-14's retro: Claude Desktop's Auto-fix event on a CR's PR asked the steering session to\n   fix the PR and to merge the base branch in, while the CR's own Driver was fixing it. The\n   request: the host reference says an Auto-fix event on a CR with a live Driver is left to that\n   Driver, and the project's rebase rule ranks above Auto-fix's merge rule.\n2. Real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137; `evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`): the\n   app attached the five sandbox PRs the Drivers opened to the steering session, so Auto-fix\n   watched them and asked to merge `main` into PRs #2 and #5 while their Drivers were rebasing.\n   The host reference could also say how the user turns Auto-fix off for an eval.\n3. Same run: the heartbeat's prompt ended in \"every 30m\", which the `/loop` skill reads as a\n   fixed cron interval, while the host card wants each wake scheduled with `ScheduleWakeup`.\n   The heartbeat's prompt should not end in \"every 30m\".\n\n## The problem\n\n- Auto-fix is a feature of the app, not of chantier. The app binds a PR opened by a subagent to\n  the session that started it, and then sends that session events (\"fix the CI\", \"merge the base\n  in\"). The host reference knows the feature only as an allow rule\n  (`mcp__ccd_pr__set_monitor`, \"No chantier card uses it\"): it says nothing of an event that\n  arrives.\n- An event that is acted on breaks three rules at once: the Foreman never codes, rebases or\n  pushes (`foreman.md`); one CR has one Driver, and two writers on one branch race; the project's\n  method is a rebase (`concepts.md` rule 8, the project's own rules), and Auto-fix asks for a\n  merge commit.\n- Even ignored, each event starts a turn of the steering session, which the user pays for.\n- The heartbeat section says \"runs the `/loop` skill in dynamic mode with `chantier status\n  --refresh …`, every `30m`, each wake scheduled with `ScheduleWakeup`\". Read literally, the\n  agent puts \"every 30m\" in the loop's prompt, and `/loop` then takes its fixed-interval mode:\n  the sentence gives two contradictory orders.\n\n## What would fix it\n\n1. A short section in `hosts/claude-code.md`: what an Auto-fix event is, that the steering\n   session never acts on one, that a CR with a live Driver is that Driver's, what happens for a\n   CR with no live Driver, and that the project's rules and the cards rank above what the event\n   asks (a rebase, never the base merged in).\n2. The same section says how the user turns Auto-fix off, and `evals/README.md` asks for it\n   before a real campaign.\n3. The heartbeat section is reworded so the loop's prompt holds the command only, and the 30\n   minutes are the delay of each scheduled wake.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- chantier arming, disarming or unbinding the app's PR monitor itself: chantier never writes the\n  user's settings.\n- `hosts/copilot.md`: Copilot has no Auto-fix and its heartbeat is another mechanism.\n- How a Driver learns of its own red CI (the backlog item `driver-ci-from-pr-checks`).\n- The agent files, the CLI and the mod.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: What does the steering session do with an Auto-fix event on a CR whose Driver is live? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): nothing on the PR: no fix, no merge, no rebase, and no relay to the Driver; it ends the turn at once. The Driver reads its own CI and already holds the branch, and a relay would spend messages against the host's limit for news the Driver has.\n- Q: And when the CR has no live Driver (handed back ready, waiting for the user)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the event is only a signal: the steering session checks the forge itself and, if the PR is really red or in conflict, follows the card's usual route (the CR goes back to a Driver with a note); it never does what the event asks itself. The existing rules already cover a ready PR that turns red, and the Foreman never codes or pushes.\n- Q: Does the rule hold in direct mode, where the Chief of Staff steers? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, the same rule for whichever session steers. PR 137 was a direct run, and in both modes the fix is a Driver's.\n- Q: What about an Auto-fix event on a PR that is not a CR's (the user's own PR in the same session)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): out of chantier's rule; the reference speaks of a batch's PRs only. That PR is between the user and the app.\n- Q: Which rule wins when Auto-fix asks to merge the base branch in? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the project's rules, then the cards, always rank above what a host event asks: a CR's branch is brought up to date the project's way (here a rebase), never by the merge the event asks for. It is the order of precedence chantier already has; the reference only states that a host event has no rank in it.\n- Q: Does chantier turn Auto-fix off, or unbind the PRs, by itself? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, never; no card uses the app's PR monitor, to arm it or to disarm it. The host reference already says chantier never writes the user's settings, and a session that changes the app's behaviour is a surprise.\n- Q: How sure must the \"how to turn Auto-fix off\" text be? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it is written from a dated trial or the app's documentation and marked proved or documented; with neither, the line is marked unproved and names the trial, as every line of that reference. A wrong menu path in a card is worse than none.\n- Q: Does the eval guide ask for Auto-fix off? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, `evals/README.md` lists \"Auto-fix off for the session\" among the operator's steps before a real campaign, pointing to the host reference. In an eval the events skew the measured cost and the run's record; the operator is the user and reads that page anyway.\n- Q: Does the heartbeat's interval change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, 30 minutes stays; only the wording changes: the loop's prompt holds the `chantier status --refresh` command and no interval, and the 30 minutes are the delay of each `ScheduleWakeup`. The fault is a sentence read two ways, not the rhythm.\n- Q: Does the heartbeat fix belong in this CR? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes. Same file, same run's findings, a few words; a CR of its own would cost a Driver for one sentence.\n- Q: Do `foreman.md` and `chief-of-staff.md` change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): at most one pointer each to the host reference's new section; the rule itself lives in `hosts/claude-code.md`. The cards only point to what is proper to a host.\n- Q: Does the eval audit or the scenario gain a check (no merge of the base in a CR's branch)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, not in this CR. It is a new campaign behaviour to weigh on its own; the next real campaign proves the rule, and the new section is marked unproved until then.\n- Q: Which gates does this CR pass? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and `just scenario`, as a change to the cards. AGENTS.md says so; no `just mod`.\n\n## Open for the user\n\n- Q: During a normal batch (not an eval), does chantier ask you to turn Auto-fix off, or does it stay silent and only ignore the events? Each ignored event still starts a short turn of the steering session, which you pay for; turning Auto-fix off is a step you take in the app, and you lose it for your own PRs in that session. — Recommended: stay silent during a batch. The host reference says how to turn it off and that an event costs a turn; only the eval guide asks for it off. No preflight check and no \"For you\" line: chantier cannot see whether Auto-fix is on, and a line at every go would be noise. If the cost of the ignored turns shows in a real batch, a tip can come later, from that measure.\n\n- Q: (open) During a normal batch, does chantier ask the user to turn Auto-fix off? — User (2026-11-12, \"ok\"): no, it stays silent and only ignores the events; the host reference documents how to turn it off and the cost; only the evals guide asks for it off before a campaign; no preflight check, no For you line.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# Change Specification: Claude Desktop's Auto-fix events are left to the CR's Driver; the heartbeat's prompt names no interval\n\n## Goal\n\nClaude Desktop's Auto-fix sends the steering session events about a batch's PRs (\"fix the CI\",\n\"merge the base branch in\") while the CR's own Driver is working on that PR. The steering session\nmust never act on such an event: a CR's PR is its Driver's, and the project's rules and the cards\nrank above anything a host event asks. The user learns from the host reference how to turn\nAuto-fix off and what an ignored event costs; the eval guide asks for it off before a real\ncampaign. In the same file, the heartbeat's sentence is reworded so the `/loop` prompt holds the\ncommand only and the 30 minutes are the delay of each scheduled wake.\n\nNo tracker issue.\n\n## Background (the evidence this CR answers)\n\n- Batch-14 retro (`.agents/changes/5-archives/261112-1924-191-eval-kit-end-to-end-dry-run/retro.md`,\n  line 10): the desktop app's Auto-fix event on PR 136 asked the steering session to fix the PR\n  and merge the base branch in while the CR's own Driver was fixing it.\n- Real-direct run 1 (`evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`, finding 2, and \"Direct mode:\n  the host lines this run proved\", heartbeat bullet): the app bound the 5 sandbox PRs the Drivers\n  (subagents of the steering session) opened to the steering session's PR bar, Auto-fix was on\n  for them, and it sent merge-conflict events for PRs #2 and #5 asking for a merge commit while\n  their Drivers were rebasing. The Chief of Staff ignored them. In the same run the `/loop` skill\n  read a trailing \"every 30m\" as a fixed cron interval, while the host reference wants each wake\n  scheduled with `ScheduleWakeup` (the run armed it at 1800 s).\n\n## What changes\n\nFour files, all text. No CLI, no agent file, no hook, no type, no scenario check.\n\n### 1. `skills/chantier/references/hosts/claude-code.md`: a new section \"Auto-fix events\"\n\nPlace it right after the section \"## Heartbeat\" (before \"## Configuration\"). It says, in the\nreference's style (short sentences, each behaviour with its mark):\n\n1. **What it is.** Auto-fix is a feature of the Claude Desktop app, not of chantier. The app\n   binds a PR opened by a subagent to the session that started it (the steering session: the\n   Foreman, or the Chief of Staff in direct mode) and then sends that session events about the\n   PR: fix its failing checks, resolve a conflict by merging the base branch in. Cite where it was\n   seen: batch-14 (PR 136) and real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PRs #2 and #5 of the sandbox).\n2. **An event is not an order.** It is neither the user's word nor a line of the batch (`order`,\n   `asks`, hand-back). The project's rules, then the cards, always rank above what a host event\n   asks; a host event has no rank in that order. A CR's branch is brought up to date the\n   project's way (here a rebase, `concepts.md` rule 8 and the project's rules), never by the merge\n   of the base branch the event asks for.\n3. **A CR with a live Driver** (the CR is `working`, its Driver recorded): the steering session\n   does nothing on the PR: no fix, no merge, no rebase, no push, and no relay of the event to the\n   Driver. It ends the turn at once. Why, in one line: the Driver reads its own CI and holds the\n   branch, two writers on one branch race, and a relay spends messages against the host's limit\n   for news the Driver already has.\n4. **A CR with no live Driver** (handed back `ready` or `blocked`, waiting for the user): the event\n   is only a signal. The steering session checks the forge itself (`gh pr checks`, `gh pr view\n   --json mergeable`, or `glab` on GitLab). If the PR is really red or in conflict, it follows the\n   card's usual route: the CR goes back to a Driver with `take <cr> --note \"<why>\"`\n   (`foreman.md`, \"Your means\": a red check, or the ready PR that stopped merging with\n   `--note \"rebase: …\"`). It never does what the event asks itself. If the forge shows nothing\n   wrong, nothing is done.\n5. **Both steerings.** The same rule holds for whichever session steers: the Foreman, or the Chief\n   of Staff in direct mode.\n6. **A PR that is not a batch's** (the user's own PR in the same session): out of chantier's rule;\n   it is between the user and the app. The section speaks of a batch's PRs only.\n7. **chantier never arms, disarms or unbinds the PR monitor.** No card uses the app's PR monitor\n   (`mcp__ccd_pr__set_monitor`), to arm it or to disarm it, and chantier never writes the user's\n   settings.\n8. **The cost.** Even ignored, each event starts a turn of the steering session, which the user\n   pays for.\n9. **How the user turns Auto-fix off.** Written only from the app's documentation (with its link,\n   marked **documented**) or from a dated trial (marked **proved**, with where and when). With\n   neither, the line says the path is not known, is marked **unproved**, and names the trial that\n   settles it (for instance: in the Claude Desktop app, find the setting that turns Auto-fix off\n   for a session or a PR, turn it off, open a PR from a subagent, and see that no Auto-fix event\n   arrives; record the labels and the date here). Never a guessed menu path: a wrong path in a\n   card is worse than none. Say that turning it off also loses Auto-fix for the user's own PRs in\n   that session.\n10. **During a normal batch chantier stays silent** about Auto-fix: it does not ask the user to\n    turn it off, has no preflight check for it and gives no \"For you\" line for it (chantier cannot\n    see whether Auto-fix is on, and a line at every go would be noise). It only ignores the\n    events as above. Only the eval guide asks for Auto-fix off, before a real campaign. A tip may\n    come later, from a measure of the ignored turns' cost in a real batch.\n11. **The mark of the section.** The rule (points 2 to 5) is **unproved** until the next real\n    campaign shows it; name the trial: an Auto-fix event arrives on a CR's PR during a real eval\n    campaign, \n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-208 Change Specification: Claude Desktop's Auto-fix events are left to the CR's Driver; the heartbeat's prompt names no interval\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [.agents/changes/5-archives/261112-1924-191-eval-kit-end-to-end-dry-run/retro.md](../../../5-archives/261112-1924-191-eval-kit-end-to-end-dry-run/retro.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [evals/README.md](../../../../../evals/README.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/foreman.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/foreman.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/hosts/claude-code.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/hosts/claude-code.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/hosts/copilot.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/hosts/copilot.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-208-change-specification-claude-desktop-s-au`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-208`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-209", "title": "Audit: two checks fail on lawful behaviour", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/209-audit-direct-false-fails", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Audit: two checks fail on lawful behaviour\n\n## The request\n\nFrom real-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137, findings 4 and 5): 4 of the audit's 6 FAILs were the\naudit's own mistakes. Fix `evals/kit/audit.py` so both checks pass on what the cards allow.\n\n## The problem\n\n- **J3, the Verifier's detached worktree (3 FAILs).** `worker.md` lets one worker create one\n  worktree: the Verifier, a detached worktree of the CR's base for its \"before\" capture (`git\n  worktree add --detach <path> <base>`, removed at its end). `audit.py` checks \"creates no\n  worktree: the Driver did\" for every worker, the Verifier included, with a pattern that matches\n  any `git worktree add`. Three Verifiers of run 1 did what their card says and were failed.\n- **C9, the report refresh with no epoch (1 FAIL).** `status-layout.md` and `chief-of-staff.md`\n  require `status --refresh` with no epoch before a report; only the loop's wake carries\n  `--epoch`. In direct mode the steering transcript is the Chief of Staff's, so it holds both\n  kinds. `DIRECT_EPOCH_COMMANDS` matches every `status --refresh` and fails those with no epoch.\n- A false FAIL costs a hand check at every run and hides the real ones: the run's two true slips\n  (a Verifier's write to the host's scratchpad, the rules read before the claim) sat among four\n  wrong lines, and the scorecard marks C9 and J3 \"seen, wrong\".\n\n## What would fix it\n\n- J3: a Verifier's `git worktree add --detach` is not a breach. Any other worktree creation by a\n  worker, a QA driver or a review step still fails, and so does a Verifier's worktree that is\n  not detached.\n- C9: in a direct batch, a `status --refresh` with no epoch passes. Every other steering command\n  of the list still needs its epoch.\n\nDone when: the audit run again on run 1's workspace reports 2 FAILs (J3 scratchpad write, J14\nrules), the four others pass, and the eval kit's dry run still passes.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The two real FAILs of run 1: the Verifier's scratchpad write (the Verifier's card) and J14\n  (item `rules-after-claim`).\n- Any card or CLI change: this is the eval kit alone.\n- New checks (for instance that the Verifier removes its worktree), and the Copilot audit.\n- The run 1 report, which is not rewritten.\n", "02-answers.md": "- Q: How wide is the J3 exemption? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): a Verifier's `git worktree add --detach` only; a Verifier's worktree that is not detached, and any worktree made by another worker, a QA driver or a review step, still fail. That is exactly what `worker.md` allows (\"the one worktree a worker creates\"), and a wider exemption would hide a real breach.\n- Q: Does the audit also check the base of that worktree, or that the Verifier removes it? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The request is to stop two false FAILs; a new check is a new rail line with its own evidence, another item if a run shows the need.\n- Q: Does the C9 exemption hold in Foreman mode too? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, direct batches only. A Foreman's refreshes are its wakes, which carry the epoch (`foreman.md`); the report refresh with no epoch is the Chief of Staff's, and only in direct mode is its transcript the steering one.\n- Q: In direct mode, is a wake that forgot its epoch then unseen? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): accepted. The command alone cannot tell a wake from a report refresh, and a wake with no epoch moves no heartbeat, which the heartbeat rail (C10) already reads.\n- Q: Do the two other FAILs of run 1 (the Verifier's scratchpad write, J14) change? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, they stay FAILs. They are real slips, fixed in the cards by their own items.\n- Q: What proves the fix? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the audit run again on run 1's kept workspace gives 2 FAILs where it gave 6, and the kit's dry run passes; if the workspace is gone, the PR says so and shows the dry run alone. Run 1 is the measured case, and the dry run is the evidence the project asks for an eval-kit change.\n- Q: Is the scorecard or the run 1 report edited? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the report no; `evals/SCORECARD.md` only where its J3 or C9 wording disagrees with the cards. A report is a record of its run, and the rails' text already allows both behaviours.\n- Q: Which gate? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` only. A change to the eval kit alone needs no long gate (AGENTS.md).\n- Q: Run 1's batch is closed, so audit.py cannot re-run on its workspace; what proof then? — Chief of Staff (on the user's word of 2026-11-12, no user impact): run the new J3 and C9 logic on the commands run 1 flagged, read from the run's transcripts still on disk (C:/Users/gaeta/Projets/chantier-eval/real-direct-2026-11-12), showing the four false FAILs pass and the two real ones stay FAILs; plus the dry run. No planted case.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# Audit: two checks fail on lawful behaviour\n\n## Goal\n\nReal-direct run 1 (2026-11-12, PR 137; report `evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md` on\n`origin/main`, findings 4 and 5) failed 6 of 636 audit checks, and 4 of those were the audit's\nown mistakes. Fix `evals/kit/audit.py` so its J3 and C9 checks pass on what the cards allow, and\nstill fail on what they forbid. A false FAIL costs a hand check at every run and hides the real\nones.\n\nThis change touches the eval kit only.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. J3: a Verifier's detached worktree is lawful\n\n**The cards.** `skills/chantier/references/worker.md` (lines 9-10 and 114-116) lets one worker\ncreate one worktree: the Verifier makes a detached worktree of the CR's base for its \"before\"\ncapture (`git worktree add --detach <path> <base>`, \"the one worktree a worker creates\") and\nremoves it at its end. `driver.md` (lines 45-46) says the same. The J3 row of\n`evals/SCORECARD.md` already says \"no worker creates a worktree (except the Verifier's detached\none)\".\n\n**The bug.** In `claude_checks`, `evals/kit/audit.py` lines 493-495:\n\n```python\nif a.role in WORKERS | {\"qa-driver\"} | reviews:\n    trees = [c for c in a.bash() if GIT_WORKTREE_ADD.search(c)]\n    check(not trees, f\"{a.id[:10]} ({a.role}) creates no worktree: the Driver did\", \"J3\")\n```\n\nThis applies to every worker, the Verifier included, and `GIT_WORKTREE_ADD` (line 89) matches any\n`git worktree add`. Three Verifiers of run 1 (`a575690943`, `aa4b6f3168`, `ac57a2037e`) each ran\n`git worktree add --detach … origin/main` and failed.\n\n**The change.**\n\n- When the agent's role is `verifier`, a `git worktree add` that carries `--detach` in its own\n  arguments is not counted. Its own arguments run from the end of the `worktree add` match to the\n  next command separator (`;`, `&`, `|`, newline), the same way the pushes are split at line 477.\n  This is so that a chained command (`git worktree add --detach a x; git worktree add b y`) still\n  fails on its second, non-detached call. Both `--detach` and its short form `-d` count as\n  detached.\n- Every other case still fails J3:\n  - a Verifier's `git worktree add` without `--detach`;\n  - any `git worktree add` by another worker (investigator, coder, inspector, debugger,\n    conflict-resolver), by a QA driver, or by a review step.\n- The check keeps its rail (J3) and its line for the other roles. For a Verifier, the line may\n  say what is allowed (for instance `creates no worktree but its detached one of the base`). The\n  Driver chooses the exact words.\n- The audit does not check the base of the detached worktree, or that the Verifier removes it.\n\n### 2. C9: the report refresh with no epoch passes in a direct batch\n\n**The cards.** `skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md` (lines 11-12) and\n`chief-of-staff.md` (line 27, line 312, and lines 525-530 in \"Steering a batch yourself\") require\n`$CHANTIER status --refresh` *with no epoch* before every report. Only the loop's wake carries\n`--refresh --epoch <n>`. In direct mode the steering transcript is the Chief of Staff's own, so it\nholds both kinds of refresh.\n\n**The bug.** `DIRECT_EPOCH_COMMANDS` (`evals/kit/audit.py` lines 69-70) matches every\n`status --refresh`. The C9 check (lines 516-521) then fails every steering command it matches that\nlacks `--epoch`. Run 1 failed one report refresh this way.\n\n**The change.**\n\n- In a direct batch (`direct`, from `batch.json`'s `steering: direct`), a `status --refresh` with\n  no `--epoch` no longer fails C9. The simplest way is to take `status --refresh` out of\n  `DIRECT_EPOCH_COMMANDS`, but the Driver chooses the means.\n- In a direct batch, every other command of the list still needs its epoch: `take`, `cancel`,\n  `pause`, `agents set` and `order done`.\n- A Foreman batch is unchanged: `EPOCH_COMMANDS` keeps `status --refresh`, because a Foreman's\n  refreshes are its wakes, and those carry the epoch (`foreman.md` lines 77 and 208).\n- The C9 line printed in direct mode stops naming the refresh among the commands that carry the\n  epoch. Today it reads \"every steering refresh, take, agents set, order done, cancel and pause\n  carries its epoch\". The Foreman line is unchanged.\n- **Accepted consequence:** in direct mode, a wake that forgot its epoch is no longer caught by\n  C9. The command alone cannot tell a wake from a report refresh. A wake with no epoch moves no\n  heartbeat, and the heartbeat rail (C10) already reads that.\n- Keep the comment at line 70 accurate. If the module docstring or a comment names the refresh as\n  an epoch command for direct mode, correct it there too.\n\n### 3. `evals/SCORECARD.md`, only where it disagrees with the cards\n\n- **J3 (line 49):** it already says \"except the Verifier's detached one\". Leave it unchanged.\n- **C9 (line 35):** its rail lists `status --refresh` among the commands that carry the epoch,\n  and its \"audit\" column says \"every steering command of the steering transcript carries\n  `--epoch`\". That disagrees with the cards' report refresh with no epoch. Narrow it to the\n  loop's wake, for instance \"`status --refresh` on a wake\". Say in the audit column that in a\n  direct batch a `status --refresh` with no epoch is the report refresh and passes. Change nothing\n  else in the row.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- The two real FAILs of run 1 stay FAILs: the Verifier `ab3ea4ac68`'s write to the host's\n  scratchpad (J3, \"edits nothing but its notes and proof\") and J14 (rules read before the claim,\n  item `rules-after-claim`). Their own items fix them in the cards.\n- Any card change (`skills/`), any CLI change, `agents/`, `com.github.copilot/agents/`, `hooks/`\n  and `types/`.\n- New checks: the Verifier's worktree base, its removal, or anything else.\n- A planted case in the kit's dry run for either check.\n- The Copilot audit.\n- The run 1 report (`evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md`), which stays as written. A\n  report is a record of its run.\n- A C9 exemption in Foreman mode.\n\n## Evidence\n\n1. **`just check`** passes. This is the gate: the eva\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-209 Audit: two checks fail on lawful behaviour\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [evals/SCORECARD.md](../../../../../evals/SCORECARD.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/worker.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/worker.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-209-audit-two-checks-fail-on-lawful-behaviou`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-209`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-210", "title": "The steering session fast-forwards a clean main checkout after each merge it records", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/210-main-checkout-fast-forward", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# The steering session fast-forwards a clean main checkout after each merge it records\n\n## The request\n\nBatch-14's retro: the price of `claude-fable-5-2` merged (commit `3dc912f`), but the main\ncheckout stayed at an older commit, so `chantier cost` went on leaving that model's calls\nunpriced. The request: the steering session fast-forwards a clean main checkout after each merge\nit records, never touching untracked files, and not during an eval, when the main checkout must\nnot change.\n\n## The problem\n\n- In this repository the plugin runs in place: the cards and the CLI are read live from the main\n  checkout (AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING.md \"Set up\"). A merge on the forge changes nothing there\n  until someone pulls, so a batch that fixes chantier keeps running the unfixed chantier.\n- In any project, the main checkout falls behind the default branch as the batch merges: the\n  user's files show old code, and whatever reads the project from there (its `AGENTS.md` through\n  `chantier rules`, the user's own tools) reads the past.\n- Nobody owns the step. The cards give the steering session one git duty in the main checkout,\n  the cleanup of merged CRs before `pause` (`foreman.md`: \"Never code, review, commit, rebase,\n  push…\"), and `concepts.md` rule 8 says the user's uncommitted changes are never touched. The\n  CLI runs no git command that changes anything.\n\n## What would fix it\n\nAfter the steering session records a merge (`order merged`, the Foreman's or, in direct mode,\nthe Chief of Staff's), it brings the main checkout to the default branch's new head, by a\nfast-forward only and only when that is safe: the checkout is on the default branch, has no\nchange to a tracked file, and git can move it without touching an untracked file. In any other\ncase it does nothing and says why in one line. A project's rule can forbid it; this repository's\nrule already does during an eval.\n\n## What it touches\n\n- `foreman.md` (the list of what the Foreman never does, and its git duties) and\n  `chief-of-staff.md` (direct mode, the reports, the table of what needs the main checkout).\n- `concepts.md` if \"the main checkout\" needs the new duty named; `status-layout.md` if the\n  report gains a line.\n- `scenario/` (the batch's flow changes: `just scenario` must show it) and the eval kit's audit\n  where it reads the main checkout.\n- Not the CLI: the agent runs git.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Anything but a fast-forward: no reset, rebase, stash, checkout of another branch, or clean.\n- Reloading the plugin or the session's cards after the fast-forward: a card loads at the next\n  session or with `/reload-plugins`, as today.\n- A planning surface's own checkout, which the cards already keep in step with its remote.\n- A new CLI command for it.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Who does the fast-forward? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the session that records the merge: the Foreman in Foreman mode, the Chief of Staff in direct mode; never a Driver or a worker. The request says \"the steering session\", and it is the only one that sits on the main checkout and knows the merge is recorded.\n- Q: Does the CLI do it (inside `order merged`)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, the agent runs git; the CLI may at most say what is off, as it does today. The CLI runs no git command that changes anything, by AGENTS.md.\n- Q: What counts as a \"clean\" main checkout? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no change, staged or not, to any tracked file; untracked files do not count and are never moved, read or deleted. The request says so, and this very checkout always has untracked files (the planning folder, the user's notes).\n- Q: What if the incoming commits add a file that exists untracked in the checkout? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): git refuses the fast-forward, and the session leaves it refused and says so; it never forces, moves or deletes the file. \"Never touching untracked files\" has no exception.\n- Q: What if the main checkout is not on the default branch? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): nothing is done: no checkout of another branch, and no update of the local default branch behind the user's back; one line says it. The user chose that branch, and here a switched branch changes the running plugin.\n- Q: What if the local default branch has diverged from the remote (local commits)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the fast-forward is impossible, so nothing is done and one line says it; never a reset, a rebase or a merge commit. Only a fast-forward is without loss.\n- Q: What does a skipped or refused fast-forward cost the batch? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): nothing: the batch goes on, the session says once why (dirty, other branch, diverged, refused by git or by the host) and tries again at the next merge. It is a convenience, never a gate; no workaround, by the no-fallback rule.\n- Q: What about a steering session that cannot write the main checkout (a worktree or remote session)? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it does not try another way; it says so at once, as the fail-fast rule asks. The host reference already says such a session is not fit for the main checkout.\n- Q: How does the session know an eval is running? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): it does not guess: the card says \"unless the project's rules forbid it\", and this repository's AGENTS.md already forbids any change to the main checkout during an eval. A user's project rules rank above the cards, and the cards must not know this repository's evals.\n- Q: Inside an eval's workspace, does the batch under test fast-forward the sandbox's main checkout? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, like any project's batch: it is the behaviour under test, and the eval kit's audit and the dry run are brought in line in the same CR. AGENTS.md's eval rule protects chantier's own checkout, not the sandbox's.\n- Q: With an external planning surface, which checkout is meant? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): the code repository's clone, under the same conditions; the surface's own checkout keeps its existing rule. The stale code is in the code clone, and the surface is already kept in step by `chief-of-staff.md`.\n- Q: Does the session reload the plugin or read its cards again after a fast-forward? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no. The CLI is read live, which is what the request needs (the price table); the cards load at the next session or with `/reload-plugins`, as CONTRIBUTING.md says.\n- Q: Which gates does this CR pass? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check`, `just scenario` (the flow changes, and the scenario shows a fast-forward done and one refused) and `just eval-dry-run` if the audit changes. AGENTS.md and \"Changing the kit\" say so.\n\n## Open for the user\n\n- Q: Is the fast-forward on by default in every project chantier runs in, or only where the project asks for it? It moves the files of your own working copy while you may have them open; today chantier changes nothing there but the planning folder and the merged CRs' worktrees and branches. — Recommended: on by default, under the strict conditions above (default branch, no tracked change, fast-forward only), with one line in the next report (\"main checkout: fast-forwarded to `<sha>`\", or \"not fast-forwarded: `<why>`\"); a project turns it off with a rule in its `AGENTS.md`. It is what your request describes, a fast-forward of a clean tree loses nothing, and a stale checkout is the more common surprise. The other choice is off by default and on by a key in `chantier.yaml`: safer, but then chantier's own repository needs the key and most users never learn of it.\n- Q: When does it happen: after each merge recorded, or once, at the batch's pause? After each merge, in this repository, the CLI of a running batch changes under it (it is read live): good for a price or a fix, a risk when a merged CR changes the record's format mid-batch. — Recommended: after each merge, as your request says. The stale CLI is the measured fault; a CR that changes the record's format is rare and already has to handle a batch in flight. At the pause only would be safer and would not have fixed the batch-14 case.\n\n- Q: (open 1) O\n\n…", "03-change-spec.md": "# The steering session fast-forwards a clean main checkout after each merge it records\n\n## Goal\n\nIn the user's terms (batch-14's retro): the price of `claude-fable-5-2` merged (commit `3dc912f`),\nbut the main checkout stayed at an older commit, so `chantier cost` went on leaving that model's\ncalls unpriced. In this repository the plugin runs in place: the CLI is read live from the main\ncheckout, so a batch that fixes chantier keeps running the unfixed chantier until someone pulls.\nIn any project, the main checkout falls behind the default branch as the batch merges.\n\nAfter each merge it records, the steering session brings the main checkout to the default\nbranch's new head, by a fast-forward only, and only when that is safe. In any other case it does\nnothing and says why in one line. It is on by default in every project; a project turns it off by\na rule in its `AGENTS.md`. It is a convenience, never a gate.\n\n## Decisions carried from `02-answers.md`\n\nEach one is binding; the sections below implement them.\n\n1. **Who:** the session that records the merge, the steering session: the Foreman in Foreman\n   mode, the Chief of Staff in direct mode. Never a Driver or a worker. In Foreman mode the Chief\n   of Staff does not do it (it is the Foreman's work).\n2. **Not the CLI:** the agent runs git. The CLI runs no git command that changes anything\n   (AGENTS.md); `order merged` is unchanged.\n3. **Clean:** no change, staged or not, to any tracked file. Untracked files do not count and are\n   never moved, read or deleted.\n4. **An untracked file in the way** (the incoming commits add a path that exists untracked in the\n   checkout): the fast-forward is refused and left refused, said in one line; the file is never\n   forced, moved or deleted. \"Never touching untracked files\" has no exception, ignored files\n   included (see \"The procedure\", step 5: git overwrites an ignored file by default, so the\n   command carries `--no-overwrite-ignore`).\n5. **Not on the default branch** (another branch, or a detached HEAD): nothing is done, no\n   checkout of another branch, no update of the local default branch behind the user's back; one\n   line says it.\n6. **Diverged** (the local default branch has commits the remote does not): nothing is done, one\n   line says it; never a reset, a rebase or a merge commit.\n7. **Cost of a skip:** nothing. The batch goes on; the session says once, for that merge, why\n   (dirty, other branch, diverged, refused by git or by the host) and tries again at the next\n   merge it records. Never a gate, never an ask, no workaround (no-fallback rule).\n8. **A session that cannot write the main checkout:** it tries no other way and says so at once\n   (the fail-fast rule). The existing checks already keep such a session from steering (the\n   Foreman's worktree check, preflight P7 in direct mode); what remains is a refusal by the host,\n   which is case 7 with the host's exact text.\n9. **An eval:** the session does not guess. The card says \"unless the project's rules forbid\n   it\"; this repository's `AGENTS.md` already forbids any change to the main checkout during an\n   eval. The cards name no eval of this repository.\n10. **Inside an eval's workspace:** the batch under test fast-forwards the sandbox workspace's\n    main checkout like any project's batch; it is behaviour under test. The eval kit's audit and\n    the dry run are brought in line in this CR.\n11. **External planning surface:** the checkout meant is the project's code clone (the one `take`\n    names, `git -C <clone>`), under the same conditions. The surface's own checkout keeps its\n    existing rule (`chief-of-staff.md`, \"With a planning surface\").\n12. **No reload:** after a fast-forward the session neither reloads the plugin nor reads its cards\n    again. The CLI is read live, which is what the request needs; the cards load at the next\n    session or with `/reload-plugins`, as CONTRIBUTING.md says.\n13. **Gates:** `just check`, `just scenario` (it shows a fast-forward done and one refused) and\n    `just eval-dry-run` (the audit changes).\n14. **On by default** (the user, 2026-11-12): in every project, under the strict conditions\n    (default branch, no tracked change, fast-forward only, untracked files never touched), with one\n    line in the next report: done to `<sha>`, or not done and why. A project turns it off by a rule\n    in its `AGENTS.md`.\n15. **When** (the user, 2026-11-12): after each recorded merge, not once at the pause.\n16. **The promise \"chantier writes only in the planning folder\"** (the user, 2026-11-12, \"ok\"):\n    chantier's own `AGENTS.md` and `docs/use-cases.md` line 63 gain the clause \"…, and\n    fast-forwards a clean main checkout after a merge it records\", in this CR.\n\n## The procedure (the card's text, in substance)\n\n**When.** Right after the steering session handles a recorded merge:\n\n- Foreman mode: when it handles the Chief of Staff's `order merged <cr>` line (the user's merge),\n  before it marks that order `order done`; and right after its own `order merged <cr> --epoch <n>`\n  (a release's PR or a granted fix it merged).\n- Direct mode: right after the Chief of Staff's own `order merged <cr> --epoch <n>`.\n- Several merges handled in one turn (a stack merged at once, several \"I merged\" at once) get one\n  attempt after the last of them; it covers them all.\n- The order of the existing steps after a merge (rebase notes, `take`, the cleanup before `pause`)\n  is unchanged; the fast-forward is independent of them.\n\n**The checkout.** In-project: the main checkout, where the steering session runs. With a planning\nsurface: the project's clone, by `git -C <clone>` (the clone `take` names); never the surface's\ncheckout. Below, `git` means `git -C <checkout>` where needed. `<remote>` and `<default>` are the\nproject's, as its rules and the batch name them (the `--default-branch` given to `open`).\n\n**The steps,** stopping at the first that says no; each \"no\" is one line, the `<why>` below:\n\n1. The project's rules (`$CHANTIER r\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-210 The steering session fast-forwards a clean main checkout after each merge it records\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [AGENTS.md](../../../../../AGENTS.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [docs/planning-modes.md](../../../../../docs/planning-modes.md)\n- [docs/use-cases.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases.md)\n- [evals/README.md](../../../../../evals/README.md)\n- [evals/SCORECARD.md](../../../../../evals/SCORECARD.md)\n- [evals/real-foreman/RUNBOOK.md](../../../../../evals/real-foreman/RUNBOOK.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/chief-of-staff.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/concepts.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/concepts.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/foreman.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/foreman.md)\n- [skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md](../../../../../skills/chantier/references/status-layout.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-210-the-steering-session-fast-forwards-a-cle`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-210`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-211", "title": "Change Specification: parallel-rebase-cost", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": [], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/211-parallel-rebase-cost", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Parallel mode: pay less for each merge\n\n## The request\n\nIn a parallel batch each merge sends the other open CRs back to rebase, run the long gate and CI\nagain and verify again. Batch-11 paid this several times (its retro). Measure it, and see whether\nready CRs can be merged in an order that limits rebases. Also seen in real-direct run 1\n(2026-11-12, PR 137, finding 9): CR-101 delete-item paid three rebases, each with a\nconflict-resolver ($3.32 of $9.22). The plan could chain CRs that all touch the same files\n(there, the README's Routes table and the item template).\n\n## The problem\n\n- The rule today (`foreman.md`, \"Parallel\"; `chief-of-staff.md`; `driver.md`): after each merge,\n  every CR with a PR still open goes back to a Driver; \"conflict or not, its checks run again at\n  the new head\": rebase, push, long gate, CI, Verifier. With n PRs open, n merges cost about\n  n²/2 such passes.\n- What the records already say:\n  - Batch-11 retro: CR-181, CR-176 and CR-182 went back after a merge; two of the three patches\n    were identical or clean after the rebase. For CR-184 and CR-185 the rebase was clean or\n    context-only, and \"the gate re-run, not the rebase, was the cost (about 13 minutes of the long\n    gate each time)\". Its own note: a PR the forge reports mergeable needs no rebase note.\n  - Batch-14 retro: one gate lock queued four long gates behind one another; the re-gates after\n    each merge fed that queue.\n  - Real-direct run 1: five CRs that all append to the same README table. Every rebase of the\n    slow CR conflicted; three conflict-resolvers, and one resolution added a byte-order mark that\n    cost a round.\n- So there are two different costs: (a) checks run again on a rebase that changed nothing in the\n  CR's patch (the common case, paid in gate minutes and Verifier calls); (b) real conflicts on a\n  hot spot that every CR of the batch edits (paid in conflict-resolvers and rounds).\n- The order of merges does not help much by itself: among PRs that overlap, each merge still\n  moves the others, whatever the order. What helps is not sending back a PR the merge did not\n  touch (a), and not starting side by side CRs known to edit the same lines (b).\n\n## What would fix it\n\n1. **Measure first, from what is recorded.** A table in the CR's PR, from batch-11's and run 1's\n   `events.jsonl` and `cost --by cr`: per merge, the CRs sent back, whether the rebase changed\n   the patch, and what the pass cost (gate minutes, agents started, dollars). No new CLI command.\n2. **A ready PR the merge did not touch stays ready** (open for the user, below): the steering\n   session reads the forge before it sends a rebase note.\n3. **The plan proposes a chain for a known hot spot** (open for the user, below): CRs whose\n   specifications edit the same lines, such as one shared table. The cards already allow it\n   (\"you may propose a queue with no real dependency … the plan gives the reason\", and the\n   Foreman's chain); they also say \"touching the same file is no reason to wait\". The change is\n   to name the hot-spot case, not to chain on a shared file.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- A merge-order advice line in the reports: no measured gain (above).\n- Series and stack batches, and the stack driver's restack.\n- A new CLI tool or a refusal in `rails.py`: the judgement is the steering session's, the forge\n  read is the agents'.\n- The batch-wide gate lock (item `stop-own-gate`), the race of a rebase note with a ready\n  hand-back (item `rebase-note-check-status`), byte-order marks (item `check-rejects-bom`).\n- A project whose rules ask for an up-to-date branch before a merge: its rules rank above, and\n  every PR is rebased as today.\n", "02-answers.md": "- Q: How is the cost measured? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): from the records of batch-11 and real-direct run 1 (`events.jsonl`, `cost --by cr`, the retros), as a table in the CR's PR; no new CLI command and no new eval. The data exists, and a CLI tool comes only from a measured need (AGENTS.md).\n- Q: Does the CR give a merge order to follow, as the request asks to \"see\"? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no; the PR says why in one paragraph. Among PRs that overlap every merge moves the others whatever the order, and the user merges when and what they want; the saving is in the PRs a merge did not touch.\n- Q: Which modes? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): parallel only. Series and stack already rebase only the next CR or restack once.\n- Q: Does a `working` CR rebase at each rebase note? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): no, once, before its next push, however many notes came; the card already says so and the CR only makes it unmistakable if the measure shows Drivers rebasing per note. A working CR pays its checks at its push anyway.\n- Q: Does a `blocked` CR with an open PR follow the same rule as a ready one? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): yes, whatever the user decides below applies to both. It waits for something else and gains nothing from a rebase it will redo.\n- Q: A project that requires an up-to-date branch before a merge? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): its rules rank above the card: every open PR is rebased after each merge, as today. The project's rules always win.\n- Q: Where does the judgement live? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): in the cards (`foreman.md`, `chief-of-staff.md`, `driver.md` where they disagree), read from the forge by the steering session; nothing in the CLI. The CLI knows no role and no forge.\n- Q: Which gate? — Advisor (Fable, answering in the user's place on the user's word of 2026-11-12): `just check` and `just scenario`. It changes cards, and the scenario plays a parallel batch.\n\n## Open for the user\n\n- Q: After a merge in a parallel batch, does a ready PR that the merge did not touch stay ready, with no rebase and no second long gate, CI and Verifier pass? Today every open PR goes back, conflict or not. — Recommended: yes, when the forge reports the PR mergeable and the merged PR changed none of the files the CR changes; a PR that conflicts, or shares a file with the merged one, goes back as today. Why: batch-11 measured clean rebases whose only cost was 13 minutes of long gate each, and run 1 a quadratic number of passes; the file test keeps the checks where two changes can really interact. What the user gives up: a ready PR is then proved on a base that is one or more merges old, so two PRs green apart can still break `main` together, seen only by CI on `main`.\n- Q: Does the batch's plan propose to chain CRs whose specifications edit the same lines (one shared table, one template block), so they run one after the other instead of side by side? — Recommended: yes, as a proposal in the plan with its reason, approved by the go and refusable per CR; never for a shared file alone, never without the user's go, and the batch stays parallel by default. Why: in run 1 the CR that kept working while four others merged into the same README table cost $3.32 of $9.22 through three conflict resolutions. What the user gives up: those CRs take longer on the clock and a slot may stay free, against the standing preference to use every slot (2026-11-06); the cards allow such a chain already, the change is that the Chief of Staff looks for the case.\n\n- Q: (open 1) Does a ready PR untouched by a merge stay ready with no rebase? — User (2026-11-12): no. A ready PR is rebased after each merge, in stack and in parallel mode alike, so it always merges without a merge commit; in series mode the question does not arise (a CR starts only once the other is ready). The advisor's recommendation is declined.\n- Q: (open 2) Does the plan propose to chain CRs whose specs edit the same lines? — User (2026-11-12, \"oui je suis d'accord si c'est bien explique, ca cree des dependances entre CR dans le batch pour eviter des rebase qui seraient de toute facon arriver\"): yes, provided the plan explains it well: an intra-batch dependency between the two CRs, with its reason (a rebase that would happen anyway), approved at the go and refusable per CR; never for a merely shared file.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# Change Specification: parallel-rebase-cost\n\nSource: `intake/request.md` (batch-11 retro; real-direct run 1 of 2026-11-12, finding 9),\n`01-intent.md`, `02-answers.md` (2026-11-12: answers 1-8 by the Fable advisor in the user's place,\non the user's word, taken as the user's; the two \"Open for the user\" questions answered by the user\nat the end of the file). Cards are relative to `skills/chantier/references/`; line numbers as on\n`main` at 8c003ae.\n\n## Goal\n\nIn a parallel batch, each merge sends every other open PR back to a Driver: rebase, push, long\ngate, CI and Verifier again. With n PRs open, n merges cost about n²/2 such passes. The user wants\ntwo things:\n\n1. **The cost measured**, from what batch-11 and real-direct run 1 already recorded, so the\n   project knows what the rebase passes cost and where.\n2. **Rebases that would happen anyway avoided at the plan**: when two CRs' specifications edit the\n   same lines (one shared table, one template block), the plan proposes that the second CR wait\n   for the first, as an intra-batch dependency explained in the plan, so it starts on the first\n   one's work instead of conflicting with it at each merge. In run 1, CR-101 delete-item kept\n   working while four others merged into the same README Routes table and the same item\n   template: three rebases, each with a conflict-resolver, $3.32 of the batch's $9.22, and one\n   resolution added a byte-order mark that cost a round.\n\nWhat does **not** change, on the user's word (2026-11-12, open question 1): every ready PR is still\nrebased after each merge, in parallel and stack mode alike, so it always merges without a merge\ncommit. The advisor's \"a ready PR the merge did not touch stays ready\" is declined and is not part\nof this CR.\n\n## What changes\n\n### 1. The measure, in the PR (no code, no new command)\n\nThe Driver builds one table and puts it in the PR description, under its own heading (for\ninstance \"Cost of the rebase passes\"), from the records alone. No new CLI command, no new eval\n(answer 1).\n\nRows: one per merge in a parallel batch, for batch-11 and for real-direct run 1. Columns:\n\n| Column | What it holds |\n| --- | --- |\n| Merge | the CR merged (number and slug) and when |\n| Sent back | each CR sent back by that merge, and its state then (`ready`, `blocked`, `working`) |\n| Patch changed by the rebase | no (clean, or context only) / yes (a resolved conflict), from the CR's notes or a range-diff |\n| Pass cost | long gate minutes, agents started (Verifier, Inspector, conflict-resolver, Coder), dollars |\n\nThen a total per batch: passes, passes whose patch did not change, gate minutes, agents, dollars.\n\nSources, read-only:\n\n- **Batch-11:** the archived CRs of batch-11 in this repository's planning folder,\n  `.agents/changes/5-archives/` (`261111-*-174-…` to `261111-2309-185-driver-liveness` and the\n  others numbered 174 to 185): each `STATUS.json`'s `steps` and `notes` (the rebase notes, the\n  long-gate passes with their heads and times, the verdicts), their `work/gates/*.log` where kept,\n  and the batch's retro, kept in `5-archives/261111-2309-185-driver-liveness/retro.md` (the\n  \"Parallel mode made every merge cost a rebase pass\" and \"about 13 minutes of the long gate each\n  time\" lines). The batch's `events.jsonl` is no longer in the planning folder (the spec-writer\n  found no `events.jsonl` left under `.agents/changes/`): use it only if the Driver finds it.\n- **Real-direct run 1:** the report, `evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md` on `main`\n  (\"What happened\", finding 9, \"Numbers\", `cost --by cr` at the audit), and, if the Driver can\n  reach it, the run's workspace and its archive (`5-archives/261112-2003-101-delete-item/`, with\n  the batch's `retro.md`).\n- The batch-14 retro (`5-archives/261112-1924-191-eval-kit-end-to-end-dry-run/retro.md`) may be\n  quoted for the gate queue the re-gates fed; it is not a row source.\n\nA figure the records do not give is written \"not recorded\", never estimated (rule 2 of\n`concepts.md`). Dollars per pass are given only where a record gives them (for instance a CR whose\nonly extra spend was the pass); otherwise the CR's total from `cost --by cr` or its archived\n`STATUS.json` `cost_usd` is given as such, with the number of passes it includes.\n\nThe PR description also carries, in one paragraph, why the CR gives **no merge order** to follow\n(answer 2): among PRs that overlap, each merge moves the others whatever the order, and the user\nmerges when and what they want; the saving is in not starting side by side the CRs that edit the\nsame lines.\n\n### 2. A working CR rebases once per push (only if the measure shows the need)\n\n`driver.md`, the paragraph at lines 213-226, already says: \"A `note rebase: …` received while\nworking: rebase before the next push\". Answer 4: a `working` CR rebases once, before its next push,\nhowever many notes came; the CR makes this unmistakable only if the measure shows a Driver rebasing\nonce per note.\n\n- If the table shows a working CR rebased more than once between two of its pushes because several\n  `rebase:` notes came, add to that sentence of `driver.md`: \"however many `rebase:` notes came\n  since your last push, one rebase, onto the latest default branch\", and to the matching words in\n  `foreman.md`, \"Parallel\" bullet (lines 143-148): \"a `working` one gets … `note rebase: <merged cr>\n  merged`; it rebases once, before its next push\".\n- Otherwise `driver.md` is not changed, and the PR description says so in one line with the rows\n  that show it.\n\n### 3. The plan proposes a dependency for CRs that edit the same lines\n\nThe rule to name (open question 2, the user's yes, \"if it is well explained\"): when the\nspecifications of two CRs selected for a parallel batch edit **the same lines** of a file (one\nshared table, one template block, one list both append to), the plan proposes that one waits for\nthe other: an intra-batch dependency, because the rebase it saves would happen anyway. It is a\nproposal with its reason, approved by the us\n\n…", "STATUS.md": "# CR-211 Change Specification: parallel-rebase-cost\n\n🟠 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waiting for a free slot.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [README.md](../../../../../README.md)\n- [concepts.md](../../../../../concepts.md)\n- [docs/use-cases-direct.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases-direct.md)\n- [docs/use-cases-foreman.md](../../../../../docs/use-cases-foreman.md)\n- [evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md](../../../../../evals/results/real-direct-2026-11-12-run1.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-211-change-specification-parallel-rebase-cos`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-211`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- None.\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}, {"cr": "CR-212", "title": "Version PR for chantier v0.9.0, at the top of the batch's stack", "state": "queued", "round": 0, "cost": null, "pr": null, "waits": ["CR-196 working", "CR-197 working", "CR-198 working", "CR-199 working", "CR-200 queued", "CR-201 queued", "CR-202 queued", "CR-203 queued", "CR-204 queued", "CR-205 queued", "CR-206 queued", "CR-207 queued", "CR-208 queued", "CR-209 queued", "CR-210 queued", "CR-211 queued"], "blocked": null, "folder": "4-batch/1-pending/212-release-v0-9-0", "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Intent\n\nRelease chantier v0.9.0 (minor, from v0.8.0) at the top of this batch's stack, so the user finds\nin the morning a complete stack ready to merge: every P1 and P2 CR of the batch, then the version\nPR with its release notes on top. The user's merge of the whole stack, bottom-up, is the go for the\nrest of the project's release procedure (CONTRIBUTING.md \"Release\", steps 4 to 6).\n\nThis batch runs the shape the CR `release-cr-in-batch` makes normal, before that CR's code is on\n`main`: today's CLI still refuses a kind-`release` CR before the others merge, so the version PR is\nan ordinary CR of the batch that waits for every other CR, and the steps after the merge are run by\nthe steering session from the project's procedure.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n- Q: Which version? — Chief of Staff, on the user's words of 2026-11-12 (\"fait toutes les P1 et P2 en terminant sur la release\"; the release notes and version are settled by the agent from the project's state when the grill did not): v0.9.0, minor: new features since v0.8.0, nothing breaking a user's setup.\n- Q: Where does the release PR sit? — User (2026-11-12, \"tu livreras une stack prete a mergee avec le PR de release en dernier\", then B): at the top of the batch's stack, after every other CR, started once they are all ready.\n- Q: Which work do the notes cover? — Chief of Staff, on the user's words (release notes \"en fonction de l'etat du projet en y incluant les modifications de la stack + les instructions du projet cible\"): everything since v0.8.0 on `main`, plus every CR of the stack below the version PR, as docs/release-process.md says (every `feat:` and `fix:` commit exactly once).\n- Q: Any confirmation before the version PR starts? — User (2026-11-12): none; the batch runs autonomously and the release PR on top stays fixable before the merge.\n- Q: What is the go for tag and publication? — User (2026-11-12): the user's merge of the whole stack, release PR included. The steering session then follows CONTRIBUTING.md \"Release\" steps 4 and 5 (`just tag`, then the forge release with the prepared notes); step 6 (the thread) stays the user's.\n- Q: CONTRIBUTING.md says a release starts \"from an up-to-date `main`\" and asks for the eval campaigns before a release. — Chief of Staff: the user's word for this batch covers starting from the stack's top instead; `release-cr-in-batch` makes it the documented rule. Only real-direct ran before this release (real-foreman passed with findings earlier on 2026-11-12); `full` and the two quick campaigns did not: said to the user before the batch opens.\n- Q: Visuals? — Chief of Staff: captures from this batch's real run where docs/release-process.md asks for them; the docs diagrams of the stack's CRs (batch-pace.svg among them) may be shown; nothing invented.\n- Q: Release v0.9.0 without the `full` campaign and the two quick campaigns CONTRIBUTING.md asks for? — User (2026-11-12, \"oui\"): accepted. The release notes' \"For contributors\" section says it: only real-direct (and real-foreman, with findings) ran before this release.\n", "03-change-spec.md": "# Version PR for chantier v0.9.0, at the top of the batch's stack\n\n## Goal\n\nOne PR, the top of this batch's stack, that makes v0.9.0 ready to tag: the release folder\n`docs/release-notes/<date>-v0.9.0/` and the commit `release: v0.9.0` made by `just release minor`.\nIts base is the stack's top CR; it covers everything since v0.8.0 on `main` plus every CR of the\nstack below it. The user merges the whole stack bottom-up; that merge is the go for\nCONTRIBUTING.md \"Release\" steps 4 and 5, run by the steering session after the merge (not by this\nCR's Driver).\n\n## Shape in this batch (before `release-cr-in-batch` is on `main`)\n\n- An ordinary CR of the batch (not `order add --release`): today's CLI refuses a kind-`release`\n  CR before the others merge (`rails.py:112`, `203-206`) and bases it on `main`\n  (`commands.py:1464-1547`).\n- It waits for every other CR of the batch (`order after` with all their ids, in stack order) and\n  starts when they are all ready; it is stacked last, so its PR is the top of the stack, a draft\n  until its base is `main`.\n- If a CR below changes after this PR is open (a new round, not a pure restack), the Driver redoes\n  steps 1 to 3 below at the new top: the notes must still cite every `feat:` and `fix:` once.\n\n## What the Driver does\n\n1. Read CONTRIBUTING.md \"Release\" and docs/release-process.md (the rules), then the commits since\n   `v0.8.0` on `main` and every CR below in the stack (each `03-change-spec.md`, PR description\n   and evidence).\n2. Write `docs/release-notes/<today>-v0.9.0/` as docs/release-process.md says: `release-notes.md`\n   (header, links at `v0.9.0`, two to four highlights, Smaller changes, folded All fixes and For\n   contributors; every `feat:` and `fix:` commit since v0.8.0 and of the stack exactly once; N\n   counted on the stack's top before the version commit), `tweet.md` (three posts), and the images\n   it cites. Images come from real runs or existing docs (the board screenshots of\n   `boot-mode-by-surface`, the docs diagrams of the stack's CRs); none is invented. A highlight\n   with no real capture uses a CLI call and its output instead.\n3. Run `just release minor` (bumps 0.8.0 to 0.9.0, runs `just check`, commits `release: v0.9.0`;\n   pushes nothing), then push and open the PR; its body shows `release-notes.md`, then `tweet.md`,\n   each after a `---` rule, as docs/release-process.md says.\n4. Hand back ready with the PR. The Driver never runs `just tag`, `gh release create` or anything\n   after the merge.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Tag, forge release and the thread (CONTRIBUTING.md \"Release\" steps 4 to 6): after the user's\n  merge, the steering session runs steps 4 and 5; step 6 is the user's.\n- Any code or card change: the version PR changes only the version files `just release` touches\n  and the release folder.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `just check` passes at the PR's head (it runs `scripts/release_notes.py --check`).\n- `git log --oneline -1` at the head is `release: v0.9.0`; `just version` files say 0.9.0.\n- A list in the PR body (or `work/`) mapping each `feat:` and `fix:` commit since v0.8.0 and of\n  the stack to its place in the notes.\n- No `just scenario`: the PR changes no CLI, card or scenario.\n", "STATUS.md": "# CR-212 Version PR for chantier v0.9.0, at the top of the batch's stack\n\n🟠 queued, waits for CR-196 working, CR-197 working, CR-198 working, CR-199 working, CR-200 queued, CR-201 queued, CR-202 queued, CR-203 queued, CR-204 queued, CR-205 queued, CR-206 queued, CR-207 queued, CR-208 queued, CR-209 queued, CR-210 queued, CR-211 queued · [batch-15](../../STATUS.md)\n\n**Now:** Waits for CR-196 working, CR-197 working, CR-198 working, CR-199 working, CR-200 queued, CR-201 queued, CR-202 queued, CR-203 queued, CR-204 queued, CR-205 queued, CR-206 queued, CR-207 queued, CR-208 queued, CR-209 queued, CR-210 queued, CR-211 queued.\n**Next:** A Driver starts it.\n\n## Progress\n\n- [ ] Shipped\n- [ ] Handed back\n\n## Documents\n\n- [request](intake/request.md)\n- [intent](01-intent.md)\n- [answers](02-answers.md)\n- [specification](03-change-spec.md)\n- [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../../../../CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [docs/release-process.md](../../../../../docs/release-process.md)\n\n## Where\n\n- Branch: `chantier/batch-15/cr-212-version-pr-for-chantier-v0-9-0-at-the-to`\n- Worktree: `C:\\Users\\gaeta\\Projets\\chantier.worktrees\\batch-15-CR-212`\n- Driver: `-`\n\n## Decisions and open questions\n\n- chain: the version PR v0.9.0 waits for every other CR of the batch (user's word, option B); it starts once they are all ready, after the stack driver has stacked them, so its base is the stack's top\n\n## Cost\n\nNo figure yet.\n"}}]}, "backlog": [{"priority": "P3", "item": "audit-force-upstream-exemption", "title": "audit.py's -u/--set-upstream exemption accepts 'git push -f -u origin main': check forced first (found by CR-191's Inspector, batch-14, pre-existing).", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "docs-sphinx-rtd", "title": "Rewrite the documentation as a Sphinx site on Read the Docs", "spec": false, "docs": {"02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n## Grill of 2026-11-10 (batch-09 paused; the user: \"Grillons tous les P1 et P2\")\n\n1. **The theme.**\n   - The user: \"Même chose que la doc de okf-schema je crois que c'est pydata.\"\n   - The user wants what okf-schema's docs do.\n   - Checked by the Chief of Staff in `gsemet/okf-schema`'s `docs/source/conf.py`: `html_theme = \"furo\"`. So the theme is **Furo**, as in okf-schema, not pydata.\n   - The Chief of Staff tells the user.\n2. **Making \"no AI slop\" checkable.**\n   - The user chose \"Guide + revue + exemples (Recommandé)\".\n   - A short style guide with good and bad examples.\n   - An agent reviews each page against that guide.\n   - The pages are laid out in Diátaxis sections.\n3. **What becomes of `docs/` and the README.**\n   - The Chief of Staff told the user that okf-schema uses Furo.\n   - The user: \"Sphin prend la place de docs en portant tout en diataxis mais le readme garde les présentation avec les images . Certains parti peuvent être déplacée dans la doc. Attention les images devront aussi apparaître dans la doc\".\n   - The Sphinx site replaces `docs/`, and everything is carried over into Diátaxis sections.\n   - The README keeps its presentation with the images. Some of its parts may move into the docs.\n   - The images also appear in the docs.\n5. **Set aside.**\n   - After the full restatement, the user: \"Met de cote on traitera plus tard la doc\".\n   - The CR is not called grilled enough. It moves to P3, with its answers kept.\n4. **The visual identity.** Not shared with the landing page (see `landing-page`): the docs keep Furo, in okf-schema's style.\n"}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "landing-page", "title": "A landing page on GitHub Pages that shows what chantier brings at a glance", "spec": false, "docs": {"02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n## Grill of 2026-11-10 (batch-09 paused; the user: \"Grillons tous les P1 et P2\")\n\n1. **A visual identity shared with the Sphinx site.** The user chose \"Non, chacun la sienne\": the landing page has its own style, and the docs keep Furo.\n2. **What to build it with.**\n   - The user: \"Met la landing page de cote pour l'instant\".\n   - The CR is set aside: the Chief of Staff moves it to P3.\n   - The grill stops here.\n3. **How the tour runs.** The user chose \"Au défilement (Recommandé)\": each step animates as the page scrolls, at the reader's pace, and on mobile.\n"}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "planning-file-types-test", "title": "Planning-folder file types checked by a test (batch-11 retro). CR-184 Inspector caught a plain-text number file in the planning folder, where chantier writes only JSON and Markdown. A structure test or the long gate should catch a non-JSON, non-Markdown file the CLI writes there.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "planning-server", "title": "Intent: a planning server owned by the Chief of Staff", "spec": false, "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Intent: a planning server owned by the Chief of Staff\n\n2026-11-10, the user, after a study of Beads and similar tools (in a Chief of Staff side session).\nA follow-up of `planning-repo` (P1, the external planning surface), to take up after it; it does\nnot replace it. Related: `remote-board` (inbox, a read-only progress board) and\n`planning-surface-positioning` (P1).\n\n## What the user wants\n\n- **An external planning surface for real use.** The user's words: putting the plan in the\n  project hits its limits very fast; an external planning surface is needed, even if heavier.\n- **One writer: the Chief of Staff.** In chantier the only writer is the Chief of Staff, Grillers\n  included. It can therefore be the guarantor of a server that does the writes, the push and the\n  rebase of the surface, and serves the ticket tracking.\n- **One Chief of Staff per project**, several projects in one surface repository\n  (`projects/<name>/`, as in `planning-repo`).\n- **The CLI does the persistence**, the sync of the surface included. The agents still do git and\n  the forge on the project's code.\n- **Multi-session** (Chief of Staff, Grillers, the Foreman and its Drivers), on several hosts.\n- **Generic:** a GitHub PR or a GitLab MR; Claude Code, Codex, GitLab Duo, Copilot.\n- **Configuration:** the \"no setup\" rule holds for the target project only. The external surface\n  may take a YAML/JSON configuration. Coding rules stay in each project's `AGENTS.md`.\n- **Minor outside contributions without chantier:** someone must be able to contribute to a\n  project without chantier. It would be good if the contribution still ended as a report in the\n  archives.\n\n## The study (2026-11-10), for the grill\n\n- **Beads** (`gastownhall/beads`, MIT; v1.0 in April 2026, 1.3.x by Sept. 2026):\n  - A Go binary, `bd`, on a Dolt database in `.beads/`. Embedded mode has one writer; several\n    writers need a `dolt sql-server`.\n  - `issues.jsonl` is only an export. The sync is `bd dolt push/pull` to `refs/dolt/data` on the\n    git remote.\n  - Fields: description, design, acceptance, notes, `--spec-id`, metadata, external-ref. Priority\n    0-4. Statuses open/in_progress/blocked/deferred/closed. Dependencies blocks/parent-child/related.\n  - `bd ready`; `--claim`; `heartbeat`/`reclaim` leases.\n  - Gates `human`, `gh:pr`, `gh:run`, `timer`; `merge-slot`.\n  - Contributor routing to a separate planning repo; `bd repo sync`; `bd github sync`;\n    `beads-mcp`.\n- **Mapping to chantier:** CR = bead; intent/spec/answers = description/design+acceptance/comments;\n  P1-P4 = priority; `depends.json` = `blocks`; `take`'s rule = `bd ready`; slot = claim; ask =\n  human gate; merged/dropped = closed.\n  - Nothing in Beads holds chantier's budget, rounds, cost, Foreman epochs, STATUS or stack: that\n    frame stays chantier's.\n- **Main blocker, the user's word: upstream churn.**\n  - The storage changed (JSONL, then Dolt) and the repository moved (steveyegge, then\n    gastownhall).\n  - v1.2.2 was a recovery release that re-shipped v1.1.2, with a data-downgrade risk.\n  - Yegge abandoned Gas Town and reusable harnesses (August 2026, \"The Shape of Things to Come\").\n    He runs a private harness, Wheelhouse, but keeps Beads as his work ledger, on a shared Dolt\n    server.\n  - His thesis: share the primitives (task graph, worker protocol); keep roles, lifecycle and\n    \"done\" in the project.\n- **Leaning:**\n  - Do not depend on Beads as the store.\n  - Keep the storage behind the CLI, so a backend (the git surface, a server, Beads someday) can\n    be swapped.\n  - Borrow its model where it pays.\n- Others seen: Backlog.md (one Markdown file per task, MCP); tbd (a dedicated sync branch);\n  Gas City (Gas Town as an SDK); SupSpec, DevFleet, `stacked-mr`, `gh-stack` (spec DAG to PRs,\n  stacks).\n\n## Points for the grill\n\n1. **The writer is a CLI process; the Chief of Staff is its guarantor.** The Chief of Staff starts\n   and owns a `chantier serve`, standard library only. It applies the writes, commits, rebases and\n   pushes the surface, and exposes the tracking over HTTP/MCP. `seat.json` names who guarantees it\n   and where it is reached. The Chief of Staff's LLM stays on the path of decisions (orders), not\n   of writes: a hand-back or a Griller's answer must not wait for its turn.\n2. **`$CHANTIER` as a thin client** once a surface is configured: the same commands, the write\n   sent to the server.\n   - With the server down, the write is refused, with no fallback.\n   - Today Grillers, the Foreman and the Drivers write through the CLI themselves\n     (`griller.md`: `file`, `triage`, `01-intent.md`, `02-answers.md`).\n   - With no surface configured, the in-project mode is unchanged.\n3. **Lifetime:** a chat session is not a daemon. What happens when the Chief of Staff's session\n   ends or compacts, and who restarts the server.\n4. **Reach:** one machine (localhost) first; then a team (several machines, authentication), where\n   the guarantor is a role, not a laptop.\n5. **Several Chiefs of Staff push one surface repository** (one per project): commits touch\n   separate `projects/<name>/` sub-trees, so a rebase is trivial. To check against\n   `planning-repo`'s obsidian-git autosync.\n6. **What is committed:** the append-only `events.jsonl` and the Markdown people write. The\n   STATUS files are derived: regenerated after a pull, never merged.\n7. **Leases** (`heartbeat`/`reclaim`), worth taking (the user): replace the \"session file written\n   in the last 5 minutes\" heuristic, for Drivers and for the server itself.\n8. **Gates**, lower priority: a CR records what it waits for, typed by the fact, never by the forge\n   (a human answer, a merged review request, a green pipeline). Any agent on any host can then\n   ask what blocks a CR.\n9. **An archive report for a PR/MR merged outside chantier**, built from the forge (title, link,\n   author, what changed). To square with `planning-repo`, where outside CRs come through a PR on\n   the surface.\n10. **Beads as an optional backend or bridge**\n\n…", "02-answers.md": "# Answers\n\n## 2026-11-10, the user, during the Beads study\n\n> la regle 'aucune configuration' est pour le projet cible, mais en mode externe on pourrait\n> accepter une configuration yaml/json. Par contre les regles de codage doivent rester dans le\n> projets AGENTS.md. l'idee c'est que des contributions mineurs externes doivent pouvoir etre\n> faite sans chantier, meme si ce serait bien qu'elles se terminent par un rapport dans les\n> archives.\n\n- \"No setup\" is for the target project. The external surface may take a YAML/JSON configuration.\n  Coding rules stay in the project's `AGENTS.md`.\n- Minor outside contributions can be made without chantier; ideally they end as a report in the\n  archives.\n\nOn the risks of adopting Beads as the store:\n\n- Installing a binary is not a problem.\n- It would be multi-session.\n- The CLI would do the persistence and the sync.\n- **The upstream churn is the main blocker.**\n\nOn what to borrow:\n\n- **Hash IDs:** rejected: \"ce serait pas trié et j'ai déjà un slug\".\n- **Heartbeat/reclaim:** \"ça vaudrait le coup\".\n- **Gates:** they must stay generic: a GitLab MR as well as a GitHub PR; Codex, GitLab Duo or\n  Copilot as well as Claude Code.\n- **The external planning surface:** \"le point le plus important : je touche très vite les\n  limites de mettre le plan dans mon projet. Il faut une surface de planification externe, mais\n  c'est plus lourd.\"\n\n> pour moi dans chantier le seul écrivain c'est le chief of staff, même avec des grillers. il\n> peut donc être le garant du serveur (qui fait les écritures, push/rebase et sert un serveur de\n> tracking des tickets)\n\n> alors je pense qu'il faut 1 chief of staff par project dans un meme depot. met tout dans une\n> CR en P3 que l'on continue plus tard\n\n- One Chief of Staff per project, several projects in one surface repository.\n- Filed as P3, to continue later.\n\n## 2026-11-10, the user: Markdown browsable on the forge\n\n> pour moi le fait de tout avoir en markdown est primordial je veux pouvoir naviguer dans le\n> projet avec la forge, ca peut passer par une branche speciale ou mieux notre projet special.\n>\n> ces comparaisons sont importantes a mettre dans l'etude sur le CR\n\n- **Everything in Markdown is essential:** the user must be able to browse the plan on the forge.\n- **Where on the remote:** a dedicated branch is acceptable; a separate planning project is better.\n  A hidden ref, as Beads does now with `refs/dolt/data`, is ruled out because the forge cannot\n  show it. So is a database as the store.\n- The comparison tables go into the CR's study: `study/plan-storage-comparison.md`.\n\n## 2026-11-10, the user: the storage table, corrected\n\nThe user rewrote the \"chantier, studied\" column of the storage table, now in\n`study/plan-storage-comparison.md`, section 2. What changed against the first draft:\n\n- **Format:** the append-only events are additional Markdown and JSON files. The table no longer\n  says the STATUS files are regenerated and never merged.\n- **Writes:** the other sessions talk to the Chief of Staff's session.\n- **Reads:** the files are directly readable on the forge. Tracking over HTTP/MCP is no longer\n  named.\n- **Sync delay:** after each write, within 5 minutes at most.\n- **Conflicts:** the server raises them to the Chief of Staff for an automated resolution. If two\n  sessions have not synced and both try to push, the Chief of Staff resolves to its best guess or\n  asks the user. This replaces \"no conflict within a project\".\n- **Setup of the surface:** to be defined (no longer \"a YAML or JSON config\").\n- Dolt considered for the Markdown, then set aside for now (the user: \"on l'écarte pour\n  l'instant\").\n\n## 2026-11-10, from planning-repo's grill (Chief of Staff's session)\n\n- `planning-repo` was split into three CRs (its `02-answers.md`, answer 6). The third CR is the\n  sync taken over by chantier, and it joins this CR: the surface becomes chantier-sourced\n  (answer 3). Chantier does the autosync and the several projects. n Chiefs of Staff on n\n  projects of one surface synchronise their pushes and rebases with each other. At worst there\n  are about three checkouts of the surface.\n- A running batch's state stays local to the steering machine (answer 4).\n- No OKF for now (answer 5)."}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "plugin-reload-needed", "title": "Plugin reload needed as a named block (batch-11 retro). The epoch-1 Foreman ran an old write guard after main removed it and a round was spent for nothing. A CR blocked by a stale loaded plugin should be a named block cause with a take --note path, or the Chief of Staff restarts the Foreman before a batch when a hook of the plugin changed on main.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "pr-body-outside-reader", "title": "PR body for an outside reader (batch-11 retro). CR-185 PR body took two Inspector passes on wording: roles and batch terms leaked from the internal references. The Driver card should say the PR body is written for an outside reader from the first draft.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "prove-eval-kit-on-host", "title": "Retro batch-13: nothing of the eval kit was proved on the host (setup.py's allow rules, sandbox.py merge on a real PR, reset --yes with real refs/pull/*). Fix: the first paid campaign proves them, running setup.py from the main checkout since its merge rule embeds the checkout's path.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "spec-external-fact-source", "title": "A spec needing an external fact names its source (batch-11 retro). CR-181 shipped without the claude-fable-5-2 price because no Driver had a network tool to read a price page. The spec-writer card should make a spec that needs an external fact name its source or the tools the Driver needs.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "P3", "item": "status-spend-age", "title": "real-direct run 1 retro (2026-11-12, PR 137): plain `status` (and the board) showed a stale spend, $0.57, long after `cost` said $9.57, until `status --refresh`. Fix: `status` and STATUS.json say how old the spend figure is.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "inbox", "item": "epoch-required-everywhere", "title": "Steering commands accept a missing --epoch. take, pause, order done, agents set and the other steering commands still accept a call with no --epoch, though only the loop's status --refresh should go without it once stop-own-gate makes cancel refuse it (found while specifying stop-own-gate, 2026-11-12). Decide which commands must refuse a missing epoch (exit 2) and say so in the cards and the scenario.", "spec": false, "docs": {}}, {"priority": "inbox", "item": "visualization-backend", "title": "Intent: a visualization backend beside the Claude Code mod", "spec": false, "docs": {"01-intent.md": "# Intent: a visualization backend beside the Claude Code mod\n\n2026-11-12, the user, in a Griller session.\n\n## The request\n\nThe user's words (French): think about a visualization backend, as a complement to the Claude\nCode mod, which does not show in remote mode.\n\n## What exists\n\n- **The CR board mod** (`hooks/register.tsx`, CR-108 and later): a read-only pane over the\n  planning folder's `STATUS.json`, refreshed every 15 seconds. It shows on the machine running\n  the session only, not in a Remote Control view (anthropics/claude-code#99217).\n- **The remote board** (`chantier board`, CR-182): a standard-library web page started by the\n  Chief of Staff, read-only, tied to the seat, on `127.0.0.1:8765` by default, reachable over\n  Tailscale or the LAN with no password. It renders its own HTML.\n- **`planning-server`** (P3): a future server owned by the Chief of Staff for an external\n  surface; its constraint says any page goes into `chantier board`, one HTTP server per planning\n  folder, one start and stop rule.\n\n## Open\n\nWhat \"backend\" adds over `chantier board` is to be grilled: see `02-answers.md`.\n", "02-answers.md": "# Answers: visualization-backend\n\n## 2026-11-12, Griller session\n\n**What does `chantier board` lack?** The view is limited; one cannot really act from it; and in\nremote it does not show (the mod does not show on the user's phone, for example). The user wants\na mechanism to *see* the state of the planning surface **and interact with it**, and something\nmore visual. Assumption to work from: network communication is possible (an `ip:port` is\nreachable); no reverse proxy for now.\n\n**Is the web board used today?** It works very well on the same PC as Claude, but not from\nanother PC or Mac on the same network, nor from the phone.\n- Griller's note: preflight reports no `chantier.yaml` in this repository, so the board listens\n  on its default `127.0.0.1:8765`, reachable from this machine only; `board: host: 0.0.0.0` (or\n  the Tailscale address) is what `docs/boards.md` gives for other machines.\n\n**Which actions from the view?** All of these:\n- read the Markdown files (of a CR);\n- add an intake: paste an idea, or fetch it from GitHub (an issue), and so on;\n- grill a little, above all by choosing among decisions (options offered);\n- follow the progress as a kanban;\n- follow the costs during a batch **without causing an extra turn** (of the Chief of Staff);\n- answer the asks;\n- file and triage (inbox, P1-P4, `3-ready`, abandon);\n- batch orders (go, add a CR, slots, budget, rounds, pause);\n- talk to the Chief of Staff.\n\n**Who executes an action from the page?** The Chief of Staff decides: the page passes it on, and\nthe Chief of Staff applies it as if the user had told it.\n\n**Protection once it acts and listens on the network?** A token: a secret generated by chantier\n(in the URL or as a simple password), given to the user by the Chief of Staff.\n\n**Split into several CRs?** Not now: keep grilling; the user first needs prototypes, to see what\nit can look like. The split comes later. (Proposed for then: the read view; the page-to-Chief of\nStaff channel with the token; grill by choices; intake from GitHub.)\n\n**An action sent while the Chief of Staff is busy or its session closed?** A queue: the action\nwaits, shown \"pending\" on the page, and the Chief of Staff handles it at its next turn; with the\nsession closed, the page says so.\n\n**Live costs during a batch: how fresh?** On demand: a \"recompute\" button; otherwise the last\nknown cost with its age.\n\n**The prototype:** a clickable HTML mockup fed with the real data of batch-15 (`STATUS.json`),\nactions simulated, nothing sent or written. Both layouts, phone and desktop, shown side by side.\nViews: the kanban with a CR sheet (its Markdown files); the asks and the chat to the Chief of\nStaff (choice buttons, the \"pending\" queue); the costs (per CR, age, \"recompute\").\n\n**Visual reference:** the user showed a SaaS task kanban (a \"Tasks\" board): a left sidebar\n(workspace, navigation, projects with counts), a header with counters (open, overdue, blocked),\nview tabs (Overview, Lists, Board, Timeline), filter chips, columns with a count and a colored\ndot (To do, In progress, Blocked, In review, Done), cards with a priority tag, labels, project,\ntitle, two-line description, a progress bar, sub-task and comment counts, a date; a floating\n\"Ask …\" button for the assistant. The prototype takes this look.\n"}}], "merged": [{"cr": "CR-191", "title": "Eval kit: an end-to-end dry run as evidence", "batch": "batch-14", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/136", "cost": 9.1868, "when": "2026-11-12T19:24:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-195", "title": "Change Specification: README reorganisation", "batch": "batch-14", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/135", "cost": 1.7705, "when": "2026-11-12T17:58:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-194", "title": "Change Specification: PowerShell quoting of `gh --jq`, and the exact `--epoch` list", "batch": "batch-14", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/133", "cost": 1.335, "when": "2026-11-12T17:06:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-192", "title": "Change Specification: price claude-fable-5-2 in the CLI's table", "batch": "batch-14", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/134", "cost": 1.3267, "when": "2026-11-12T17:06:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-193", "title": "Change Specification: a Driver's checkpoint commit carries a real message", "batch": "batch-14", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/132", "cost": 0.6843, "when": "2026-11-12T13:21:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-190", "title": "Fix the eval kit's review findings before the first sandbox campaign", "batch": "batch-13", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/131", "cost": 1.9899, "when": "2026-11-12T12:17:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-189", "title": "Reproducible eval campaigns on the sandbox", "batch": "batch-13", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/130", "cost": 8.0223, "when": "2026-11-12T11:46:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-188", "title": "Build the eval sandbox project", "batch": "batch-13", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/129", "cost": 2.2603, "when": "2026-11-12T11:07:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-186", "title": "A ready CR sent back to its Driver can be handed back again", "batch": "batch-12", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/124", "cost": 2.1079, "when": "2026-11-12T07:06:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-187", "title": "Change Specification: the heartbeat says something true in direct mode", "batch": "batch-12", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/123", "cost": 2.1141, "when": "2026-11-12T06:09:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-185", "title": "Change Specification: driver-liveness", "batch": "batch-11", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/122", "cost": 0.3444, "when": "2026-11-11T23:09:00Z"}, {"cr": "CR-184", "title": "Gate proof and small CLI fixes", "batch": "batch-11", "pr": "https://github.com/gsemet/chantier/pull/121", "cost": 2.3934, "when": "2026-11-11T22:37:00Z"}], "merged_total": 85, "trashed_total": 36};
